������\n��\n�������������������� ��������������\n���������������������������� ������������\n������������������������������������ ����������������\n���������������������� ��������������������\n�������������������������������������������������� ������������\n���������������������������������� ������������\n�������������������������� ��������������������\n�������������������������������������������������������������������� ��������������������\n��\n�� ������������������ �������� �������������������� ���������� �������� ������������ ���������� ���������� ���������������������� ������ ������������������������������������\n����������������\n��\n������ ����������������������������������������������������\n������ ����������������������������������������\n������ ������������������������������������\n������ ������������������������������������������������������\n������ ��������������������������������������������\n������ ����������������������������������������������������\n������ ����������������������������������������������������������������������\n������ ����������������������������������������������\n������������������\n�������� ������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������\n�������������������������� ������ �������������������"}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|3|UNSTARRED |120", "t": "PMKVY", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF SKILL DEVELOPMENT AND ENTREPRENEURSHIP \n \nLOK SABHA \n \nSTARRED QUESTION NO. 120 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 10.02.2020 \n \nWORLD BANK LOAN FOR SANKALP \n \n*120. SHRI SELVAM G:  \n SHRI DHANUSH M. KUMAR: \n \n \nWill the Minister of SKILL DEVELOPMENT AND ENTREPRE NEURSHIP \nbe  pleased to state: \n \n(a) whether the Government is implementing Skills A cquisition and Knowledge \nAwareness for Livelihood Promotion (SANKALP) Scheme  throughout the country \nalong with the loan granted by World Bank for this scheme;  \n \n(b) whether the Government has assessed the perform ance of the scheme as per \nthe World Bank requirement, if so, the details thereof; \n \n(c) the amount of funds allocated by the Government  since its inception, \nState/UT-wise; \n \n(d) whether the scheme has successfully achieved th e targets set by the \nGovernment so far and if so, the details thereof an d if not, the steps taken by the \nGovernment in this regard; \n \n(e) the details of the number of trainees who got t raining under this Scheme and \nplaced abroad so far; and \n \n(f) the other steps/initiatives taken by the Government  to focus on District level \ntraining eco-system?  \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER IN THE MINISTRY OF SKILL DEVELOPMENT AND \nENTREPRENEURSHIP \n(DR. MAHENDRA NATH PANDEY) \n \n \n(a) to (f) A Statement is laid on the Table of the House.  \n  \n\n \nSTATEMENT REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PARTS (a) TO (f) OF LOK SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION NO. 120 TO BE ANSWERED ON 10.02.2020  \n \na. Yes, the Ministry of Skill Development & Entrepr eneurship (MSDE) is \nimplementing Skills Acquisition and Knowledge Aware ness for Livelihood Promotion \n(SANKALP) Scheme throughout the country along with the loan granted by World \nBank. \n \nb. The governance structure of the SANKALP Scheme p rovides for a Project \nGovernance Board (PGB) at the apex level to review the progress of the project \nperiodically and issue directions.The meeting of th e PGB was held in August 2019 \nwherein the Board expressed its satisfaction on the  overall progress of the project. \nFurther, the performance of the scheme is also asse ssed by the World Bank on a \nperiodic basis. The overall implementation progress  for SANKALP has been graded \n“Satisfactory” as per the World Bank’s latest assessment report. \n \nc. Since inception t ill date, an amount of Rs 225.42 crore has been san ctioned \nto the State/ UTs under SANKALP. Out of these, Rs 2 13.72 crore have been \nreleased to 23 States as State Incentive Grants whi le Rs 11.70 crore have been \nreleased to strengthen the skill ecosystem of117 As pirational Districts of 28 States. \nThe details of the funds sanctioned State/ UT wise are at Annexure-1. \n \nd. As per the project design, seven Disbursement Li nked Indicators (DLIs) have \nbeen agreed between Government of India and the Wor ld Bank. Further, as per \nrequirement of the World Bank, an Independent Verif ication Agency (IVA) i.e. Indian \nInstitute of Management, Indore (IIM-Indore) has be en onboarded to verify the \nachievement against these DLIs. Till date, IVA has submitted the verification report \nfor successful achievement of targets for 5 DLIs th at have been further submitted to \nthe World Bank. \n \ne. SANKALP is not a training scheme therefore no tr ainings are provided under \nSANKALP. \n \nf. The following major steps/ initiatives have been  undertaken under SANKALP \nwith a focus on strengthening the District level training eco-system: \n \n(i) Mahatma Gandhi National Fellowship (MGNF): MGNF  has been launched to \nsupport the District administration in improving sk ill development program delivery \nas well as developing a cadre of committed and comp etent development leaders. \nAs a pilot, 75 fellows shall be deputed in 75 distr icts across Gujarat, Karnataka, \nMeghalaya, Rajasthan, Uttar Pradesh and Uttarakhand for a span of two years. \n \n(ii) Award for Excellence in District Skill Develop ment Planning (DSDP): To \npromote and recognize decentralised planning, Distr ict Skill Development Plan \n(DSDP) Awards have been constituted by the Ministry  under SANKALP. The award \nwas launched in FY 2018-19, wherein districts acros s all States/ UTs were invited \nto participate. 225 districts from across the count ry participated by submitting their \nDSDPs out of which seven districts were identified for Awards in the category of \nAwards for Excellence. \n \n(iii) Capacity building Workshops: To build the cap acity of the District Skill \nCommittee (DSC) members and the district functionar y, thematic workshops are \nbeing conducted under SANKALP.  Till date workshops  have been held with DSC \nmembers of Chhattisgarh, Kerala, Maharashtra, Nagal and, Tamil Nadu and Uttar \nPradesh for development of District Skill Development Plans (DSDPs).  \n \n \n\nAnnexure-1 \n \nDetails of funds sanctioned to State/ UTs under SANKALP \n \n(Amount in Rs.) \nS.No  State/UT  State \nIncentive \nGrant \nFunds for \nAspirational \ndistricts \nTotal  \n1 Andhra Pradesh  7,76,25,000  30,00,000  8,06,25,000  \n2 Chhattisgarh  "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|3|UNSTARRED |121", "t": "Promotion of Golf Tourism", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF TOURISM \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.121 \nANSWERED ON 03.02.2020 \n \nPROMOTION OF GOLF TOURISM \n \n121.  SHRIMATI MEENAKASHI LEKHI:  \n \nWill the Minister of TOURISM be pleased to state:  \n \n(a)  whether there is any proposal to est ablish the country as the \nnext Golf Tourism hub;  \n(b)  if so, the strategies put in place for promoting golf tourism;  \n(c)  the details of proposed investment in promoting golf tourism; \nand  \n(d)  the prospective benefit in terms of foreign exchange from such \ntourism? \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF STATE FOR TOURISM (INDEPENDENT CHARGE) \n(SHRI PRAHLAD SINGH PATEL) \n \n(a) to (c):  Realizing the potential to develop Golf tourism as the \nNiche Tourism product for attracting both International & Domestic \ntourists, Ministry of Tourism has taken formulated strategy & taken \nvarious initiatives. The  Ministry of Tourism has framed \nguidelines for extending financial support for promotion of golf \ntourism.  \n \nThe Ministry of Tourism has constituted the India Golf Tourism \nCommittee (IGTC ), which is the nodal body for golf tourism in the \ncountry. The Ministry invites Expressions of Interest (EOI) from the \nGolf Clubs, Golf Event Managers, State Governments/UT \nAdministrations, Approved Tour Operators/Approved Travel Agents \nand Corporate Houses seeking financial support from the Ministry of \nTourism for Golf Events, Golf Shows, Golf Promotional Workshops/ \nEvents/Annual Meetings/Seminars eligible for financial support, with \n\na view to promote Golf Tourism to and/ or within India under \nIncredible India brand.   \n \nMinistry of Tourism also promotes Golf tourism as one of the \ntourism products within India and overseas through various media \ncampaigns under Incredible India Brand.  \n \n(d): Foreign Exchange Earnings through specific type of tourism is \nnot e stimated by the Ministry. However, the provisional Foreign \nExchange Earnings through tourism in terms of Crore Rupees for the \n2018 and 2019 is 194881 & 210981 respectively.  \n \n******* "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|3|UNSTARRED |122", "t": "Education for Tribals in their Mother Tongue", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF TRIBAL AFFAIRS \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.��122 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 03.02.2020 \n \nEDUCATION FOR TRIBALS IN THEIR MOTHER TONGUE \n \n��122.  SHRI RAMDAS C. TADAS: \nWill the Minister of TRIBAL AFFAIRS be pleased to state: \n(a) the current status of education with regard to Scheduled Tribes in the country; \n(b) the steps taken by the Government to provide education to the children of Scheduled Tribes in their own \nmother tongue at pre-primary and primary level; \n(c) the details of ratio of scheduled tribe teachers in schools, State-wise; \n(d) whether the adaption programmes regarding tribal languages and culture are run for regular and contractual \nteachers in tribal regions; \n(e) whether the Government supports any initiative to publish t he text books and children books in tribal \nlanguages and if so, the details thereof; and \n(f) whether the tribal festivals and occasions are considered while making the teaching time-table and if so, the \ndetails thereof? \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF STATE FOR TRIBAL AFFAIRS \n(SMT. RENUKA SINGH SARUTA)   \n \n(a)� : As per the census 2011, the literacy rate of Scheduled Tribes (STs) is 59 % as compared to 73 % \nfor all category.Further, as per  71stround (J anuary �� June, 2014) survey conducted by National Sample \nSurvey Office (NSSO), Ministry of Statistics & Programme Implementation������ ���������������� ��������������������������\n������������������������������������������������������������������is 67% and corresponding figure for all category is 76%.  \n \n As per data of Ministry of Human Resource Develo pment, the average annual Drop -Out rate of \nScheduled Tribe students for the year 2014-15 in respect of Primary, Upper Primary and Secondary levels is \n6.93, 8.59 and 24. 68 respectively. The Gross Enrolment Ratio (GER) of Scheduled Tribe Students for the \nyear 2016-17 in respect of Primary, Upper Primary, Elementary, Secondary and Senior Secondary levels is \n101.6, 95.7, 99.6, 73.5 and 42.7 respectively.  \n \n(b)� : Section 29 (2) (f) of the Right o f Children to Free and Compulsory Education (RTE) Act, 2009 \n�������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������� �������������� ��������������������������������������������\nCurriculum Framework (NCF), 2005 emphasizes the importance of imparting primary educatio n in the \nmother tongue of the child. Since education is in the Concurrent List of the Constitution, States have the \nliberty to decide the medium of instruction in schools. The NCF clearly states that the Three -Language \nFormula is an attempt to address the challenges and opportunities of the linguistic situation in India. As per \n����������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������\nlanguage.  \n \n(c)Percentage of Scheduled Tribe Teachers in Primary with Upper Pr imary and Secondary Schools �� All \nAreas, All Management is given at Annexure �� I.  \n \n(d)Governments of Andhra Pradesh , Jharkhand and Telangana  have informed that such programmes are \nconducted in their States. \n \n(e): Ministry of Tribal Affairs extends support to State Governments / UTs for mother tongue-based education \nand development of bilingual Primers for enhancement of learning achievement level amongst the Scheduled \nTribe Students. So far 82 language primers have been developed by various State Governmen ts as detailed \nbelow: \n \n \n\n \nState Primers Details Language / tribe covered \nTripura 14 Primers Kokborak, halam, Mog, Garo, Kuki, Mizo \nOdisha 5 Primers Juang, Kisan, Koya, Oram, Saora \nMaharashtra 11 Primers Gondi, Halbi, Kokni, Kolami, Korku, Madiya,          \nMavchi, Pardhi, Pawari, Thakri \nMadhya Pradesh 15 Primers Halbi, Kudhukh, Bhili, Gondi, Korku, \nKerala 6 Primers Kattunaikan, Paniyan \nChhattisgarh 5 Primers Kukudu, Praja, Halbi, Bharia \nJharkhand 5 Primers Kukudu, Khadia, Khorat \nTelangana 5 Primers Gondi, Koya, Kolami, Kondh, Banjara \nWest Bengal 16 Primers Olchiki, Kuduk \n \n \n \n(f)  Ministry of Tribal Affairs advocates for considering tribal festivals and occasions while making \nthe teaching time-table through issuance of advisories to the State s from time to time.  Government of \nJharkhand has informed that academic calendar is prepared considering local holidays and tribal \nfestivals. Government of Telangana has informed that State Government is giving flexibility to the \nDistrict Collectors, Proj ect Officers of Integrated Tribal Development Agencies to declare local \nholidays to Education Institutions during Tribal Festivals. \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n\nAnnexure I \nAnnexure I referred to in reply to part (c) of Lok Sabha Unstarred Question No. 122 for 03.02.2020 \n������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������"}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|3|UNSTARRED |123", "t": "Exemptions to attract Foreign Tourists", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF TOURISM \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.†123 \nANSWERED ON 03.02.2020 \n \nEXEMPTIONS TO ATTRACT FOREIGN TOURISTS \n \n†123. SHRI SANJAY KAKA PATIL:  \n \nWill the Minister of TOURISM be pleased to state:  \n \n(a)  the names of the countrie s which the Government has given any \nexemptions in the field of tourism so as to attract foreign \ntourists to India;  \n(b)  the names of the countries included in this list;  \n(c)  whether the Government has signed/proposes to sign any \nagreement with other co untries to promote international \ntourism;  \n(d)  if so, the details thereof; and  \n(e)  whether there is possibility of organizing International Tourism \nconference by the Government for this purpose? \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF STATE FOR TOURISM (INDEPENDENT CHARGE) \n(SHRI PRAHLAD SINGH PATEL) \n \n(a) & (b): No, Sir. Does not arise.  \n \n(c) & (d): The signing of Agreement with various countries is an \nongoing process and the Ministry of Tourism has signed \nAgreements/Memorandum of Understanding (MoUs)/ Letters of Intent \n(LoIs)/Protocol in the field of tourism cooperation with other countries \nand multilateral fora, details of which is at Annexure.  \n \n(e): No, Sir. The Ministry of Tourism, however holds Joint Working \nGroup (JWG) Meetings with countries. In addition, the Ministry  of \nTourism   regularly organises International Buddhist Conclave, India \nTourism Mart, International Tourism Mart, etc. by inviting dignitaries \nof foreign Governments, leading tour operators, travel agents, media \nrepresentatives, opinion makers, etc. from overseas to boost foreign \ntourist arrivals to the country.   \n********\n\nANNEXURE \n \nSTATEMENT IN REPLY TO PART S (c) AND (d) OF L OK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. †123 ANSWERED ON 03.02.2020 \nREGARDING EXEMPTIONS TO ATTRACT FOREIGN TOURISTS   \n \nAgreements / Memorand um of Understanding / Letter of Intent / \nProtocol signed with foreign countries by the Ministry of Tourism \nSl. \nNo \nName of the country \nwith whom  Agreement \n/MoU/LoIs /Protocols \nsinged \nMoU / Agreement /LoI, \nProtocol \nDate of \nsignature \n1.  Iraq  Protocol 25.10.1986 \n2.  Portugal  Agreement 29.07.1991 \n3.  Syria  Agreement 08.10.1991 \n4.  Israel  Agreement 18.05.1993 \n5.  Singapore  Agreement 24.01.1994 \n6.  Russia  Agreement 30.06.1994 \n7.  Iran  MoU 18.04.1995 \n8.  Mexico  Agreement 28.03.1996 \n9.  Cyprus  Agreement 07.11.1996 \n10.  Greece  Agreement 13.01.1998 \n11.  Indonesia  MoU 08.02.2000 \n12.  Italy  Agreement 26.06.2000 \n13.  Sri Lanka  MoU 23.01.2004 \n14.  Brazil  Agreement 25.01.2004 \n15.  Serbia  MoU 02.11.2004 \n16.  IBSA Tripartite \nAgreement \nAgreement 15.10.2008 \n17.  Poland  Agreement 25.04.2009 \n18.  Malaysia  MoU 27.10.2010 \n19.  ASEAN  MoU 12.01.2012 \n20.  Mauritius  MoU 12.03.2013 \n21.  Japan  Memorandum 22.01.2014 \n22.  Nepal  MoU 25.11.2014 \n23.  France  LoI 09.04.2015 \n\n \n******** \n \n \n24.  China  Agreement 15.05.2015 \n25.  Tanzania  MoU 19.06.2015 \n26.  Uzbekistan  Agreement 06.07.2015 \n27.  Turkmenistan  MoU 11.07.2015 \n28.  Egypt  MoU 24.08.2015 \n29.  U.A.E. MoU 03.09.2015 \n30.  Cambodia  MoU 16.09.2015 \n31.  Maldives  MoU 10.04.2016 \n32.  Qatar  MoU 05.06.2016 \n33.  South Africa  MoU 08.07.2016 \n34.  Kyrgyzstan  MoU 20.12.2016 \n35.  Oman MoU 11.02.2018 \n36.  Bulgaria MoU 05.09.2018 \n37.  Malta MoU 17.09.2018 \n38.  Romania MoU 19.09.2018 \n39.  Colombia MoU 04.10.2018 \n40.  Korea MoU 05.11.2018 \n41.  Argentina MoU 18.02.2019 \n42.  Saudi Arabia MoU 20.02.2019 \n43.  Croatia MoU 26.03.2019 \n44.  Paraguay MoU 27.09.2019 \n45.  Philippines MoU 18.10.2019 \n46.  Finland MoU 21.11.2019 "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|3|UNSTARRED |124", "t": "UGC", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF HUMAN RESOURCE DEVELOPMENT \nDEPARTMENT OF HIGHER EDUCATION \n                                           \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION No. 124 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 03.02.2020 \nUGC \n124. DR. T.R.PAARIVENDHAR:  \nSHRI A. GANESHAMURTHI:  \nSHRI A.K.P. CHINRAJ:  \nWill the Minister of HUMAN RESOURCE DEVELOPMENT be pleased to state:  \n \n(a) whether it is true that a committee headed by Shri M.M. Salukhe had suggested to a \nnew national board (UGC) to conduct the examination on-demand system;  \n(b) whether the new national board will address the needs of millions of students country \nwide;  \n(c) if so, the details thereof;  \n(d) the purpose and motive to bring the new system of on-demand exam; and  \n(e) the salient features of the proposed new on-demand exam? \nANSWER \nMINISTER OF HUMAN RESOURCE DEVELOPMENT \n(SHRI RAMESH POKHRIYAL 'NISHANK') \n(a): Yes, Sir. The University Grants Commission has informed that the Committee has \nsuggested for the establishment of a National Board to conduct Examination On -\nDemand. \n(b) & (c): This system is envisaged to provide flexibility to the students, specially those \nenrolled under open and distance education mode, to undertake assessment based upon \ntheir readiness.  \n(d) & (e): The Committee has recommended the following salient features for this system: \n(i) Related syllabus, study mate rial and question banks  to be made available to the \ncandidates; \n(ii) Open to all, no age limit, minimum requirements of eligibility etc; \n(iii) Use of technology/automation; \n(iv) Ensure highest dependability, openness, reliability, transparency and recognition. \n****** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|3|UNSTARRED |125", "t": "Quality of Education", "head": " \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF HUMAN RESOURCE DEVELOPMENT \nDEPARTMENT OF SCHOOL EDUCATION AND LITERACY \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 125 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 03.02.2020 \n \nQuality of Education \n \n        125. SHRI PARTHIBAN S.R.:  \n \n         Will the Minister of HUMAN RESOURCE DEVELOPMENT be pleased to state: \n \n(a) whether any survey/study has been carried out recently to evaluate the quality of primary, secondary  \nand higher education in the country;  \n \n(b) if so, the details and the outcome thereof;  \n \n(c) the steps taken by the Government to improve the critical thinking of students in primary and second ary \neducation in the prevailing global scenario; and  \n \n(d) the details of specific programmes in this regard being implemented? \n \nANSWER \nMINISTER OF HUMAN RESOURCE DEVELOPMENT \n(SHRI RAMESH POKHRIYAL ‘NISHANK’) \n \n \n \n(a) and (b): The National Council for Educational Research and Training (NCERT) conducts periodic \nnational surveys to assess learning achievement of children in Classes – III, V, VIII, and X. The National  \nAchievement Survey (NAS) 2017 was held on 13th November 2017 for assessing the competencies \ndeveloped by the children at the grade levels III, V and VIII from across 701 districts covering all  the 36 \nStates and UTs and covered 22 lakh students from 1.10 l akh schools. NAS (2017) was administered with \ndistricts as the unit of reporting in different subject areas such as languages, mathematics, EVS/Sc ience and \nSocial Sciences in the Government and Government aided schools. The competency based test was based \non the Learning Outcomes which were recently incorporated in the Central Rules for t he Right of Children \nto Free and Compulsory Education  Act by the Government of India. Similarly, National Achievement \nSurvey for Class X was also conducted throughout the country on February 05, 2018 in government, \ngovernment aided and private schools. The learning levels of 1.5 million students in 44,304 school s across \n610 districts in 34 States and UTs were assessed, in 5 subject areas of English, Mathematics, Science, Social \nScience and Modern Indian Language (MIL).  \n \n \n\n \nThe NAS district report cards have been shared with the States and UTs to help in identifying gaps at the \ndistrict level and devise strategies for further improvement. Subsequently, a framework of intervent ion has \nbeen developed and shared with States to improve the quality of learning in the schools. \n(c) and (d) : The Central Governmen t has taken the following initiatives for improving quality of education \nand develop critical thinking of students:  \n (i)  To ensure competencies, the Central rules to the Right of Children to Free and Compulsory Education \n(RTE) Act, 2009 have been amended  and class -wise, subject-wise Learning Outcomes have been shared \nwith all States and Union Territories.  \n(ii)  The online D.El.Ed. course was started from 3rd October, 2017 and 9,58,513 teachers have \nsuccessfully completed the training. \n(iii)  In order to provide e -learning material, different facilities like e- PATHSHALA, DIKSHA portal, \nSWAYAM (Study Webs to Active Learning for Young Aspiring Minds), SWAYAM PRABHA DTH \nChannels have been made available by the Ministry. \n(iv) Under Samagra Shiksha, funds are  given to all States and UTs for strengthening of infrastructure and \nprovision of other facilities in schools to ensure that all schools meet the prescribed norms, besid es other \ninterventions to improve the quality of education such as training of in -service teachers, headmasters and \nprincipals, remedial teaching for academically weaker students, provision of library grants to schoo ls, ICT \nand digital initiatives, strengthening of teacher education institutions, Rashtriya Avishkar Abhiyan , Padhe \nBharat Badhe Bharat, etc. \n(v)  An integrated Teacher Training Programme titled NISHTHA (National Initiative for School Heads and \nTeachers’ Holistic Advancement) has been launched, to train 42 lakh teachers, and other functionarie s to \nmake classrooms learner -friendly and improving children’s competencies including critical thinking, \nproblem solving, creativity, as well as social-personal qualities such as cooperation, team work etc.   \n(vi) The Government of India has decided to participate in the Programme for Internat ional Students \nAssessment (PISA) to be conducted by the Organization for Economic Cooperation and Development \n(OECD) in 2021.  \n(vii) The Central Board of Secondary Education (CBSE) has undertaken various examination reforms to \npromote competencies and quality of learning : \n(a)  Change in design of question papers with nearly 33% increase in internal choice in all  major subjec ts \nfor classes X and XII and introduction of objective type questions. It has been decided to include 1 0% \ncompetency based Questions in the annual examination of class 10 in the current year. \n(b) Experiential learning has been introduced from 2019 -20 to promote self thinking, creativity and"}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|3|UNSTARRED |126", "t": "Spiritual Tourism Circuits under Swadesh Darshan Scheme", "head": " \n \n \n \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA\n \nMINISTRY OF AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE\n \nDEPARTMENT OF AGRICULTURE, COOPERATION & FARMERS WELFARE\n \n \nLOK  SABHA\n \nSTARRED QUESTION NO.\n126\n \n \nTO BE ANSWERED ON THE   \n11\nTH\n \nFEBRUARY\n, 20\n20\n \n \nSUBSIDY TO FARMERS\n \n \n*126. \n  \nDR. SUKANTA MAJUMDAR: \n \nSHRI BHOLA SINGH: \n \n \nWill the Minister of AGRICULTURE\n \nAND FARMERS \nWELFARE   \nकृ\u0000ष\n \nएवं\n \n\u0000कसान\n \nक\u0000याण\n \nमं\u0000ी\n \n \nbe pleased to state:\n \n \n(a)\n \nwhether the Government provides any subsidy to farmers on agricultural inputs like \nfertilizers, seeds, farm equipment, irrigation, diesel, power and outputs like godown, MSP, \ninsurance, etc. and if so, the details thereof; \n \n \n \n(b)\n \nthe details of subsidy pro\nvided to farmers during the last four years, State\n-\nwise; \n \n \n(c)\n \nwhether as per WTO, support in terms of subsidy provided to the agriculture by some of \nthe developed countries is higher as compared to India; \n \n \n(d)\n \nif so, the details thereof and the reasons t\nherefor; \n \n \n(e)\n \nwhether subsidy schemes for farmers have not been reaching the target group due to \nlacuna in its implementation process; and \n \n \n(f)\n \nif so, the corrective steps being taken by the Government for extending benefits of \nsubsidy and incentives to \nthe farmers for farm mechanization?\n \nANSWER\n \nMINISTER OF AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE\n \nकृ\u0000ष\n \nएवं\n \n\u0000कसान\n \nक\u0000याण\n \nमं\u0000ी\n \n \n \n \n  \n(SHRI NARENDRA  SINGH TOMAR)\n \n(a) to (f):\n \nA statement is laid on the Table of the House.\n \n \n \n\n \n \n \n \n \n \nSTATEMENT REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PARTS \n(a) TO (\nf\n) OF L\nOK SABHA \nSTARRED \nQUESTION NO.\n  \n126   \nDUE FOR REPLY ON \n11\nTH\n \nFEBRUARY, 2020.\n \n \n \n \n(a) & (b):\n \nThe Government provides Subsidies to farmers on the following agricultural \ninputs\n:\n \n(i) \nFertiliser\n \nUrea is being provided to farmers at a statutory \nnotified Maximum Retail Price (MRP). \nThe MRP of 45 kg bag of Urea is Rs.242 per bag (exclusive of charges towards neem\n-\ncoating and taxes as applicable) and the MRP of 50 kg bag of Urea is Rs.268 per bag \n(exclusive of charges towards neem coating and taxes \nas applicable). The difference \nbetween the delivered cost of Fertilizers at farm gate and net market realization by the urea \nunits is given as subsidy to the Urea manufacture/importer by the Govt. of India. Accordingly, \nall farmers are getting Urea at affo\nrdable subsidized price.\n \nWith respect to\n \nPhosphatic and Potassic (P&K) Fertilizers\n, t\nhe D/o Fertilizers is \nproviding subsidy on P&K Fertilizers. Further, \nas far as P&K is concerned, the Government \nhas implemented Nutrient Based Subsidy Policy w.e.f. 1.4.20\n10 for Phosphatic and Potassic \n(P&K) Fertilizers.\n \n \nUnder the policy, a fixed amount of subsidy, decided on annual basis, is \nprovided on subsidised P&K fertilizers depending on their nutrient content.\n \nUnder this policy, \nMRP is fixed by fertilizer companies \nas per market dynamics at reasonable level which is \nmonitored by the Government. Accordingly, any farmer including\n \na poor and marginal farmer\n \nwho is buying these fertilizers is getting benefits of subsidy.\n \nDetails of the subsidy \nannexed at Annexure\n-\nI.\n \nCont\nd…2/\n-\n \n\n \n \n \n-\n2\n-\n \n(ii) \nSeeds\n \n \nThe Department is implementing \nSub\n-\nMission on Seeds & Planting Materials (SMSP) from \nthe year 2014\n-\n15\n \nto promote production and multiplication of quality seeds of agricultural \ncrops, so that the required quantities of seeds could be made available to farmers in the \ncountry\n. To upgrade the quality of farmers’ saved seeds, \nfinancial assistance for distribut\nion \nof foundation/certified seeds at 50% cost of the seeds for cereal crops and 60% for pulses, \noilseeds, fodder and green manure crops for production of quality seeds is available \n/provided for one acre per farmer under the component Seed Village Programm\ne of SMSP. \nThe objective of this scheme is to make available improved /high yielding varieties of seeds \nto farmers in time and to achieve self sufficiency in respect of seeds at village level itself.\n \nThe\n \nabove \nscheme/ component is demand driven and \nimplemented by\n \nthe concerned \nState/implementing\n \nagencies for welfare of all the categories of farmers which help in \nenhancing productivity/production of various crops and also improving profitability of the \nagriculture sector (farmers’ income) in the count\nry.  \nDetails of the subsidy S\ntat\ne\n-\nwise is \nannexed at Annexure\n-\nI\nI\n.\n \n(iii) \n    \nMechanization & Technology\n \n(A) \n   \nA Sub Mission on Agricultural Mechanization (SMAM) is being implemented w.e.f. \n2014\n-\n15.\n \n \nThe SMAM\n \n \nprovides a suitable platform for converging all\n \nactivities for inclusive \ngrowth\n \n \nof agricultural mechanization\n \nby providing a\n \n \n‘single window\n’\n \napproach for \nimplementation with a special focus on small & marginal farmers with the following \nobjectives: \n-\n \nContd…3/\n-\n \n\n \n \n \n-\n3\n-\n \n(1)\n \n \n \nIncreasing the reach of farm \nmechanization to small and marginal farmers and to \nregions where availability of farm power is lo"}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|3|UNSTARRED |127", "t": "Income Tax Exemption", "head": " \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA\n \nMINISTRY OF FINANCE\n \nDEPARTMENT OF REVENUE\n \nLOK SABHA\n \nUNSTARRED QUESTION N\nO. 127\n \nTO BE ANSWERED ON MONDAY 3\nRD\n \nJANUARY, 2020 MAGHA 14\n, 1941 (SAKA)\n \nINCOME TAX EXEMPTION\n \n127.\n \nJASBIR SINGH GILL\n:\n \n \nWill the Minister of \nFINANCE \nbe pleased to \nstate:\n \n(a)\n \n \nwhether it is a fact that the Government has decided any limit for exemption of Income Tax;\n \n(b)\n \nif so, the details thereof;\n \n(c)\n \nthe details of limit of exemption of Income Tax before the general election of 2019; and\n \n(d)\n \nthe\n \ntime by which exemption limit of Rs.5 lacs would be implemented?\n \n                                                      \n \n                                                   \n  \nANSWER\n \n \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN MINISTRY OF FINANCE\n \n \n(SHRI ANURAG SINGH THAKUR)\n \n \n(a) and (b):\n \nThe\n \nFinance Act of respective years, \ninter\n-\nalia\n, provides for the rates of income\n-\ntax as well as \nthe \nbasic exemption limit on the income of individuals, \nHindu undivided family\n, \nassociation of persons or \nbody of individuals,\n \nartificial juridical person \nas defined under the Income\n-\ntax Act, 1961 (the Act).  The \nFinance Bill\n \nprior to its enactment as an Act,\n \nis tabled before the Parliament \nduring the Union Budget which \nis being presented every year in Lok Sabha on 1\nst\n \nFebruary si\nnce 2017\n.\n \n \n(c)\n \nBefore the general election of 2019, the exemption limits were provided under the Finance Act, 2019 \nand were the same as the basic exemption limits as provided under the Finance (No. 2) Act, 2019 which is \nas below:\n \n \nNature of Person\n \nBasic \nExemption\n \nIncome Level\n \nIndividual (other than senior and very senior \ncitizen), HUF, association of persons, body \nof individuals and artificial juridical person.\n \nRs 2,50,000 /\n-\n \nIndividual, resident in India who is of the age \nof sixty years or more but \nless than eighty \nyears.\n \n(senior citizen)\n \nRs 3,00,000\n \nIndividual, resident in India who is of the age \nof eighty years or more\n.\n \n(very senior citizen)\n \nRs 5,00,000\n \n \n(d)\n \nVide Finance Act, 2019, section 87A of the Act was amended to provide a 100% tax rebate to \nindividuals having total income up to Rs. 5 lakhs. \n \n                                              \n------\n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|3|UNSTARRED |128", "t": "Banking Corruption", "head": " \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF RURAL DEVELOPMENT \nDEPARTMENT OF RURAL DEVELOPMENT \nLOK SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION NO. 128 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 11.02.2020 \nLAVATORIES IN HOUSES UNDER PMAY-G \n \n*128.  SHRI ARVIND DHARMAPURI: \n \nWill the Minister of  RURAL DEVELOPMENT  be pleased to state: \n \n(a)  whether the houses constructed under Pradhan M antri Awas Yojana (PMAY-G) do \nnot have any lavatories; \n(b)  if so, the details thereof along with the numb er of such houses identified; \n(c)  the reasons therefor; \n(d)  the amount actually sanctioned for PMAY-G hous es during the last five years to the \nState of Telangana; and \n(e)  the steps being taken by the Government for pr oviding lavatories in the said houses? \n \nANSWER  \nMINISTER OF RURAL DEVELOPMENT  \n(SHRI NARENDRA SINGH TOMAR) \n \n \n(a) to (e): A statement is laid on the table of the House. \n  \n****** \n  \n\nSTATEMENT IN REPLY TO PART (a) TO (e) OF LOK SABHA STARRED \nQUESTION NO. *128 TO BE ANSWERED ON 11.02.2020 REGA RDING \nLAVATORIES IN HOUSES UNDER PMAY-G \n \n(a) to (e): As per the Framework for Implementation  (FFI) of Pradhan Mantri Awaas Yojana-\nGramin (PMAY-G), construction of a toilet has been made an integral part of the PMAY-G \nhouse. The PMAY-G house is treated as complete only  after the toilet has been constructed. \nProvision has been made for an assistance of Rs. 12 ,000 for construction of toilets to eligible \nbeneficiaries through funding from Swachh Bharat Mi ssion-Gramin (SBM-G), Mahatama \nGandhi National Rural Employment Scheme (MGNREGS) o r any other dedicated financing \nsource. \n \nAn amount of Rs.247.88 crore was released to the St ate of Telangana during 2015-16 under \nthe erstwhile rural housing scheme Indira Awaas Yoj ana (IAY). Thereafter, PMAY-G was \nlaunched w.e.f. 01.04.2016. An amount of Rs. 190.79  Crore was released to the State of \nTelangana as 1 \nst  instalment of 2016-17 under PMAY-G. No funds have been released to the \nState thereafter. Neither the physical progress has  been shown by the State on AwaasSoft, \nMIS of PMAY-G, the only source of reporting progres s, nor utilisation certificates have been \nreceived from the State so far. Accordingly, State has been requested to surrender the fund \nreleased under PMAY-G.  \n \n**************** \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|3|UNSTARRED |129", "t": "Off-Budget Expenses", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF FISHERIES, ANIMAL HUSBANDRY AND DAIRYING  \nDEPARTMENT OF FISHERIES \n \n \nLOK SABHA \n \nSTARRED QUESTION No. *129 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 11TH FEBRUARY, 2020  \n \nUpgradation of Pisciculture Infrastructure \n \n \n*129. SHRI PINAKI MISHRA:  \n       \n   \n \nWill the Minister of FISHERIES, ANIMAL HUSBANDRY AND DAIRYING मत्स्यपाऱन, \nपशुपाऱन और डेयरी मंत्री be pleased to state: \n \n(a)  whether any plans are in place to upgrade the physical infrastructure for pisciculture in the \ncountry, especially with respect to cage culture; \n \n(b)  if so, the details thereof;  \n \n(c)  whether the Government is proposing to extend marketing support to States for the export of \nfish to boost the revenue of the fisheries sector; and \n \n (d)  if so, the details thereof? \n \nANSWER \nTHE MINISTER OF FISHERIES, ANIMAL HUSBANDRY AND DAIRYING \n \n (SHRI GIRIRAJ SINGH) \n(a)  to  (d)  :      A Statement is placed on the Table of the House. \n \n***** \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n\n \nStatement referred to in reply to the Lok Sabha Starred Question No.*129 put in by Shri \nPinaki Mishra , Member of Parliament , Lok Sabha for answer on 11 th February, 2020 \nregarding Upgradation of Pisciculture Infrastructure \n \n(a) and (b) : Yes, Sir. The Ministry of Fisheries, Animal Husbandry and Dairying plans to upgrade the \nphysical infrastructure for pisciculture in the country and is implementing various \nschemes/programmes for development of fisheries including creation of infrastructure . Details of the \nCentral Schemes being implemented are as below:  \n \n(i) The Centrally Sponsored Scheme (CSS) on Blue Revolution: Integrated Development and \nManagement of Fisheries at a t otal central outlay of Rs.  3000 crore  is being  implemented \nsince 2015 -16 for development of fisheries in the country. The CSS inter-alia, provides \nfinancial assistance for creation of infrastructure for pisciculture such as installation of cages \nin reservoirs and open sea, cages for fish culture in brackishwater, establishment of fish brood \nbanks, hatcheries, construction of ponds, rearing ponds, fish feed mills, development of post-\nharvest infrastructure like ice plants,  cold storages, ice plants-cum-cold storages and \ndevelopment of fish landing centre  in reservoirs.  Details of the physical infrastructure \nfacilities taken up under the CSS for pisciculture  activities in the country, especially with \nrespect to cage culture are furnished at Annexure-I. \n \n(ii) A new scheme titled Fisheries and Aquaculture Infrastructure Fund (FIDF) was launched in \n2019-20 with a fund size of Rs . 7522.48 crore for providing access to concessional finance \nfor development fisheries infrastructure in the country.  Under the FIDF, the Mini stry of \nFisheries, Animal Husbandry and Dairying provides interest subvention up to 3% per annum \nfor providing the concessional finance by the Nodal Loaning Entities for development of \ninfrastrucutre in the fisheries sector.   \n \n(c) and (d) :  Yes, Sir. Ministry of Fisheries, Animal Husbandry and Dairying, Government of India is \nextending monitory support to the States and Union Territories under the Blue Revolution: Integrated \nDevelopment and Management of Fisheries  Scheme. The scheme provides financial assistance to the \nState Governments and Union Territories  for creation of  marketing infrastructure facilities for \nfisheries such as retail fish markets, mobile fish hubs, retail fish outlets (Kiosks), fish preservation and \nfish transport vehicles.  The Fisheries and Aquaculture Infrastructure Development Fund (FIDF) also \nprovides for development of modern fish markets, fish processing units, fish preservation, fish \ntransport and cold chain network infrastructure . Besides, this Marine Products Export Development  \nAuthority ( MPEDA), Ministry of Commerce and Industry  is implementing various schemes and \ntraining programmes for increasing the unit value of the production and export of value -added marine \nproducts from the country and strengthening the capture fisheries sector to enhance the compliance of \nimporting country requirements. \n \n***** \n \n \n\n \nAnnexure- I \n \nStatement referred to in reply to part (a) and (b) of the Lok Sabha Question No. 129 for answer on \n11th February, 2020 regarding Upgradation of Pisciculture Infrast ructure: Details of Pisciculture \nrelated projects/activities taken up under the Centrally Sponsored Scheme (CSS) on Blue \nRevolution: Integrated Development and Management of Fisherie. \n \n \na) 770 nos of sea cage for fish culture  \nb) 25,625 hactres area brought under aquaculture \nc) 522 nos of Re-circulatory Aquaculture Systems (RAS) \nd) 13,982 nos of cages in reservoirs and other  water bodies \ne) 535 nos of fish/prawn hatcheries \nf) 229 nos fish feed mill/plants \ng) 2026 nos. Raceways for Coldwater Fisheries \nh) Construction of 140 nos of landing centers including Reservoirs and rivers \ni) 11 nos Brood-bank facilities created \nj) 461 units of post -Harvest Infrastructure facilities viz., ice pl"}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|3|UNSTARRED |13", "t": "Launch of Portal Santusht", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF LABOUR AND EMPLOYMENT \n \nLOK SABHA \n \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 13  \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 03.02.2020 \n \nLAUNCH OF PORTAL SANTUSHT \n  \n13. SHRI GAURAV GOGOI:  \n      SHRI PASUNOORI DAYAKAR: \n  \n  Will the Minister of LABOUR AND EMPLOYMENT  be pleased to state: \n  \n(a)whether the Government proposes/has launched a portal ‘Santusht’ for \nspeedy redressal of workers as well as employer grievances and \nensuring implementation of labour laws at the grassroot level including \nmonitoring of ESIC, EPFO, etc.;  \n(b)if so, the details thereof and implementation status thereof along with \nresponse received so far; \n(c)whether the Government has any alternate means to resolve such \ngrievances amongst workers and employers who do not have access \nto computer or internet; and \n(d)if so, the details thereof? \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF STATE (IC) FOR LABOUR AND EMPLOYMENT \n(SHRI SANTOSH KUMAR GANGWAR) \n \n(a) to (d): ‘Santusht’ - Implementation Monitoring Cell (IMC) has been \nconstituted in the Office of Minister of State (Independent Charge) for \nLabour & Employment in January 2020. The objective of ‘Santusht’ is to \npromote transparency, accountability, effective delivery of public \nservices and implementation of policies, schemes of Ministry of Labour \nand Employment at grassroot level through constant monitoring. \nFor public grievances, Centralized Public Grievance Redress \nand Monitoring System  (CPGRAM) portal is already functional. Besides, \nwritten grievances as received from stakeholders are also disposed by \nthe Ministry. F urther, online portal under ‘Santusht’ for public grievances \nhas not been started so far. \n***** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|3|UNSTARRED |130", "t": "NRC", "head": " \n \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF HUMAN RESOURCE DEVELOPMENT \nDEPARTMENT OF SCHOOL EDUCATION & LITERACY \n \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 30 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 03.02.2020 \n \nNGOs Working for MDMS \n \n \n30. SHRI SELVAM G: \n    SHRI GAUTAM SIGAMAANI PON: \n    DR. HEENA GAVIT: \n    SHRI REBATI TRIPURA: \n    SHRI DHANUSH M. KUMAR: \n \nWill the Minister of HUMAN RESOURCE DEVELOPMENT be pleased to state: \n \n(a) whether the NGOs are providing meals to schools through centralised kitchens under \nMid-Day Meal (MDM) scheme and if so, the number thereof, State/ UT-wise;  \n \n(b) the number of Rural and Urban schools covered by NGOs, State/UT -wise especially \nKanchipuram district of Tamil Nadu;  \n \n(c) whether the samples collected from NGO’s centralised kitchen have been found to be \nin compliance with prescribed standards and nutriti ve values and if so, the details \nthereof and if not, the reasons therefor and the steps taken in this direction;  \n \n      (d) whether it is a fact that NGO’s engaged in preparing mid -day meals for children in \nrural areas have turned the scheme into a profit  making exercise and deviated from the \npurpose and if so, the details thereof;  \n \n(e) whether the Government is considering to eliminate the role of NGOs in preparing \nmid-day meal in rural areas and if so, the details thereof and the reaction of the State \nGovernments thereto; and  \n \n(f) the other steps taken/being taken by the Government to ensure mid -day meal are \nmandatorily tested by accredited labs each month for maintaining quality and nutrition? \n \n \n \n \n \n \n\n \n \n \nANSWER \nMINISTER OF HUMAN RESOURCE DEVELOPMENT \n (SHRI RAMESH POKHRIYAL ‘NISHANK’)   \n \n(a)  & (b): The Mid -Day Meal Scheme (MDMS) is implemented in partnership with \nStates and UTs. The overall responsibility for providing cooked and nutritious mid -\nday meal to the eligible children lies with the State Governments and Union \nTerritory Administrations. State and UT Governments engage NGOs / Voluntary \nOrganizations for preparing meals in Centralized kitchens for identified clusters of \nschools. As per the information provided by the States and UTs, at present 17 States \nand UTs have engaged NGOs / Voluntary Organizations under the Scheme.  In the \nState of Tamil Nadu no NGOs have been engaged for providing meals in schools.  \nThe State and UT-wise details of NGOs engaged and the numbers of rural and urban \nschools covered by NGOs under the Scheme are given at Annexure-I. \n   \n(c) to (f):  The Government of India has issued guidelines on quality, safety and hygiene \nin school level kitchens to all the States and UTs to address the quality issue. These \nguidelines inter -alia provide that the States/UTs must consider engaging CSIR \nInstitutes / N ABL accredited laboratories and FSSAI accredited laboratories for \nundertaking testing of samples of MDM for food safety and contamination. Further, \nthe MDM Rules, 2015 provide for mandatory testing of food samples by \nGovernment recognized laboratories to e nsure that the meals meet nutritional \nstandards and quality. As per the information provided by the States and UTs, 1483 \nsamples have been collected from schools covered by NGOs  and all are found in \ncompliance with the norms prescribed under the Scheme. T he State and UT -wise \ndetails of these samples are given at Annexure-II.  \nStates and UTs have not reported any case of engagement of NGOs those have \nturned the scheme into a profit making exercise in rural areas across the country.  As \nper revised guidelines  for engagement of Civil Society Organizations  (CSO) /Non \nGovt. Organizations (NGO) in Mid-Day-Meal Scheme.  CSO/NGO may be engaged \nfor supply of hot cooked meal under MDMS for a cluster of schools in urban areas \nor in identified rural areas which have goo d road connectivity, for the purpose of \nleveraging efficiency gains. \n******** \n  \n\n \n \nAnnexure-I \n \nAnnexure referred to in reply to part (a) & (b) of Lok Sabha Unstarred Question No. 30 for \n03.02.2020 raised by Shri Selvam G, Shri Gautam Sigamaani Pon, Dr. Heena Gavit, Shri \nRebati Tripura, Shri Dhanush M. Kumar regarding NGOs Working for MDMS \n \nDetails of NGOs engaged and the numbers of rural and urban schools covered by NGOs under \nMid-Day Meal Scheme \n \nS. \nNo. State / UT \nNumber of \nNGOs \nproviding \nMDM \nNumber of \ndistricts in \nwhich NGOs \nare serving \nMDM \nNumber of schools \ncovered by NGOs \nRural Urban Total \n1 Andhra Pradesh 7 13 3575 1358 4933 \n2 Assam 1 2 402 143 545 \n3 Bihar 6 12 856 1842 2698 \n4 Chhattisgarh 5 5 0 681 681 \n5 Dadra & Nagar Haveli 1 1 265 15 280 \n6 Daman & Diu 1 1 47 15 62 \n7 Delhi 52 13 0 2951 2951 \n8 Gujarat 3 9 1959 1443 3402 \n9 Haryana 1 4 1520 407 1927 \n10 Jharkhand 1 2 176 200 376 \n11 Karnataka 66 13 2361 2458 4819 \n12 Madhya Pradesh 23 19 0 3740 3740 \n13 Maharashtra 4 10 789 2605 3394 \n14 Odisha 6 12 2466 952 3418 \n15 Rajasthan 3 9 2291 1705 3996 \n16 Telangana 2 7 1160 938 2098 \n17 Uttar Pradesh 112 26 4634 2888 7522 \n  Total 294 158 22501 24341 4"}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|3|UNSTARRED |131", "t": "Tourism Circuits under Swadesh Darshan Scheme", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF TOURISM \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.131 \nANSWERED ON 03.02.2020 \n \nTOURISM CIRCUITS UNDER SWADESH DARSHAN SCHEME \n \n131. SHRI CHANDRA PRAKASH CHOUDHARY:  \nSHRI SANJAY SETH:  \nSHRI KURUVA GORANTLA MADHAV:  \n \nWill the Minister of TOURISM be pleased to state:  \n \n(a)  the names of the tourism circuits in Jharkhand and Andhra \nPradesh which have been selected under the Swadesh \nDarshan scheme;  \n(b)  the names of the places which have been selected under \nthese circuits;  \n(c)  the details of amount sanctioned for carrying out works under \nthe scheme; and  \n(d)  the time by which these works are likely to be completed? \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF STATE FOR TOURISM (INDEPENDENT CHARGE) \n(SHRI PRAHLAD SINGH PATEL) \n \n(a) to (d): The details of the projec t sanctioned in Andhra Pradesh \nand state of Jharkhand under the Swadesh Darshan scheme is \nplaced below:- \n \nState \n/Year of \nsanction \nDetail Sanctioned \n(Crores) \nProject \nstatus \nTimeline \nof the \nproject \nAndhra \nPradesh \n2014-15 \n \nDevelopment of  \nKakinada - Hope \nIsland - Coringa \nWildlife Sanctuary - \nPassarlapudi - Aduru \n67.84 100% Completed \n\n- S Yanam - Kotipally \nunder Coastal Circuit \nAndhra \nPradesh \n2015-16 \n \nDevelopment of \nNellore - Pulikat Lake \n- Ubblamadugu Water \nFalls – Nelapattu- \nKothakoduru- Mypadu \n- Ramateertham - \nIskapalli under \nCoastal Circuit \n59.70 90% January \n2020 \nAndhra \nPradesh  \n2017-18 \n \nDevelopment of: \nShalihundam-\nThotlakonda- \nBavikonda- \nBojjanakonda- \nAmravati- Anupu \nunder Buddhist \nCircuit \n52.34 45% March \n2020 \nJharkhand \n2018-19 \n \nDevelopment of \nDalma- Chandil- \nGetalsud- Betla \nNational park - \nMirchaiya- Netarhat \nunder Eco Circuit \n52.72 0% 18 Months \n \n******* "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|3|UNSTARRED |132", "t": "FRDI Bill", "head": " \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF HOME AFFAIRS \n \nLOK SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION NO. *132 \n \nTO BE ANSWERED ON THE 11TH FEBRUARY, 2020/ MAGHA 22, 1941 (SAKA) \n \nTUKDE TUKDE GANG \n \n*132.   SHRI JASBIR SINGH GILL: \n SHRI VINCENT H. PALA: \n \nWill the Minister of HOME AFFAIRS be pleased to state:  \n \n(a) whether an organisation called the ‘Tukde Tukde Gang’ has been identified \nand catalogued by the Ministry of Home Affairs, National Crime Records Bureau \n(NCRB) or any Central/State Law Enforcement Agency/Police Force or any \nCentral or State Intelligence Structures; \n \n(b) whether the terminology ‘Tukde Tukde Gang’ is based on specific inputs \nprovided by the Ministry or other law enforcement/intelligence agencies; \n \n(c) whether the Ministry of Home Affairs/NCRB or any other law enforcement or \nIntelligence organisation has drawn up a list of alleged leaders and members of \nthe ‘Tukde Tukde Gang’; \n \n(d) whether any penal action/punishment (and specifically under which sections \nof the IPC and/or other Acts) by the Ministry of Home Affairs or any other law \nenforcement agency/intelligence structure is contemplated against the members \nof the ‘Tukde Tukde Gang’; and \n \n(e) if so, the details thereof? \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF HOME AFFAIRS \n(SHRI G. KISHAN REDDY) \n \n(a) to (e):     A Statement is laid on the table of the House. \n \n \n \n \n\n \n--2--   \nSTATEMENT IN REPLY TO LOK SABHA STARRED QUESTION NO. *132 FOR \nANSWER ON 11.02.2020 REGARDING “TUKDE TUKDE GANG” \n \n(a) to (e): No such information has been brought to the notice of the \nGovernment by any law enforcement agency.  \n******* \n \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|3|UNSTARRED |133", "t": "ITIs", "head": " \n \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF SKILL DEVELOPMENT AND ENTREPRENEURSHIP \n \nLOK SABHA \n \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.133 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 03.02.2020 \n \nITIs \n \n133.  SHRI MAGUNTASREENIVASULU REDDY: \n \nWill the Minister of SKILL DEVELOPMENT AND ENTREPRE NEURSHIP be \npleased to state: \n \n(a)  the details of the Industrial Training Institu tes (ITIs) functioning in the country at \npresent along with the skill trades being taught in  these institutes, State/UT-wise \nincluding Andhra Pradesh; \n \n(b)  the total estimated number of students getting  training in these institutes and the \nexisting vacancies in various categories along with  the plan to fill up these vacancies, \nState/UT-wise; \n \n(c)  whether the Government proposes to set up new ITIs in the country and if so,the \ndetails thereof, State/phase-wise; \n \n(d)   whether the Ministry has prepared any fresh p lan to upgrade ITIs in the country \nand if so, the details thereof including integratio n of ITIs with skill development \nprogrammes; and \n \n(e)  the details of the amount spent on improvement  of ITIs in the country during each \nof the last three years and the current year, State-wise? \n \n \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF \nSKILL DEVELOPMENT AND ENTREPRENEURSHIP \n(SHRI R.K. SINGH) \n \n(a) to (b) There are 15697  Industrial Training Institutes (ITIs) functioning in the \ncountry at present and imparting training in 137 sk ill trades. The detail of State/UT wise \nITIs, including the state of Andhra Pradesh, is att ached at Annexure I and list of 137 \ntrades is at Annexure II. \n \nAround 21 lakh trainees are undergoing training aga inst the total seating capacity \nof 26.65 lakh in various trades categories. Admissi on in ITIs is under the administrative \ncontrol of concerned State Directorates and state w ise rounds of admissions are \nconducted by them to fill vacancies in various categories. \n \n \n \n \n\n(c) Setting up of Industrial Training Institutes (I TIs) and its operations are in the \ndomain of respective State/UT Governments. \n \n (d)  Yes sir , top grade ITIs, in the country including their inte gration with skill \ndevelopment program, a World Bank funded scheme Skills Strengthening for \nIndustrial Value Enhancement (STRIVE)  has been approved by Cabinet Committee for \nEconomic Affairs (CCEA). It is a central sector sch eme launched in October 2017 for a \ntotal cost of Rs. 2200 crore, and 5 years duration;  with an objective to improve relevance \nand efficiency of skills development provided throu gh ITIs and apprenticeship. The \nscheme implemented with effect from financial year 2018-19, has following four \ncomponents:  \nS.No. \n Component \n1 To Improve Performance of ITIs \n2 To Increase Capacities of State Governments to su pport and \nmanage ITIs and apprenticeship training  \n3 To Improve Teaching and Learning  \n4 To Improve and Broadened Apprenticeship Training \n \n(e)  The details of the amount spent on improvement of I TIs in the country during the \nlast three years and the current year, State-wise, under different schemes as given \nbelow, is attached in annexure III, IV& V respectively. \n1. World Bank assisted Vocational Training Improvement  Project (VTIP):  The \nscheme, interalia covers up-gradation of 400 Govern ment ITIs have been \ncovered in 34 States/UTs. The scheme was closed on September 2018 . \n2. Skills Strengthening for Industrial Value Enhanceme nt (STRIVE) is a new \ncentral sector World Bank funded project at a total  cost of Rs. 2200 crore. The \nproject duration is 5 years and the closing date is November 2022.  \n3. Upgradation of existing Govt. ITIs into Model ITIs:  - Under this scheme, the \nfunds were released for improvement of Govt. ITIs. \n\nAnnexure-I \nAnnexure referred to in reply of part (a) to (b) of  LSUQ No.133 on ITIs raised by \nSHRI MAGUNTA SREENIVASULU REDDY for answer on 03.02.2020  \n \nSTATE/UT WISE DETAILS \nSl. No  \n \nName of State/UT \n \nNumber of ITIs \n1 ANDAMAN AND NICOBAR ISLANDS 4 \n2 ANDHRA PRADESH  513  \n3 ARUNACHAL PRADESH 6 \n4 ASSAM 37 \n5 BIHAR 1264 \n6 CHANDIGARH 2 \n7 CHHATTISGARH 235 \n8 DADRA AND NAGAR HAVELI 1 \n9 DAMAN AND DIU 2 \n10 DELHI 60 \n11 GOA 15 \n12 GUJARAT 530 \n13 HARYANA 405 \n14 HIMACHAL PRADESH 292 \n15 JAMMU AND KASHMIR 41 \n16 JHARKHAND 327 \n17 KARNATAKA 1557 \n18 KERALA 651 \n19 LAKSHADWEEP 1 \n20 MADHYA PRADESH 1167 \n21 MAHARASHTRA 994 \n22 MANIPUR 1 \n23 MEGHALAYA 6 \n24 MIZORAM 3 \n25 NAGALAND 2 \n26 ODISHA 580 \n27 PUDUCHERRY 15 \n28 PUNJAB 384 \n29 RAJASTHAN 1923 \n30 SIKKIM 3 \n31 TAMIL NADU 592 \n32 TELANGANA 302 \n33 TRIPURA 20 \n34 UTTAR PRADESH 3302 \n35 UTTARAKHAND 195 \n36 WEST BENGAL 265 \nTotal 15697 \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n\nAnnexure-II  \n \nAnnexure referred to in reply of part (a) to (b) of  LSUQ No.133 on ITIs raised by \nSHRI MAGUNTA SREENIVASULU REDDY for answer on 03.02.2020  \n \nTotal no. of Trades: 137 \nList of trades (Engineering, Non Engineering & Divy ang) under Craftsmen Training \nScheme (CTS) \nEngineering Trades: 73 \nSl. No. \n Name of the Trade \n1 Electrician \n2 Electronics Mechanic \n3"}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|3|UNSTARRED |134", "t": "School Textbooks", "head": " \n \n \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF HUMAN RESOURCE DEVELOPMENT \nDEPARTMENT OF SCHOOL EDUCATION & LITERACY \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 134 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 03.02.2020 \n \nSchool Textbooks \n \n134. SHRI PINAKI MISRA: \n \nWill the Minister of HUMAN RESOURCE DEVELOPMENT be pleased to state: \n \n(a) whether the Paika  Bidroha of 1817 has been included in the school history \ntextbooks as the first war of independence against British Rule; \n \n(b) whether there are plans to popularise the rebellion in schools through other \nmethods like workshops, plays, competitions, or lectures; and \n \n(c) if so, the details thereof? \n \n \nANSWER \nMINISTER OF HUMAN RESOURCE DEVELOPMENT \n(SHRI RAMESH POKHRIYAL 'NISHANK') \n \n(a) The ‘Paika Bidroha’ has been mentioned under the heading `The Khurda \nUprising – A case Study` in the National Council of Educational Research and \nTraining (NCERT)’s Social Science textbook , namely, “Our Pasts -III” (Part-II) for \nClass VIII. \n \n(b) No such proposal is under consideration at present. \n \n(c)  Does not arise. \n \n**** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|3|UNSTARRED |135", "t": "Preservation of Traditional Culture of Haryana", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA\n \nMINISTRY OF AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE\n \nDEPARTMENT OF AGRICULTURE, COOPERATION & FARMERS WELFARE\n \n \nLOK  SABHA\n \nSTARRED QUESTION NO.\n135\n \n \nTO BE ANSWERED ON THE   \n11\nTH\n \nFEBRUARY\n, 20\n20\n \n \nFARMERS REGISTERED UNDER PM\n-\nKISAN SCHEME\n \n \n*135. \n \nSHRI ARVIND GANPAT SAWANT: \n \nSHRI MANNE SRINIVAS REDDY: \n \n \nWill the Minister of AGRICULTURE\n \nAND FARMERS \nWELFARE   \nकृ\u0000ष\n \nएवं\n \n\u0000कसान\n \nक\u0000याण\n \nमं\u0000ी\n \n \nbe pleased to state:\n \n \n(a) \n \nthe number of farmers registered under the Pradhan Mantri Kisan Samman Nidhi \nYojana\n \n(PM\n-\nKISAN Scheme) and the number of farmers benefited, out of them, State\n-\nwise; \n \n \n(b) \n \nthe details of funds allocated for the said scheme and the total amount being disbursed \nto the beneficiaries; \n \n \n(c) \n \nwhether\n \nit is true that all the enrolled farmers are not getting the benefit under the above \nscheme and if so, the reasons therefor; \n \n \n(d) \n \nthe manner in which the Ministry ensures that only genuine farmers are getting \nbenefited under the scheme; and \n \n \n(e) \n \nwheth\ner the Government has a mechanism to check whether the said scheme has \nbrought any major changes in the lives of beneficiaries, if so, the details thereof and if not, the \nreasons therefor?\n \n \nANSWER\n \nMINISTER OF AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE\n \nकृ\u0000ष\n \nएवं\n \n\u0000कसान\n \nक\u0000याण\n \nमं\u0000ी\n \n \n \n \n  \n(SHRI NARENDRA  SINGH TOMAR)\n \n \n(a) to (e):\n \nA statement is laid on the Table of the House.\n \n \n \n \n\nSTATEMENT REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PARTS (a) TO (e) OF LOK SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION NO.  135   DUE FOR REPLY ON 11\nTH\n \nFEBRUARY, 2020.\n \n \n(a):\n \nAs on 06.02.2020, a total of \n96,073,451\n \nfarmer families have been successfully \nregistered under PM\n-\nKISAN out of which 84,472,629 beneficiaries have received benefits \nunder the Scheme. The State\n-\nwise details of the same are given at Annexure\n-\n1.\n \n \n(b):\n \nBudget\nary allocation of Rs. 20,000 Crore and Rs. 75,000 Crore have been provided in \nFinancial Year 2018\n-\n19 & 2019\n-\n20 respectively for the implementation of PM\n-\nKISAN \nScheme, out of which, as of 06.02.2020, an amount of Rs. 50522.2 Crore has been \ntransferred in th\ne bank account\ns\n \nof beneficiaries across the country since inception of the \nScheme.\n \n(c)\n:\n     \nPM\n-\nKISAN is a continuous and ongoing Scheme, in which the financial benefits are \ntransferred to the bank accounts of the identified beneficiaries as and when their \ncorrect and \nverified data is uploaded by the concerned States / UTs on PM\n-\nKisan web\n-\nportal.  The data \nof beneficiaries so uploaded by the concerned States/UTs undergoes a multi\n-\nlevel \nverification and validation by various concerned agencies before making p\nayment to the \nbeneficiaries\n, hence, merely registration on the Portal does not ensure ben\nefit transfer to the \nbeneficiaries\n.\n \n(d)\n:\n \n \nIn order to release PM\n-\nKISAN benefits, Ministry receives verified details i.e. details of \nland holdings, villages, bank relat\ned details, Aadhar car\nd number etc. of the beneficiaries\n \nfrom \nthe concerned States/UTs. These details are required to verified by the PM\n-\nKISAN \nPortal, initially the account validation is done through Public Finance Management System \n(PFMS). From December, \n2019 onwards AADH\nAR authentication of beneficiaries\n \ndata is \nalso made mandatory. In case, any mismatch of provided details has been noticed by the \nabove \n  \nmentioned \n \nagencies, \n   \ndetails related\n  \n \nto concerned beneficiaries are required to be \n \nContd…2/\n-\n \n\n \ncorrected or amen\nded by the concerned States/UTs.  For this purpose\n \ndetails of concerned \nbeneficiaries are made available to the concerned States/UTs. This way, Ministry ensures \nben\nefit transfer to the beneficiaries\n \nonly on the basis of verification of the\n \nconcerned State/UT \nGovernments. In the cases of self\n-\nregistration through the Farmers Corner in the portal, \nMinistry seeks \nverified data of the beneficiaries\n \nfrom the concerned Stat\ne\n/UT Governments \nbefore transfer the benefit under PM\n-\nKISAN. \n \n(e)\n:\n \nAgricul\nture is a State subject. The prime responsibility of development of Agriculture \nrests with the concerned State/UT Governments. Government of India, however, through its \nvarious Schemes/Missions, supplements the efforts of the State/UT Gove\nrnments for the \nd\nevelopment of a\ngriculture and welfare of the farmers. With a view to provide income support \nto all landholding farmer families across the country, to enable them to take care of expenses \nrelated to agricultural & allied activities and to save them from the\n \nclutches of the \nmoneylenders, PM\n-\nK\nISAN was launched. The Scheme was\n \nlaunched on 24.02.2019 a\nnd the \nsame is in nascent state.  As such\n, \nno\n \nformal study of the Scheme has been done yet.\n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n\n \nAnnexure\n-\n1   \n \n \nState wise details of farmers registered and benefitted under PM\n-\nKISAN \n \n(as on 06/02/2020)\n \nState/UT\n \nNo. of Farmers\n \nSuccessfully\n \nRegistered under\n \nPM\n-\nKISAN\n \nFarmers benefitted\n \nun"}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|3|UNSTARRED |136", "t": "CSR Fund for MPLAD", "head": "�\n�\n������������������� �\n����������������������������� �\n�\n��������� �\n����������������������� � �� �\n����������� ��������� � ����������� ������ �\n������� ������������� �\n�\n�\n������������������� ��\n�������� �\n� �� �������������������� ���\n�������������������������������������� �������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������\n�������������������� ��\n�\n������������������������������ ������������������������������������������� �����������\n��������������������������� ������������������������������������������ ��������� �\n�\n���������������������������������� ���������������������������������� ������������������\n������������������������ ����������������������������������������������� ��������������������\n��� �\n�\n���� �������� ���� ������� ���� ���� � ������������ ��� �������� ���� ����� ������ � ������\n��������������������������������� �������������������������\n�\n������ �\n������������������������������������� �\n������������������������������������������� ������������������������������������������������������ �\n������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������� �� ����\n���� ��� �������������� ���� ��� ���� ���������� ����� ����� ������ ��������� ������ ��������\n�������������������������������������������������� ���������������������� �����������������\n����������������������������������������������������������������������������������������\n���������������������������������������������� ������������������������������������������\n��������������������������������������������������������������� ����������������������\n�����������������������������������������������������������������������������������������\n������������������������������������������������������������������������ �������������\n����������� ���������������������������������������� � ���������������������������������\n������������������������������������������������������������������������������������\n����������������������������������� ��������������������������������������������������\n�������������������������������������������������������������� ������������� ����������\n����������\n�\n������ �"}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|3|UNSTARRED |137", "t": "Liberalised Remittance Scheme", "head": "O.I.H. \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF RURAL DEVELOPMENT \nDEPARTMENT OF RURAL DEVELOPMENT \nLOK SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION NO. 137 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 11.02.2020 \nBPL FAMILIES \n \n*137.  SHRI SUNIL KUMAR PINTU: \n \nWill the Minister of  RURAL DEVELOPMENT \n be pleased to state: \n \n(a)  the number of families in the rural areas of t he country which have shifted from \nBelow Poverty Line (BPL) category to Above Poverty Line (APL) category and from \nAPL category to BPL category during the last three years including the reasons \ntherefor, State and district-wise particularly in Bihar; \n(b)  whether the Government has conducted any surve y in this regard; and \n(c)  if so, the outcome thereof? \n \nANSWER  \nMINISTER OF RURAL DEVELOPMENT  \n(SHRI NARENDRA SINGH TOMAR) \n \n  \n(a) to (c): A statement is laid on the Table of the House. \n  \n\nSTATEMENT REFERRED TO IN THE REPLY TO PART (a) TO (c)  OF THE LOK \nSABHA STARRED QUESTION NO. *137  DUE FOR ANSWER ON 11.02.2020 \n(a) to (c): The erstwhile Planning Commission (now NITI Aayog) defined and estimated \npoverty line using monthly per capita consumption e xpenditure (MPCE) data from the Large \nSample Survey on Household Consumer Expenditure car ried out by the National Sample \nSurvey Office (NSSO). The latest available poverty line was estimated in 2011-12.  \nThereafter, no BPL estimation has been done in a centralized manner. \nWhile the estimation of poverty using poverty line had been done by the erstwhile Planning \nCommission, the Ministry of Rural Development had b een conducting Below Poverty Line \n(BPL) Census to identify the poor households in the  rural areas, who could be the potential \nbeneficiaries under various programmes of the Gover nment of India. The first BPL Census \nwas conducted in 1992 for the Eighth Five Year Plan . Subsequently, BPL census was \nconducted in 1997, for the Ninth Five Year Plan and  in 2002 for the Tenth Five Year Plan \nperiod. In 2011, in place of BPL Survey, a Socio Ec onomic and Caste Census 2011 (SECC \n2011)  was conducted in association with the States/UTs un der technical and financial support \nfrom the Government of India.  \nAfter 2002 BPL Survey, recognizing the multi-dimens ional nature of poverty, SECC-2011 \nwas conducted by the Government. SECC-2011 provides  data of households on various \naspects of their socio-economic status viz., housin g, land-holding/landlessness, educational \nstatus, status of women, the differently abled, occ upation, possession of assets, scheduled \ncaste/ scheduled tribe (SC/ST) households, income, etc. The purpose of SECC, 2011 was to \nidentify rural poor households, not on the basis of  any pre-defined poverty line, but on the \nbasis of three criteria based categories: (i) autom atically excluded households, (ii) \nautomatically included households and (iii) deprive d households. The State/Union Territory–\nwise numbers of rural households in the said three categories are given at Annexure-1. The \ndistrict-wise information of Bihar on numbers of ru ral households in the said three categories \nis given at Annexure- 2. Beneficiary selection under various programmes of  Government of \nIndia has been made using the “automatically includ ed households” and “deprived \nhouseholds” data of SECC-2011 as the basis. \n  \n  \n\nAnnexure-1 \n \nReferred in Part (a) of the Lok Sabha starred question no. *137 due for answer on 11.02.2020  \nStates/UTs \nTotal \nHouseholds \nAutomatically \nExcluded \nHouseholds  \nAutomatically \nIncluded \nHouseholds \nDeprived \nHouseholds  \nJAMMU & KASHMIR 1601606 761875 13791 586345 \nHIMACHAL PRADESH 1263756 840852 1938 259855 \nPUNJAB 3269467 2438567 8004 778245 \nCHANDIGARH 15657 9250 10 3925 \nUTTARAKHAND 1479742 823330 4726 429888 \nHARYANA 2969509 1779954 6519 997129 \nNCT OF DELHI 1051097 881667 1127 89744 \nRAJASTHAN 10223073 4069999 72091 5165212 \nUTTAR PRADESH 26015592 12466832 68190 10381355 \nBIHAR 17829066 4793001 37657 10876054 \nSIKKIM 88723 39442 235 33480 \nARUNACHAL PRADESH 201842 118987 3559 72937 \nNAGALAND 284310 97323 969 182441 \nMANIPUR 448163 147003 4963 236653 \nMIZORAM 111626 44437 512 66499 \nTRIPURA 697062 165435 33343 401458 \nMEGHALAYA 485897 151711 1224 327506 \nASSAM 5743835 1689138 33451 2892859 \nWEST BENGAL 15756750 3302481 203209 10056266 \nJHARKHAND 5044234 1566811 52045 2694061 \nODISHA 8677615 1628400 119772 5730372 \nCHHATTISGARH 4540999 819609 112084 3179327 \nMADHYA PRADESH 11288946 3301696 396787 6748026 \nGUJARAT 6920473 3236193 31216 2967972 \nDAMAN AND DIU 31795 16707 3519 6313 \nDADRA & NAGAR HAVELI 45352 15780 298 25378 \nMAHARASHTRA 13841960 5440356 227678 6064157 \nANDHRA PRADESH 9344180 3595077 59470 4822104 \nTELANGANA 5643739 3143322 13543 2136159 \nKARNATAKA 8048664 4022702 30074 2836539 \nGOA 220731 185010 135 23816 \nLAKSHADWEEP 10929 9410 13 1455 \nKERALA 6319215 4388457 14289 1469167 \nTAMILNADU 10088119 4657981 38549 4704939 \nPUDUCHERRY 115249 65854 311 40336 \nANDAMAN & NICOBAR ISLANDS 68481 39354 168 15976 \n Total 179787454 70754003 1595469 87303948 \n \n \n\nAnnexure-2 \n \nR"}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|3|UNSTARRED |138", "t": "Loan Scheme for Farmers", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF RURAL DEVELOPMENT \nDEPARTMENT OF RURAL DEVELOPMENT \nLOK SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION NO. 138 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 11.02.2020 \nPMGSY IN KERALA \n \n*138.  SHRI M.K. RAGHAVAN: \n \nWill the Minister of  RURAL DEVELOPMENT  be pleased to state: \n \n(a)  the details of roads identified for developmen t under the Pradhan Mantri Gram Sadak \nYojana (PMGSY) in Kozhikode, in particular and other districts of Kerala, in general; \n(b)  the total funds sanctioned, spent and un-spent  under the scheme during the last three years \nparticularly in Kozhikode district and in the State of Kerala, in general; and \n(c)  the manner in which the Government proposes to  speed up the road connectivity? \n \nANSWER  \nMINISTER OF RURAL DEVELOPMENT  \n(SHRI NARENDRA SINGH TOMAR) \n \n \n(a) to (c): A Statement is laid on the Table of the House. \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n\nStatement referred to in reply to Parts (a) to (c) of Lok Sabha Starred Question No. *138  \ndue for answer on 11.02.2020  \n \n(a): The State of Kerala has been sanctioned its fu ll entitlement under Pradhan Mantri Gram \nSadak Yojana (PMGSY)-I and PMGSY-II.  Details of district-wise roads sanctioned and \ncompleted under PMGSY-I and PMGSY-II including Kozh ikode district of Kerala is at \nAnnexure. Under PMGSY-III, 1425 km road length has been allocated to the State of Kerala and \nthe state has initiated planning activities for road identification.  \n \n(b):  The total funds sanctioned, utilized and unspent under PMGSY during the last three years in \nthe State of Kerala and Kozhikode district are as under:- \n (In Rs Crore) \n* Funds are sanctioned to each district (PIUs) based on their actual fund request. \n \n \n(c): The progress of implementation of rural roads under PMGSY is regularly monitored by way \nof Regional Review Meetings (RRMs), Performance Rev iew Committee (PRC) Meetings, Pre-\nEmpowered/Empowered Committee Meetings, Video Conference with the State. At District level, \nthe District Development Coordination and Monitorin g Committee (Disha) headed by a Member \nof Parliament (LS) monitors the implementation of v arious schemes of Government of India \nincluding PMGSY. In addition to this, special revie w meetings are also held by Secretary/ \nAdditional Secretary/ Joint Secretary, Ministry of Rural Development with Chief Secretaries/ \nPrincipal Secretaries of the States.  \n \n***** \n  \nYear  Fund sanctioned to \nState \n(Central Share & \nState Share) \nFund \nUtilized \n(State) \nFund  \nun-spent \n(State) \nFund \nsanctioned to \nKozhikode* \nFund Utilized \nby \nKozhikode* \n2016 -17  305.67  267.43  38.24  18.98  18.98  \n2017-18 280.19 206.09 74.10 13.30 13.30 \n2018-19 162.92 225.16 0 10.28 10.28 \n\nANNEXURE \nAnnexure referred to in part (a) of Lok Sabha Starred Question No. *138 to be answered on 11.02.2020 \n \n  PMGSY-I \nSr.No.  District Name No of \nRoadwork \nSanctioned  \nRoad \nLength \nSanctioned  \nNo of \nBridge \nWork \nSanctioned  \nNo of \nRoad \nWork \nCompleted  \nNo of \nBridge \nWorks \nCompleted  \nRoad \nLength \nCompleted  \nBalance \nRoad \nLength \n1 Alappuzha 71 101.008 0 66 0 90.666 7.915 \n2 Ernakulam  65  130.695  1 65  1 130.318  0.000  \n3 Idukki 112 401.094 0 104 0 375.083 26.183 \n4 Kannur  131  367.308  0 127  0 353.320  11.836  \n5 Kasaragod 96 247.091 0 93 0 239.030 7.700 \n6 Kollam  78  166.137  0 78  0 165.962  0.000  \n7 Kottayam 62 153.448 0 55 0 128.157 23.409 \n8 Kozhikode 131 289.146 0 126 0 271.523 17.670 \n9 Malappuram 136 3