heme at present; \n(d)  whether the Government is considering to incre ase the amount of assistance being \nprovided for the construction of houses under PMAY- G, if so, the details thereof and \nif not, the reasons therefor; \n(e)  whether any scheme to broaden the eligibility list of Pradhan Mantri Awas Yojana is \nunder consideration of the Government and if so, the details thereof; \n(f)  the details of the number of housing units con structed under PMAY-G so far, State-\nwise and year-wise; and \n(g)  the time by which the remaining housing units are targeted to be constructed? \n \nANSWER  \nMINISTER OF RURAL DEVELOPMENT  \n(SHRI NARENDRA SINGH TOMAR) \n \n \n(a) to (g): A statement is laid on the Table of the House. \n  \n\nSTATEMENT IN REPLY TO PART (a) to (g) OF LOK SABHA STARRED \nQUESTION NO.*139 TO BE ANSWERED ON 11.02.2020 REGAR DING \nIMPLEMENTATION OF PMAY-G \n \n(a): To achieve the objective of “Housing for All” by 2022, the Ministry of Rural \nDevelopment is implementing Pradhan Mantri Awaas Yo jana – Gramin (PMAY-G) w.e.f. 1 \nst  \nApril, 2016 with a target for construction of 2.95 crore houses in rural areas by 2022. \n \n(b): State/ UT-wise targets and achievements under PMAY-G are at Annexure I.  \n \n(c) and (d): Under PMAY-G, beneficiaries are provid ed an enhanced unit assistance of Rs. \n1.20 lakh in plains (which was Rs. 70,000 under the  erstwhile Indira Awaas Yojana) and Rs. \n1.30 lakh in hilly states, difficult areas and Inte grated Action Plan (IAP) districts (which was \nRs. 75,000 under the erstwhile Indira Awaas Yojana)  for construction of a pucca house. In \naddition, there is provision of 90/95 persondays of  unskilled labour wages through \nconvergence with Mahatma Gandhi National Rural Empl oyment Guarantee Scheme \n(MGNREGS) and Rs. 12,000 for construction of toilet  through convergence with Swachh \nBharat Mission – Gramin (SBM-G), MGNREGS or any oth er dedicated source of \nfunding. At present, there is no proposal for enhancing the unit assistance under PMAY-G. \n  \n(e): For households, who though eligible as per the  provisions of the scheme, could not be \nincluded in the Permanent Wait List (PWL) of PMAY-G prepared on the basis of SECC 2011 \ndata, an exercise was conducted across the country for identifying such households and \ncapturing their details through the mobile applicat ion “Awaas+”. A total of 3.67 crore \nhouseholds has been uploaded on Awaas+ from across the country. The Ministry has \nconstituted an Expert Committee for analysis of “Awaas+” data. \n \n(f): State/UT-wise and year-wise number of houses c onstructed under PMAY-G, as on \n06.02.2020, are given in Annexure II. \n \n(g): The remaining houses are targeted for construction by 2022. \n \n********  \n\nAnnexure I \n \nANNEXURE REFERRED IN STATEMENT IN REPLY TO PART (b)  OF LOK \nSABHA STARRED QUESTION NO.*139 TO BE ANSWERED ON 11 .02.2020 \nREGARDING IMPLEMENTATION OF PMAY-G \nStates/UT-wise target, number of houses sanctioned and completed under PMAY-G, as on \n06.02.2020  \nS. \nNo. State Name Phase I (2016-17 to 2018-19) Phase II (2019-20) \nTarget Sanctioned  Completed  Target Sanctioned  Completed  \n1 \nARUNACHAL \nPRADESH 11221 \n 2528  156  7500  421  0 \n2 ASSAM 259814  235608  202524  256043  166375  14632  \n3 BIHAR  1176617  1133362  790498  1012359  787858  60645  \n4 CHHATTISGARH 788235  788235  737812  151100  151094  224  \n5 GOA 427  161  25  0 0 0 \n6 GUJARAT 204703  204336  191021  130301  91413  9554  \n7 HARYANA 21502  21031  16702  0 0 0 \n8 \nHIMACHAL \nPRADESH 7385 \n 7015  6710  900  794  134  \n9 \nJAMMU AND \nKASHMIR 38772* \n 33087  19597  62932  35241  82  \n10 JHARKHAND 528791  528795  486792  322000  292498  63159  \n11 KERALA 42431  17577  16589  0 0 0 \n12 MADHYA PRADESH  1403593  1402823  1336988  832100  372723  148015  \n13 MAHARASHTRA 449820  432874  370094  354501  242694  24611  \n14  MANIPUR  9740  9748  8356  8900  186  0 \n15 MEGHALAYA 20745  20697  15407  17200  6900  52  \n16 MIZORAM 6600  6220  2427  1500  0 0 \n17 NAGALAND 8481  4244  1060  5900  0 0 \n18 ODISHA 992558  992560  873894  740464  538627  177545  \n19 PUNJAB 14000  13984  13608  10000  2823  0 \n20 RAJASTHAN 687091  686213  660803  450816  363764  61215  \n21  SIKKIM  1079  1079  1045  0 0 0 \n22 TAMIL NADU 327552  327535  208659  200000  54175  4018  \n23 TRIPURA 24989  24989  24290  28838  19768  1268  \n24 UTTAR PRADESH 1282616  1280947  1259378  178900  164817  "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|3|UNSTARRED |14", "t": "Funds Allocated for Conservation of Monuments", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA\nMINISTRY OF FINANCE\nDEPARTMENT OF FINANCIAL SERVICES\nLOK SABHA\nSTARRED QUESTION NO. *14\nTO BE ANSWERED ON THE 3rd FEBRUARY, 2020, MAGHA 14, 1941 (SAKA)\nEDUCATION LOAN\n*14. SHRI THOMAS CHAZHIKADAN:\nWill the Minister of FINANCE be pleased to state:\n(a) whether the Government is aware of the delay in disbursal of education loan by\nnationalised banks;\n(b) if so, the details thereof;\n(c) whether the Government is aware that the nationalised banks are demanding collateral\nsecurities for approving education loans;\n(d) if so, the details thereof; and\n(e) the number of education loan applications approved by nationalised banks in the country\nduring the last three years?\nANSWER\nFINANCE MINISTER\n(SMT. NIRMALA SITHARAMAN)\n(a) to (e): A Statement is laid on the Table of the House.\n******\n\nSTATEMENT REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PART (A) TO (E) OF LOK SABHA\nSTARRED QUESTION NUMBER *14 FOR 3 rd FEBRUARY, 2020 REGARDING\nEDUCATION LOAN BY SHRI THOMAS CHAZHIKADAN\n(a) to (e): Education loans are sanctioned as per the guidelines contained in the Model\nEducational Loan Scheme laid down by Indian Banks’ Association (IBA) whichinter-alia\nprovides for education loan applications to be normally disposed off by banks within 15\ndays to one month of the receipt of loan application complete in all respects, and\ncommunication of decision regarding sanction/rejection within 15 days of the receipt of\ncomplete application. Further, the Scheme has several provisions to enable speedy disposal\nof loan applications including, inter-alia, issue of standard acknowledgement with\nreference number on receipt of application containing contact details of the bank official to\nbe contacted in case of delay in disposal of application; rejection of loan application, if\nany, to be done with the concurrence of the controlling authority of the branch concerned,\nand to be conveyed to the applicant stating clearly the reasons for rejection; and\ndisbursement of the loan in stages as per the requirement/demand directly to the\nInstitutions/Vendors of equipments or instruments to the extent possible in order to avoid\ndelays. Government has also launched an online portal viz; Vidya Lakshmi Portal to ensure\nhassle free education loans through a single window system to students. Any complaints\nreceived in respect of delay in sanction or disbursement of educational loans, including\nthose received on the Centralized Public Grievance Redress and Monitoring System\n(CPGRAMS) which are required to be disposed within 60 days, are taken up with the\nconcerned bank for redressal.\nAs per Reserve Bank of India (RBI) guidelines, all education loans up to Rs 4 lakh are\ncollateral free. Further, the Government has launched a Credit Guarantee Fund Scheme for\nEducational Loan (CGFSEL) wherein collateral free loan is given up to Rs 7.5 lakh.\nAs per the information furnished by Public Sector Banks (PSBs), the number of education\nloan applications approved by them during the last three years in the country is as under:\nYear Number of Applications Approved\n2016-17 196077\n2017-18 216962\n2018-19 199021\n2019-2020\n(Upto December, 2019-\nprovisional)\n164168\n******"}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|3|UNSTARRED |140", "t": "Vacant Posts in Universities", "head": " GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF HUMAN RESOURCE DEVELOPMENT \nDEPARTMENT OF HIGHER EDUCATION \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION No. 140 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 03.02.2020 \n \nVacant Posts in Universities \n \n†140. SHRI KANAKMAL KATARA:  \n \n     Will the Minister of HUMAN RESOURCE DEVELOPMENT be pleased to state: \n \n(a)  whether the studies are getting adversely affected due to academic and other staff \nposts lying vacant in the Universities of the country;  \n(b)   if so, the details of the posts lying vacant, University-wise; \n(c)   the details of the budgetary allocations made to the Rajasthan, University-wise; and  \n(d)   the time by which the professors and academic staff are likely to be appointed? \n \n        ANSWER \nMINISTER OF HUMAN RESOURCE DEVELOPMENT \n             (SHRI RAMESH POKHRIYAL ‘NISHANK’) \n \n(a) to (d): Occurrence and filling up of vacancies is a continuous process. The onus of \nfilling up the posts lies on Central Universities which are autonomous bodies created under \nActs of Parliament. However, the University Grants Commission (UGC) continuously \nmonitors it with Universities.  UGC has prepared the Guidelines for Recruitment of Faculty \nin Universities, Colleges and Institutions Deemed to be Universities outlining the selection \nprocedure and the time frame for recruitment which has been circulated to all Universities \nto adhere  to the guidelines. The u niversities have also been requested to take steps to \nensure that the vacancies in th e university as well as in the c olleges affiliated to university \n\nare filled up at the earliest and also the status of the recruitment process is uploaded on \nthe University Activity Monitoring Portal of UGC.  \nIn order to ensure that studies of students are not affected, the Central \nUniversities undertakes various temporary measures such as hiring ad -hoc faculty, Guest \nfaculty, Contract Facult y and Re -employment of faculty. At present, there are total \n1,77,235 sanctioned teaching posts and 3,69,587  sanctioned non teachings posts in \nvarious universities a cross the country out of which 32 ,581 teachings posts and 93,696 \nnon teaching posts  are lying vacant. A detailed statement of the teaching and non \nteachings posts in the Universities is available at \nhttps://www.ugc.ac.in/pdfnews/0053281_LSPQ-No.-140,-Annexure-I.pdf \nand https://www.ugc.ac.in/pdfnews/0567047_LSPQ-No.-140,-Annexure-II.pdf respectively.  \nFurther, a detailed statement of budgetary allocation, as provided by UGC,  made \nto Universities of Rajasthan is as under:  \n \n***** \nS.No. Name of the University Allocation (in lakhs) \n Central University (2019-20) \n1. Central University of Rajasthan 4443.48 \n State University (XII Plan Period (2012 – 2017) (extended upto 31.03.2020) \n2. J.N. Vyas University, Jodhpur 1720.00 \n3. M.D.S. University, Ajmer 1161.00 \n4. M.L. Sukhadia University, Udaipur 1193.00 \n5. Rajasthan University, Jaipur  2611.00 \n6. National Law University, Jodhpur (Rajasthan) 800.00 \n7. University of Kota, Kota, Rajasthan 700.00 \n8. Maharaja Ganga Singh University, Bikaner \nRajasthan \n280.00 \n Deemed to be Universities (2018-19) \n9. Bansathali Vidyapith, Banasthali,  No budget allocation has been \nmade. \n10. Jain VishwaBharti, Ladnun No budget allocation has been \nmade. \nTotal 12908.48 "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|3|UNSTARRED |141", "t": "Appointment of Women Directors in SEBI", "head": " \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF FINANCE \n DEPARTMENT OF ECONOMIC AFFAIRS  \n \nLOK SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION NO. 141 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON MARCH 2, 2020 \n \n EXTERNAL DEBT \n \n*141. KUNWAR DANISH ALI: \n        \n Will the Minister of FINANCE be pleased to state: \n \n(a)  the details of the external debt of the country, both long-term as well as short-term, during the   \n      last three years along with the rates of interest applicable thereon; \n(b) the rank of India among the debt-ridden countries of the world; \n(c) the details of the total amount repaid in the form of principal and interest to the foreign lenders  \n     during each of the last three years and the current year along with the total amount outstanding  \n     as on date, country-wise and institution-wise; and \n(d) the details of steps taken by the Government to lessen the loan burden of the country? \n \n \nANSWER \n \nTHE FINANCE MINISTER \n(SMT. NIRMALA SITHARAMAN) \n \n(a) to (d): A Statement is laid on the Table of the House.  \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n\nSTATEMENT REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO THE LOK SABHA  \nSTARRED QUESTION NO. 141 BY KUNWAR DANISH ALI  \nDUE FOR ANSWER ON MARCH 2, 2020 \n….. \n \n(a) & (c): Owing to strong macroeconomic fundamentals including a manageable current account   \ndeficit, India’s external debt to GDP ratio has been low at about 20 percent of GDP during the last \nthree years. Data on India’s external debt, principal and interest paid to foreign lenders are placed \nat Annexure. \n \n      (b): As per IMF and World Bank data, India’s external debt to gross national income (GNI) ratio in \n2018 was lowest among the advanced nations of G -20 and 36 OECD (Organization for Economic \nCooperation and Development) countries. India’s external debt to GNI ratio was also lower than \ntwo-thirds of around 150 low and middle income countries. Further, India ’s external debt per citizen \nin 2018 was less than all G-20 and OECD countries.  \n \n    (d): India’s external debt burden is very low. For raising the investment rate in the economy, there is \na need to increase foreign borrowings to take advantage of lower rate of interest abroad.  \n***** \n                                                                                                                        Annexure \n \nExternal debt outstanding - Annexure to Part (a) \nYear Long Term Short Term Total Interest rate \n(year end figures in US$ billion) percent \n2016-17 383.2 88.1 471.3 3.5 \n2017-18 427.1 102.2 529.3 3.4 \n2018-19 434.8 108.4 543.2 4.0 \n   Source: External Debt Management Unit (EDMU), Aid Accounts & Audit Division (CAAA),  \n   D/o Economic Affairs, M/o Finance and RBI. \n   Note: Interest rate is calculated as (total interest payments on long term debt during a year / (outstanding \n   long term debt at year end). \n \nPrincipal and interest payments - Annexure to Part (c) \n \nYear \nMultilateral Bilateral \nPrincipal Interest Outstanding \ndebt \n(year end) \nPrincipal Interest Outstanding \ndebt \n(year end) \nAll figures in US$ million \n2016-17 4,062 761 59,912 2,189 537 25,906 \n2017-18 4,016 960 63,033 2,283 482 28,321 \n2018-19 4,386 1,383 62,977 2,062 679 28,563 \n2019-20 (Apr-Sep) 2,358 925 64,207 834 266 29,471 \n  Source: External Debt Management Unit (EDMU), Aid Accounts & Audit Division (CAAA),  \n  D/o Economic Affairs, M/o Finance and RBI. \n***** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|3|UNSTARRED |142", "t": "Violence and Assaults", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF HUMAN RESOURCE DEVELOPMENT \nDEPARTMENT OF HIGHER EDUCATION \n \nLOK SABHA \n \nUNSTARRED QUESTION No. 142 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 03.02.2020 \n \nViolence and Assaults \n142. SHRI KODIKUNNIL SURESH: \nWill the Minister of HUMAN RESOURCE DEVELOPMENT be pleased to state:  \n \n(a) whether the Government is aware of the large scale violence and assault against students of \nJawaharlal Nehru University;  \n(b) if so, has the Government taken cognizance of the matter;  \n(c) whether the Government has constituted a high level enquiry committee to investigate the \nmatters pertaining to attacks against students in JNU and ascertain the veracity of allegations \nagainst the present Vice Chancellor about making preferential a ppointments made in various \nteaching posts in the university;  \n(d) whether the government is aware of any reports of students being unable to register for the \nwinter semester in JNU due to disruption of registration services for the upcoming academic \nsession; and  \n(e) if so, the details thereof? \nANSWER \n \n MINISTER OF HUMAN RESOURCE DEVELOPMENT \n(Shri Ramesh Pokhriyal 'Nishank') \n \n(a) to (e):  An incident of violence in the campus of Jawaharlal Nehru University has been \nreported. University has informed that the Crime Branch of Delhi Police is investigating the matter. \nThe registration process for Winter Semester 2020 without fine is going on smoothly. \n \n***** \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|3|UNSTARRED |143", "t": "Monitoring of Bank Frauds", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF SKILL DEVELOPMENT AND ENTREPRENEURSHIP \n \nLOK SABHA \n \nSTARRED QUESTION NO. 143   \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 02.03.2020 \n \nTARGETS UNDER PMKVY \n \n† *143. SHRI MANOJ TIWARI: \n SHRIMATI VEENA DEVI: \n \nWill the Minister of SKILL DEVELOPMENT AND ENTREPRE NEURSHIP be \npleased to state : \n \n(a) whether the targets were/have been fixed under Pradhan Mantri Kaushal Vikas \nYojana (PMKVY) for the years 2018-19, 2019-20 and 2020-21; \n \n(b) if so, the details thereof, year wise; \n \n(c) if not, the reasons therefor; \n \n(d) the details of the changes made in PMKVY since its inception; \n \n(e) whether it is a fact that due to these changes,  about 5-7 thousand small centres \nthroughout the country have become dysfunctional du e to their high operational costs and \nthe persons running these centres are burdened with  loans ranging from 20 to 25 lakhs; \nand \n \n(f) the plans being formulated by the Government to  assist these small centres? \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER IN THE MINISTRY OF  \nSKILL DEVELOPMENT AND ENTREPRENEURSHIP \n(DR. MAHENDRA NATH PANDEY) \n \n(a) to (f) A Statement is laid on the Table of the House.   \n  \n  \n\nSTATEMENT REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PARTS (A) TO (F) OF LOK SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION NO. 143 TO BE ANSWERED ON 02.03.20 20 ASKED BY SHRI \nMANOJ TIWARI AND SHRIMATI VEENA DEVI  REGARDING TARGETS UNDER \nPMKVY  \n \n(a) to (c) PMKVY (Pradhan Mantri Kaushal Vikas Yoja na) – 2.0 launched in the month \nof October, 2016 is targeted to impart skill training to 1 crore youth  for over 4 years (2016-\n20).  Under the scheme, 4 year targets were distributed year-wise as follows: \n \nShort Term Training (STT)  Physical Targets \n2016-17 15,00,000 \n2017-18 15,00,000 \n2018 -19  15,00,000 \n2019 -20  15,00,000 \nSub Total  60,00,000  \nRecognition of Prior Learning (RPL) for 2016-20 40, 00,000 \nGrand Total 1,00,00,000 \n \nAs on 17.01.2020, 40.27 lakh candidates have been t rained under short term \ntraining (STT) and 33.20 lakh candidates have been oriented under Recognition of Prior \nLearning (RPL) across the country. \n \n(d) PMKVY 1.0 was implemented for one year during t he financial year 2015-16 \nwhereas PMKVY 2.0 is being implemented during 2016- 20.  Major changes brought out in \nPMKVY 2.0 with respect to PMKVY 1.0 are involvement  of States for imparting skill \ntraining, affiliation and accreditation of training  centres through online SMART portal, all \njob roles are NSQF aligned, mandatory Training of T rainers (ToT), etc. Further, various IT \nmeasures and interventions such as Direct Benefit T ransfer, Aadhaar linkage of trainers \nand trainees, Biometric attendance of candidate etc  have been ensured under the new \nscheme.  \n \n(e) and (f) No, it is not a fact that due to the ch anges under PMKVY 2.0, a large number \nof small centres have become dysfunctional. However , under PMKVY 2.0 (2016-20) in the \nyear 2017 more than 2,500 training centres which we re franchisee centres were not \nallocated targets because of not meeting the training quality. \n \nTo enable erstwhile franchisee centres to convert t hemselves into TP owned \ncentres, multiple efforts were undertaken by NSDC a nd MSDE viz. setting up of call \ncentres, hand-holding workshops, developing user ma nuals, check list for physical \ninspection, etc.  Consequently, about 90% of such c entres have again become eligible for \nallocation of targets. \n \n******* "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|3|UNSTARRED |144", "t": "Committee Report on Forest Rights Act, 2006", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF FINANCE \nDEPARTMENT OF REVENUE \nLOK SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION No. 144 \n \nTO BE ANSWERED ON MONDAY, MARCH 2, 2020/PHALGUNA 12, 1941 \n(SAKA) \n \nIRREGULARITIES IN GST \n \n*144. SHRI KOMATI REDDY VENKAT REDDY: \n \nWill the Minister of FINANCE be pleased to state:  \n \n(a) whether the Government has taken a decision to regulate the false claims and other \nirregularities in the Goods and Services Tax (GST);  \n(b) if so, the details thereof; and \n(c) the details of the submissions made by the authorities of the State Governments in \nthis regard? \nANSWER \nMINISTER OF FINANCE \n(Ms. NIRMALA SITHARAMAN) \n�\n(a) to (c) A Statement is laid on the table of the House. \n \n\nSTATEMENT AS REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PARA A TO C OF LOKSABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION NO.144 FOR ANSWER ON 2 nd MARCH,2020/PHALGUNA \n12, 1941 (SAKA)REGARDING IRREGULARITIES IN GST. \n \nPart (a) and part (b):  Yes Sir. To identify cases of fraudulent claims under GST, the \nGovernment has taken measures to apply stringent risk parameters -based checks driven \nby rigorous data analytics and Artificial Intelligence (AI) tools based on which certain \ntaxpayers are identified whose exports and supply chain are subjected to rigorous \nchecks. Moreover, in order to facilitate exports, a standard operating procedure has been \nprescribed for exporters vide Circular No. 131/1/2020 -GST dated 23.01.2020 to \nmitigate the risk of wrongful refund claims of IGST (Integrated Goods and Services \nTax).  \n \nTo curb cases of wrongful claims of input tax credit, sub -rule (4) of rule 36 has been \ninserted to Central Goods and Services Tax Rules, 2017 vide notification No. 49/2019 – \nCentral Tax dated 09.10.2019. Vide notification No. 75/2019 – Central Tax dated \n26.12.2019, rule 86A has been inserted to CGST Rules, 2017 which e mpowers tax \nofficer, not below the rank of Assistant Commissioner, to block input tax credit \navailable in the electronic credit ledger of a taxpayer if he has reasons to believe that \nsuch credit is ineligible or has been availed fraudulently. \nPart (c): Various submissions have been made by the authorities of the State \nGovernments in this regard, inter-alia, including the following: \ni.� E-way bill verification should be undertaken by tax officers  with special watch \nfor inter -State transactions. Task forces consi sting of State and Central GST \nauthorities need to be set up to undertake such verification. \nii.� Emphasis should be placed on enforcement. \niii.� In order to weed out bogus registrations obtained by unscrupulous persons on the \nbasis of false documents, it has been su ggested to encourage tax officers to visit \nthe premises of taxpayers/dealers post registration to verify their genuineness. \niv.� The mechanism of ‘invoice matching’ should be implemented as it would go a \nlong way in curtailing cases of input tax credit frauds.  \nv.� A robust reporting system should be put in place to generate real time tax alerts \non detection of any suspicious and fraudulent transactions. \n \nBased on the valuable feedback and suggestions received from multiple stakeholders \nincluding State Governments, the GST Council makes recommendations and necessary \naction is taken by the Government. \n�\n���������� \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|3|UNSTARRED |145", "t": "Higher Education", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF HUMAN RESOURCE DEVELOPMENT \nDEPARTMENT OF SCHOOL EDUCATION & LITERACY \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 145 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 03.02.2020 \n \nHigher Education  \n \n†145. SHRI MAHABALI SINGH:  \n \n Will the Minister of HUMAN RESOURCE DEVELOPMENT be pleased to state: \n(a) Whether there is huge gap in gross enrolment ratio of boys and girls at \nsecondary, senior secondary and higher educational levels; \n(b) if so, the details thereof and the reasons therefor; and \n(c)  the manner in which the Government is planning to tackle this serious \nproblem? \n \nA N S W E R \n \nMINISTER OF HUMAN RESOURCE DEVELOPMENT \n(SHRI RAMESH POKHRIYAL ‘NISHANK’) \n \n(a) As per the Unified District Information System for Education Plus \n(UDISE+) 2018 -19 (Provisional), and as per All India Survey on Higher \nEducation 2018 -19 (AISHE), the Gross Enrolment Ratio ( GER) for boys \nand girls  at Secondary, Senior Secondary and Higher Education are given \nbelow: \n GER boys GER girls Source \nSecondary 78.00 81.32 UDISE+ 2018-19 provisional \nSenior Secondary 57.54 59.70 \nHigher education 26.29 26.36 AISHE 2018-19 \n \n(b) & (c) Does not arise.      \n \n********** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|3|UNSTARRED |146", "t": "Vacant Posts of Central Universities", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF LABOUR AND EMPLOYMENT \nLOK SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION NO.*146 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 02ND MARCH, 2020 \n \nUNEMPLOYMENT \n \n*146.  SHRI KAUSHALENDRA KUMAR: \n \nWill the Minister of LABOUR AND EMPLOYMENT be pleased to state: \n \n(a) whether it is a fact that unemployment is very common among the youth \nin the country; \n \n(b) if so, whether the Government is taking necessary steps to eradicate it \nby creating more Government and public sector job opportunities; \n \n(c) whether the Government has any plan to support private sector \nindustries to enable them to provide more jobs to the deserving youth; \nand \n \n(d) if so, the details thereof? \n \nANSWER \nMINISTER OF STATE (IC) FOR LABOUR AND EMPLOYMENT  \n(SHRI SANTOSH KUMAR GANGWAR) \n \n(a) to (d): A statement is laid on the Table of the House. \n** \n \n \n  \n\nSTATEMENT REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PARTS (a) to (d) OF LOK SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION NO. *146 DUE FOR REPLY ON 02 -03-2020 BY SHRI \nKAUSHALENDRA KUMAR REGARDING “UNEMPLOYMENT”.  \n \n(a) to (d): As per the results of annual Periodic Labour  Force Survey (PLFS) \nconducted by National Statistical Office (NSO), Ministry of Statistics and \nProgramme Implementation during 2017-18, the estimated unemployment rate \non usual status (principal status + subsidiary status) basis for persons of age \n15 years and above in the country was 6.0%.  \n \nEmployment generation coupled with improving employability is the \npriority of the Government. Government has taken various steps for \ngenerating employment in the country like encouraging private sector of \neconomy, fast-tracking various projects involving substantial investment and \nincreasing public expenditure on schemes such as Prime Minister’s \nEmployment Generation Programme (PMEGP), Mahatma Gandhi National Rural \nEmployment Guarantee Scheme (MGNREGS), Pt. Deen  Dayal Upadhyaya \nGrameen Kaushalya Yojana (DDU -GKY) and Deendyal  Antodaya Yojana-\nNational Urban Livelihoods Mission (DAY-NULM).  \n \nPromoting entrepreneurship is essential for enabling the youth to \nproductively contribute to the economic development of the country.  \nAccordingly, the Government of India has accorded priority to the \nentrepreneurship through various measures such as Startup India, Mudra \nScheme, etc. so as to train and finance entrepreneurs. \n \nUnder Skill India Mission, Ministry of Skill Development and \nEntrepreneurship is implementing a flagship scheme known as the Pradhan \nMantri Kaushal VikasYojana (PMKVY) 2016 -20 with an objective to provide \nskilling to one crore people under Short Term Training (STT) and Recognition \nof Prior Learning (RPL) across the country for four years i.e. 2016-2020.  \n \n Pradhan Mantri Mudra Yojana (PMMY) has been initiated by Government \ninter alia for facilitating self -employment. Under PMMY collateral free loans \nupto Rs. 10 lakh, are extended to micro/small business enterprises an d to \nindividuals to enable them to setup or expand their business activities.  \n\n Government has implemented the National Career Service (NCS) Project \nwhich comprises a digital portal that provides a nation -wide online platform \nfor the job seekers and employ ers for job-matching in a dynamic, efficient and \nresponsive manner and has a repository of career content to job seekers.   \n \nThe National Manufacturing Policy identifies employment intensive \nindustries like textiles and garments, leather and footwear, gems  and \njewellery and food processing, etc. as special focus attention sectors. The \npolicy provides special attention for Small Scale Industries as they offer \nemployment opportunities both for self -employment and jobs across diverse \ngeographies.  \n \nBesides these initiatives, flagship programmes of the Government such \nas Make in India, Digital India, Swachh Bharat Mission, Smart City Mission, \nAtal Mission for Rejuvenation and Urban Transformation, Housing for All, \nInfrastructure development and industrial corrid ors have the potential to \ngenerate productive employment opportunities. Ministries/Departments/States \nrun skill development schemes across various sectors to improve the \nemployability of youth and also facilitate placements. Schemes such as the \nNational Ap prenticeship Promotion Scheme (NAPS) wherein Government \nreimburses 25 percent of the stipend payable to apprentices also enhances \nemployability of the youth to access employment. \n \n Pradhan Mantri Rojgar Protsahan Yojana (PMRPY) was launched by the \nMinistry of Labour and Employment for incentivising employers for promoting \nemployment generation. Under this scheme, Government is paying entire \nemployer’s contribution (12% or as admissible) towards EPF and EPS for all \neligible new employees for all sectors for 3 years. The last date for \nregistration of beneficiaries under PMRPY through employer was 31 st March \n2019. \n***** \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|3|UNSTARRED |147", "t": "Steel Plants in Bihar", "head": "LOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.147 \nFOR ANSWER ON 03/02/2020 \n \nSTEEL PLANTS IN BIHAR \n \n147. SHRI GOPAL JEE THAKUR: \n \nWill the Minister of STEEL be pleased to state: \n \n(a) the total number of steel plants in  the country and the names of such steel  \nplants on which work is going on at present; \n \n(b) whether the Government proposes  to set up new steel plants in near future in  \nDarbhanga in Bihar for generating/increasing employment opportunities; \n \n(c) if so, the time by which the said  plants are likely to be set u p and if not, the  \nreasons therefor, and \n \n(d) whether the import of steel has  increased during the last few years and if  so, \nthe details thereof and its effects on domestic market and indigenous steel? \n \nANSWER \nTHE MINISTER OF STEEL      (SHRI DHARMENDRA PRADHAN) \n \n(a) The total number of steel plants in the country is 977.  In the public sector, \nwork is currently going on in NMDC Steel Plant in Nagarnar, Chattisgarh which is a \nGreenfield steel plant. \n \n(b)&(c): No, Sir.  Steel is a deregulated sector where market forces and commercial \nconsiderations decide construction of new steel plants. \n \n(d) The import of steel has increased marginally in the last 3 years from 7.23 \nMillion Tonnes in 2016-17 to 7.84 Million Tonnes in 2018-19 as shown table below:-  \n  \nImport of Steel (in MT) \nYear Import \n2016-17 7.23 \n2017-18 7.48 \n2018-19 7.84 \nSource:        JPC \n \nHowever, domestic steel production has consistently increased regardless \nof such imports. Import of certain steel grades which are not manufactured or \nmanufactured in adequate quantity in the country is required for supporting \nmanufacturing sector as well as the country’s  export of  machinery, automobiles \netc.  \n \n******** \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|3|UNSTARRED |148", "t": "Revenue Contribution", "head": " \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF HUMAN RESOURCE DEVELOPMENT \nDEPARTMENT OF SCHOOL EDUCATION & LITERACY \nLOK SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION NO. 148 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 02.03.2020 \n \nLiteracy Rate in Jharkhand \n \n*148    Shri Vishnu Dayal Ram: \n \nWill the Minister of HUMAN RESOURCE DEVELOPMENT be pleased to state: \n \n(a) the reasons for low literacy rate in Jharkhand; \n(b) whether the Saakshar Bharat Programme has been implemented in Jharkhand and if so, \nthe details thereof along with the funds allocation to Jharkhand to improve the literacy \nrate during the last three years; \n(c) the achievements made under the Saakshar Bharat Programme in Jharkhand; \n(d) whether any special provisions have been made to impart education in the left wing \nextremism affected districts in Jharkhand and if so, the details thereof; and \n(e) whether any special provisions have been made to impart education to the women and \nother disadvantaged groups in Jharkhand and if so, the details thereof?  \n \nANSWER \nMINISTER OF HUMAN RESOURCE DEVELOPMENT \n(SHRI RAMESH  POKHARIYAL ‘NISHANK’) \n \n(a) to (e) A statement is laid on the Table of House. \n \n*** \nSTATEMENT REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PART ( a )  to ( e) OF LOK SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION NO. 148 FOR 02.03.2020 ASKED BY SHRI VISHNU DAYAL \nRAM REGARDING “LITERACY RATE IN JHARKHAND”. \n \n \n\n(a) Various factors viz poverty, gender and social inequities etc are impeding literacy in \nJharkhand State. \n(b) Yes, Saakshar Bharat Programme has been implemented in Jharkhand from 2009 till \nMarch 2018. Details of fund allocation to Jharkhand to imp rove the literacy rate during the last \nthree years is as under:- \nYear Central Share Released State Share Released Total funds released \n2016-17 6,30,24,000 4,20,16,000 10,50,40,000 \n2017-18* -- -- -- \n2018-19 6,00,00,000 4,00,00,000 10,00,00,000 \nGrand Total 12,30,24,000 8,20,16,000 20,50,40,000 \n* The State had a high unspent balance and important information/documents were not \nfurnished by the State, due to which funds could not be released. \n \n(c) In Jharkhand, as per Census 2011, adult literacy rate is 66. 41 percent, as compared to the \nnational average adult literacy rate of 72.98 percent. Saakshar Bharat programme covered 410 \ndistricts in the country, including 24 districts of Jharkhand. \nBasic Literacy Assessment Test was conducted by NIOS to assess the proficiency level \nof functional literacy skills attained by learners. In Jharkhand, total number of 50,75,964 adult \nlearner appeared in the test and 41,44,515 succeeded (including 25.94 lakh women) \n \n(d)& (e)   Under the Saakshar Bharat Programme, districts  with female literacy rate of 50 per \ncent or below (as per 2001 Census) and all districts worst affected by left wing extremist \nviolence, were eligible for coverage under the programme.  Accordingly, a total 410 districts, \nincluding 30 worst affected left wing extremism (13 in Jharkhand) districts were identified as \neligible for coverage under the programme.  \n \nThe programme achieved 7.64 crore adult literates, (including 5.38 crore women), against \nthe target of 7 crore, as per NIOS Basic Literacy Assessment  Test results, till the last test \nconducted in March 2018. The program covered 1.88 crore SCs, 1.08 crore STs, 75 lakh \nMinority adult learners who were certified literate. In the state of Jharkhand, 25.94 lakh female \nand 15.50 lakh male adult learners (tot al 41.44 lakh adults) were made literate, including 6.95 \nlakh SC, 12.45 lakh ST and 4.65 lakh minority learners. \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|3|UNSTARRED |149", "t": "General Budget", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF FINANCE \nDEPARTMENT OF ECONOMIC AFFAIRS \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 149 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON FEBRUARY 3, 2020 \n \nGENERAL BUDGET \n \n149. SHRI  A. GANESHAMURTHI:  \n        SHRI A.K.P. CHINRAJ: \n  \nWill the Minister of FINANCE be pleased to state: \n \n(a) whether the Government has invited various industrial groups and trade/ business  \n     organizations for consultations for preparing the general budget for the year 2020 as well  \n     as requested to give their feedback, suggestions for inclusion in the general budget;  \n(b) if so, the details thereof; \n(c) whether the Government has extended the invitation to the farmers/ agriculturists to give  \n     their suggestions and feedback for pre-budget consultations; and  \n(d) if so, the details thereof and if not, the reasons therefor? \n \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF FINANCE \n(SHRI ANURAG SINGH THAKUR) \n \n(a) to (d ) Yes, Sir.   The Ministry of Finance holds the Pre-Budget consultations with the \nvarious stakeholders including represent atives of industrial groups , trade/business \norganizations and farmers/agriculturists and their suggestions/ideas are noted for preparing \nthe Union Budget . The proposals an d suggestions received from industrial groups , \ntrade/business organizations  and farme rs/agriculturists covered a range of themes/issues \nwhich, inter alia, related to revival of growth and investment, promotion of exports, ease of \ndoing business, promotion of agriculture, animal husbandry and agro processing sector and \nempowerment of farmers. \n***** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|3|UNSTARRED |15", "t": "Funds Allocated in Jharkhand under PMKVY", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF SKILL DEVELOPMENT AND ENTREPRENEURSHIP \n \nLOK SABHA \n \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 15   \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 03.02.2020 \n \nFUNDS ALLOCATED IN JHARKHAND UNDER PMKVY \n \n† 15. SHRIMATI ANNPURNA DEVI: \nSHRI PASHUPATI NATH SINGH: \n \nWill the Minister of SKILL DEVELOPMENT AND ENTREPRE NEURSHIP be pleased to \nstate : \n \n(a) the details of funds allocated for and utilised  by Jharkhand under the Pradhan Mantri \nKaushal Vikas Yojana during the last three years; \n \n(b) the district-wise details of the number of pers ons benefited by the said scheme in \nJharkhand; \n \n(c) whether the Government is monitoring the centre s, running under the Pradhan Mantri \nKaushal Vikas Yojana; and \n \n(d) the reaction of the Government thereto? \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF  \nSKILL DEVELOPMENT AND ENTREPRENEURSHIP \n(SHRI R. K. SINGH) \n \n(a) Pradhan Mantri Kaushal Vikas Yojana (PMKVY) (20 16-20) has two components known as \nCentrally Sponsored Centrally Managed (CSCM) being implemented by the National Skill \nDevelopment Corporation (NSDC) and Centrally Sponso red State Managed (CSSM) being \nimplemented by State Skill Development Missions of the States/ UTs. \n \nUnder CSCM component of PMKVY 2016-20, there is no provision of State-wise funds \nallocation. However, under CSSM component of PMKVY 2016-20, funds and corresponding \nphysical targets of PMKVY 2016-20 have been allocat ed to the States/UTs for the implementation \nof the scheme through State Skill Development Missi ons. Under this component, after evaluation \nof proposal received from Jharkhand Skill Developme nt Mission Society (JSDMS), Government of \nJharkhand, Ministry has given in-principle approval  of a total target of 57,668 candidates and \ncorresponding financial allocation of Rs. 88.79 Cr.  for FY 2017-20; out of which, Rs. 29.59 Cr. has \nbeen disbursed to State of Jharkhand during FY 2017-18 for the implementation of this component \nin the State. However, due to slow performance and keeping in the mind that the scheme is going \nto end by March’2020, the financial allocation was rationalized to Rs. 73.99 crore.  \n \n(b) The district-wise number of candidates trained / oriented under PMKVY (2016-20) in State \nof Jharkhand are given at Annexure . \n \n(c) and (d) Under PMKVY (2016-20), there are variou s provisions for monitoring of TCs as well \nas candidates. The life cycle of training process o f candidates (enrollment-training-assessment-\ncertification-placement) are tracked or monitored o n real-time basis through the Skill Development \nManagement System (SDMS) which is linked with Aadha ar enabled biometric attendance. \nEmpanelled TCs are being monitored effectively thro ugh various methodologies such as self-audit \nreporting, call validations, surprise visits, social media etc \n \nAdditionally, PMKVY Monitoring Committees review and formulate framework on the critical \ncases related to monitoring issues of the stakeholders. Also, the Committee has formulated penalty \ngrid (approved by Steering Committee for PMKVY) for  taking action against erring / non compliant \ntraining centres / stakeholders. Based on the penal ty grid, National Skill Development Corporation \n(NSDC), the implementing agency of PMKVY under MSDE , has suspended training centers for \nnoncompliance of PMKVY 2016-20 guidelines on multip le parameters and periodically publishes \nthe list on PMKVY official website.  \n \n\nAnnexure \n \nThe district wise no. of candidates trained / oriented under PMKVY (2016-20) in State \nof Jharkhand, as on 17.01.2020, are given below: \n \nS. No.  District  Trained / oriented  \n1.  Bokaro 8090  \n2.  Chatra 2690  \n3.  Deoghar 11669  \n4.  Dhanbad 7914  \n5.  Dumka 5437  \n6.  East Singhbhum 8029  \n7.  Garhwa 4631  \n8.  Giridih 2175  \n9.  Godda 2988  \n10.  Gumla 1925  \n11.  Hazaribagh 12226  \n12.  Jamtara 1878  \n13.  Khunti 2116  \n14.  Kodarma 4601  \n15.  Latehar 1222  \n16.  Lohardaga 2228  \n17.  Pakur 2169  \n18.  Palamu 3635  \n19.  Ramgarh 7429  \n20.  Ranchi 21012  \n21.  Sahibganj 1506  \n22.  Seraikela Kharsawan 2710  \n23.  Simdega 1428  \n24.  West Singhbhum 3450  \nTotal 123158  \n \n******* "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|3|UNSTARRED |150", "t": "Misappropriation of Funds", "head": " \n \n \n \nLOK SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION NO.*150 \nFOR ANSWER ON 02/03/2020 \n \nMODERNISATION AND EXPANSION OF STEEL PLANTS \n \n*150. SHRI BALLI DURGA PRASAD RAO: \nSHRIMATI QUEEN OJA: \n \nWill the Minister of STEEL be pleased to state: \n \n(a) whether the Indian steel industry continues to  grapple with uncertainties  \npertaining to availability of consistent supplies of raw materials i.e. coal and iron ore; \n \n(b) if so, the details thereof along with the action taken/proposed to be taken by \nthe Government in this regard; and \n \n(c) the measures taken/proposed to be taken by the  Government in regard to \nmodernisation and expansion of  the existing steel units as well as green -field plants \nto build a world class, cost competitive and socially responsible steel industry? \n \nANSWER \nTHE MINISTER OF STEEL      (SHRI DHARMENDRA PRADHAN) \n \n(a)to(c):  A Statement is laid on the Table of the Lok Sabha. \n \n******** \n  \n\nSTATEMENT REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PARTS (A) TO (C ) OF THE LOK SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION NO. * 150 FOR ANSWER ON 02/03/2020 ASKED BY SHRI \nBALLI DURGA PRASAD RAO AND SHRIMATI QUEEN OJA , MEMBERS OF \nPARLIAMENT REGARDING “ MODERNISATION AND EXPANSION OF STEEL \nPLANTS” \n \n(a)&(b): The production of iron ore in the country is sufficient to meet the current \ndemand/consumption of iron ore by domestic steel industry. However, the entire \ndemand of coking coal is not met from domestic production as the availability of \nhigh-quality coking coal (low -ash coal) in the country is limited and thus no option is \nleft but to resort to import of coking coal. \n \nIn order to further enhance the availability of iron ore in the domestic market, \nMinistry of Mines, Government of India vide Order dated 16.09.2019, SAIL has been \nallowed to make available in the open market 25 percent of its total mineral \nproduction in the previous year. Further, in another separate order dated 16.09.2019, \nSAIL has also been allowed to dispose of the old stock of 70 Million Tonnes of low -\ngrade iron fines and ores (including slime) lying dumped across different captive \nmines of SAIL. \n \nAs regards  coking coal, during 2018 -19 the total demand of coking coal for \nsteel industry was 58.37 MT. Out of this, 51.83 MT were met through imports, 1.6 \nMT was provided by Bharat Coking Coal Limited (BCCL) and Coal India Limited \n(CIL) and remaining was catered by captive collieries of SAIL and TATA Steel. \n \nIn order to secure coking coal supplies for the Indian steel industries, the \nGovernment has taken the following steps:- \n \ni. Setting up of new washeries by BCCL and CIL & enhancement in capacity of \nexisting washery by SAIL. \nii. To diversify the coking coal import sources, efforts are being made to import \ncoking coal from US, Russia and Mongolia. \niii. Recent allocation of Coking Coal Mines to Steel CPSEs, viz Tokisud North \nCoal Mine and Rohne Coal Mine to NMDC and Rabodih OCP Coal Mine to \nRINL. \niv. To grant long term linkage of raw coking coal to SAIL from BCCL. \nv. Extension of Tasra Coking Coal Block lease in favour of SAIL. \n \n(c) Steel is a deregulated sector and the role of Government is limited to that of a \nfacilitator. The decisio ns to modernisation and expansion of steel plants & to set up \nnew steel plants/Greenfield Projects are taken by the respective companies, based \non commercial considerations, market dynamics and techno -economic viability of \nprojects.  \n \n  The steps of modernisation and expansion of Steel CPSEs are as under: \n \ni. SAIL had undertaken modernization and expansion of its five integrated steel \nplants at Bhilai (Chhattisgarh), Bokaro (Jharkhand), Rourkela (Odisha), \nDurgapur (West Bengal) & Burnpur (West Bengal) and Spec ial Steel Plant at \nSalem (Tamil Nadu) to enhance its crude steel capacity from 12.8 Million \ntonne per annum (MTPA) to 21.4 MTPA. \nii. RINL has completed its expansion programme for doubling its capacity from 3 \nMTPA to   6.3 MTPA in April, 2015 at an estimated c ost of Rs 12,291 Cr.  All \nthe units are under operation. Along with modernization, one more Converter \nand Caster have been installed enhancing the production capacity by 1 \nMTPA. i.e., from 6.3 MTPA to 7.3 MTPA. \niii. NMDC is in the process of setting up a 3.0 MT PA Integrated Steel Plant at \nNagarnar, near Jagdalpur, Dist. Bastar, Chhattisgarh. The construction of \nplant is in advanced stage of completion. \n******** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|3|UNSTARRED |151", "t": "State School Education Acts", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF HUMAN RESOURCE DEVELOPMENT \nDEPARTMENT OF SCHOOL EDUCATION & LITERACY \n \nLOK SABHA \n \nSTARRED QUESTION NO.151 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 02.03.2020 \n \nKENDRIYA VIDYALAYAS IN LOK SABHA CONSTITUENCIES \n \n†*151. SHRI ASHOK KUMAR RAWAT: \n \nWill the Minister of HUMAN RESOURCE DEVELOPMENT be pleased to \nstate: \n \n(a)  whether there are such Lok Sabha constituencies in the country particularly in \nUttar Pradesh where Kendriya Vidyalayas (KVs) have not been set up so far;  \n(b)  if so, the names of such Lok  Sabha constituencies in the country, State/UT -\nwise particularly in Uttar Pradesh;  \n(c)  whether the Union Government proposes to set up at least one KV each in all \nthe Lok Sabha constituencies of the country;  \n(d)  if so, the details thereof; and  \n(e)  if not, the reasons therefor? \nANSWER \nMINISTER OF HUMAN RESOURCE DEVELOPMENT \n(SHRI RAMESH POKHRIYAL ‘NISHANK’) \n \n(a) to (e) A statement is laid on the Table of the House. \n \n****** \n \n  \n\nSTATEMENT REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PARTS (a) TO (e) OF \nTHE LOK SABHA STARRED QUESTION NO. 151 RAISED BY SHRI \nASHOK KUMAR RAWAT , HON’BLE MEMBER OF PARLIAMENT TO \nBE ANSWERED ON 2 ND MARCH 2020 REGARDING KENDRIYA \nVIDYALAYAS IN LOK SABHA CONSTITUENCIES.  \n \n(a) to (e)  Kendriya Vidyalayas (KVs) are opened primarily to cater to the \neducational needs of the wards of transferable Central Government \nEmployees including Defence personnel by providing a common \nprogramme of education. Proposals for opening of new KVs are \nconsidered only if sponsored by Ministries or Departments of the \nGovernment of India/State Governments / Union Territories \nAdministrations thereby committing resources for setting up a new KV as \nwell as the availability of necessary sanction of the Governm ent. The \nproposals received from various sponsoring authorities for opening of \nnew KVs also have to compete among other proposals under the \n“Challenge Method”.  \n \nAt present, there are 1227 KVs (excluding three abroad) functioning in \n438 Lok Sabha  Parliamentary Constituencies. State/UT -wise and \nParliamentary Constituency-wise details of KVs are annexed. \n****** \n  \n\nAnnexure \n \nANNEXURE REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PART (a)  to (e) OF THE LOK SABHA STARRED \nQUESTION NO. 151 FOR 2nd  March, 20 20 RAISED BY SHRI ASHOK KUMAR RAWAT, HON’BLE \nMEMBER OF PARLIAMENT TO BE ANSWERED ON 2ND MARCH 2020 REGARDING KENDRIYA \nVIDYALAYAS IN LOK SABHA CONSTITUENCIES. \n \nSTATE/UT-WISE AND PARLIAMENTARY CONSTITUENCY-WISE DETAILS OF 1227 KENDRIYA \nVIDYALAYAS FUNCTIONING ACROSS THE COUNTRY AS ON 25.2.2020. \n \nSI No. Name of State/ UT/ Parliamentary \nConstituency \nName of the Kendriya Vidyalaya \nANDAMAN & NIKOBAR   \n1. ANDAMAN & NIKOBAR PORT BLAIR NO.1 \n2.  PORT BLAIR No.2 \nANDHRA PRADESH    \n3.  ANANTAPUR  ANANTPUR (SK UNIVERSITY) \n4.  GOOTY ANANTPUR     \n5.  GUNTAKAL \n6.  BAPTLA SURYA LANKA (AFS) \n7.  TENALI,  \n8.  ELURU WEST GODAVARI (ELURU) \n9.  GUNTUR GUNTUR \n10.  KADAPA CUDDAPPAH \n11.  KAKINADA KAKINADA \n12.  KURNOOL KURNOOL \n13.  MACHHLIPATNAM MACHHLIPATNAM \n14.  NARASARAOPET SATTENPALI \n15.  IRLAPADU VILLAGE, NADENDLA \n16.  NELLORE KOTHURU, DISTT. NELLORE \n17.  ONGOLE ONGOLE, DISTT. PRAKASSAM \n18.  RAJAMPALLI YERRAGONDAPALEM \n19.  KANDUKUR TOWN \n20.  RAJAHMUNDARY RAJAHMUNDARY (ONGC) \n21.  RAJAMPET RAJAMPET \n22.  SRIKAKULAM SRIKAKULAM \n23.  TIRUPATI TIRUPATI NO.I \n24.  TIRUPATI NO.II \n25.  VENKATAGIRI, DISTT. NELLORE \n26.  VIJAYANAGRAM VIJAYANAGRAM \n27.  VIJAYAWADA VIJAYAWADA NO.I \n28.  VIJAYAWADA NO.II \n29.  VISHAKHAPATNAM BHEEMUNIPATNAM (INS KALINGA) \n30.  VISHAKHAPATNAM, MALKAPURAM \n31.  VISHAKHAPATNAM, NAD \n32.  VISHAKHAPATNAM, NAUSENA BAGH NO.I \n\n33.  VISHAKHAPATNAM, NAUSENA BAGH NO.II \n34.  VISHAKHAPATNAM, SRIVIJAYA NAGAR NO.I \n35.  VISHAKHAPATNAM, SRIVIJAYA NAGAR NO.II \n36.  VISHAKHAPATNAM, STEEL PROJECT \n37.  VISHAKHAPATNAM, WALTAIR \n \nARUNACHAL PRADESH    \n38.  ARUNACHAL EAST KHONSA \n39.  LONGDING \n40.  MIAON \n41.  PASSIGAHT \n42.  ROING \n43.  TEZU \n44.  TUTING, DISTT UPPER SIANG \n45.  NAMSAI \n46.  ARUNACHAL WEST ALONG (11 ASSAM RIFLES) \n47.  DIRANG, WEST KAMENG \n48.  ITANAGAR.NO.I  \n49.  ITANAGAR.NO.II  \n50.  KIMIN  (9 ASSAM RIFLES) \n51.  NIRJULI (NERIST) \n52.  TAWANG \n53.  TENGA VALLEY  \n54.  ZERO, HAPOLI \n55.  DAPORIJO \n ASSAM    \n56.  BARPETA BARPETA \n57.  NEW BONGAIGAON  \n58.  DHUBRI GOALPARA \n59.  PANBARI (BSF) \n60.  DIBRUGARH DIBRUGARH \n61.  DULIAJAN (OIL) \n62.  LEKHA PANI \n63.  MOHANBARI \n64.  NAMRUP (HFC) \n65.  TINSUKIA \n66.  DIPHU BOKAJAN (CCI) \n67.  DIPHU \n68.  HAFLONG (SSB) \n69.  KHATKHATI (CRPF) \n70.  GUWAHATI DIGARU (AFS) \n71.  GUWAHATI (IOC) \n72.  GUWAHATI , AMERIGOG (CRPF) \n73.  GUWAHATI IIT \n\n74.  GUWAHATI, BORJHAR  \n75.  GUWAHATI, KHANAPARA  \n76.  GUWAHATI, MALIGAON (RLY.) \n77.  GUWAHATI, NARANGI  \n78.  JORHAT JORHAT NO. 1 (AFS) \n79.  JORHAT NO.II (ONGC) \n80.  JORHAT NO.III (RRL) \n81.  SIBSAGAR NO.I (ONGC) \n82.  SIBSAGAR NO.II NAZIRA (ONGC) \n83.  KALIABOR GOLAGHAT \n84.  NOWGONG, MISA CANTT  \n85.  KARIMGUNJ CACHAR PANCHGRAM (HPC) \n86.  KARIMGUNJ \n87.  KOKRAJHAR KOK"}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|3|UNSTARRED |152", "t": "Safety and Liberty of the Students", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF HUMAN RESOURCE DEVELOPMENT \nDEPARTMENT OF HIGHER EDUCATION \n \nLOK SABHA \n \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 152 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 03.02.2020 \nSafety and Liberty of the Students \n \n152. SHRI K. NAVASKANI: \n \nWill the Minister of HUMAN RESOURCE DEVELOPMENT be pleased to state: \n \n(a) whether the Government is aware about recent events of violence in various \nuniversities of the country and proposed to obtain a comprehensive report regarding \nthe causes and impact of these events; \n(b) if so, the details thereof and if not, the reasons therefor; \n(c) the measures proposed to be taken by the Government for the safety and liberty \nof the students; and \n(d) if so, the details thereof? \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF HUMAN RESOURCE DEVELOPMENT \n(SHRI RAMESH POKHRIYAL ‘NISHANK’) \n \n \n(a) to (d) There have been reports of some violence in some universities in the \ncountry. Ministry of Human Resource Development was apprised of the factual \nposition by the concerned Central Universities. All the Central Universities are \ncompetent by their statutes to ensure proper safety and unhindered progress of the \nstudents.    \n *****  \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n\n \n \nHkkjr ljdkj \nekuo lalk/ku fodkl ea=ky; \nmPprj f”k{kk foHkkx \n \nyksd lHkk \nvrkjkafdr iz”u la[;k 152 \nmRrj nsus dh rkjh[k 03-02-2020 \n \n \n \nNk=ksa dh lqj{kk vkSj Lora=rk \n \n†152- Jh dsñ uokl[kuh%  \n \nD;k ekuo lalkèku fodkl ea=h ;g crkus dh d`ik djsaxs fd%  \n \n¼d½ D;k ljdkj ns'k ds fofHkUu fo'ofo|ky;ksa esa gky gh esa \ngqbZ fgalk dh ?kVukvksa ls voxr gS vkSj D;k ljdkj dk mDr \n?kVukvksa ds dkj.k vkSj izHkko ds laca/k esa O;kid fjiksVZ izkIr \ndjus dk fopkj fd;k gS(  \n \n¼[k½ ;fn gka] rks rRlacaèkh C;kSjk D;k gS vkSj ;fn ugha] rks \nblds D;k dkj.k gSa( \n \n¼x½ ljdkj }kjk Nk=ksa dh lqj{kk vkSj Lora=rk gsrq D;k mik; \nizLrkfor gSa( vkSj  \n \n¼?k½ ;fn gka] rks rRlacaèkh C;kSjk D;k gS\\ \n \nmRrj \nekuo lalk/ku fodkl ea=h \n¼Jh jes”k iks[kfj;ky ^fu”kad^½ \n \n¼d½ ls ¼?k½% ns”k esa dqN fo”ofo|ky;ksa esa dqN fgalk dh \nfjiksVsaZ vkbZ gSa A ekuo lalk/ku fodkl ea=ky; dks lacaf/kr \ndsUnzh; fo”ofo|ky; }kjk okLrfod fLFkfr ls voxr djk;k x;k Fkk A \nlHkh dsUnzh; fo”ofo|ky; lkafof/kd :i ls Nk=ksa dh leqfpr lqj{kk \nvkSj fuckZ/k izxfr lqfuf”pr djus gsrq l{ke gSa A  \n\n \n***** \n  "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|3|UNSTARRED |153", "t": "Bulk Petroleum Storage", "head": " \n \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF HUMAN RESOURCE DEVELOPMENT \nDEPARTMENT OF HIGHER EDUCATION \n \nLOK SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION No.*153 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 02.03.2020 \n \nAvailability of Books in Hindi Medium \n†*153. SHRI GIRISH CHANDRA: \nSHRI SHYAM SINGH YADAV:  \nWill the Minister of HUMAN RESOURCE DEVELOPMENT be pleased to state: \n(a) the names of all those central universities where the books of all the courses are \navailable both in English and Hindi languages; \n \n(b) whether the Government proposes to make books of  all the courses available to the \nstudents of all the universities in Hindi language also because a lot of students complete \ntheir Intermediate Level studies in Hindi Medium; \n \n(c) if so, the details thereof; and \n \n(d) if not, the reasons therefor? \nANSWER \nMINISTER OF HUMAN RESOURCE DEVELOPMENT \n             (SHRI RAMESH POKHRIYAL ‘NISHANK’) \n(a) to (d): A statement is laid on the Table of the House. \n \n \nSTATEMENT REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PARTS (a) TO (d) OF LOK SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION NO.  153 FOR ANSWER ON 2nd MARCH, 2020 ASKED BY \nSHRI GIRISH CHANDRA AND SHRI SHYAM SINGH YADAV,  HON’BLE MEMBERS \nOF PARLIAMENT REGARDING AVAILABILITY OF BOOKS IN HINDI MEDIUM. \n \n\n(a) to (d):  The Central Universities are autonomous institutions created under the Act of \nParliament which are governed by their own Acts, Statutes, Ordinances , etc. and are \ncompetent to take decision on their academic matters such as  medium of instruction, \nacademic content, courses, syllabi with the approval of their statutory bodies. Ministry of \nHuman Resour ce Development (MHRD) does not interfere with the routine ac ademic/ \nadministration matter of Central Universities.  But in the Conference of Central \nUniversities Vice -Chancellors held on 18.02.2016, Vice -Chancellor of all Central \nUniversities unanimously passed the resolution that in order to ensure that no student is \ndeprived of Higher Education due to language limitation , universities to ensure \ninstruction in  an Indian language as applicable to the state and English for this. \n \n***** \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|3|UNSTARRED |154", "t": "Exam for Board Members", "head": "1 \n \n \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF SKILL DEVELOPMENT AND ENTREPRENEURSHIP \n \nLOK SABHA \n \nSTARRED QUESTION NO. 154 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 02.03.2020 \n \nAGRICULTURE RELATED TRAINING TO YOUTH \n \n*154. SHRI JANARDAN SINGH SIGRIWAL:  \n \n Will the Minister of SKILL DEVELOPMENT AND ENTREPRENEURSHIP be \npleased to state: \n \n(a)  whether the Government has any plan to provide  the agriculture related training to \ncreate interest among the youth in agricultural sec tor keeping in view immense employment \npotential in the said sector and if so, the details thereof;  \n \n(b) Whether the training is being provided to the y outh for getting employment in \nhorticulture, bee-keeping and animal husbandry;  \n \n(c) if so, the details thereof; and  \n \n(d) the other measures taken/proposed to be taken b y the Government for Skill \nDevelopment in the agriculture sector? \n \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER IN MINISTRY OF  \nSKILL DEVELOPMENT AND ENTREPRENEURSHIP  \n(DR. MAHENDRA NATH PANDEY)  \n \n(a) to (d) : A Statement is laid on the Table of the House. \n \n \n  \n\n2 \n \nStatement in reply to Lok Sabha Starred Question No . 154* for reply on 02-03-2020 \nregarding “AGRICULTURE RELATED TRAINING TO YOUTH” b y Hon’ble Member Shri \nJANARDAN SINGH SIGRIWAL  \n \nThe Ministry of Skill Development and Entrepreneurs hip has signed a Memorandum of \nUnderstanding (MOU) with the Ministry of Agricultur e and Farmers Welfare on 20th March \n2018 with a view to creating an efficient and effec tive training, assessment and certification \nprocess for skill development in varied job roles i n agriculture, horticulture, animal \nhusbandry, fisheries etc. The MoU also seeks to enc ourage employment of skilled and \ncertified workforce and recognition of prior learni ng of the workforce already engaged in the \nsector besides encouraging youth to undergo skill t raining in various vocations in agriculture \nfor livelihood opportunities. Agriculture Skill Cou ncil of India (ASCI) under the Ministry has \ndeveloped 173 Qualification Packs for various job r oles for Agriculture. The skill training \ncourses are conducted through National Training Ins titutes, State Level Training Institutes \n(SAMETIs), Krishi Vigyan Kendras and State Agricult ural Universities. The major areas of \nskill trainings include mushroom production, bee-ke eping, micro-irrigation, maintenance and \nrepair of farm equipment, horticulture, organic gro wer, vermi-compost production, animal \nhusbandry, dairying, poultry, fishery etc. \n \nThe Ministry through Pradhan Mantri Kaushal Vikas Y ojana (PMKVY), Jan Shikshan \nSansthan and other non-PMKVY Programmes like Rashtr iya Krishi Vikas Yojana, State Skill \nMissions has been imparting skill training in horti culture, bee-keeping and animal husbandry \netc. As per data provided by the Agriculture Sector  Skill Council (ASCI), the number of \npersons trained in horticulture, bee-keeping and animal husbandry are as follows:-  \n \ni Horticulture 38,371 \nii Bee-keeping 4,044 \niii Animal Husbandry  (Dairy and Poultry) 48,525 \n \nBesides Short Term Trainings (STT), the Government has been facilitating Recognition of \nPrior Learning (RPL) which is providing vocational identity to the agriculture farmers and \nlabourers. RPL with bridge training is a great enab ler for upskilling/ reskilling of farmers and \nalso provides other benefits viz; access to credit,  access to market, reduction in losses, \ndigital skills through apps for accessing advisory and market information, financial literacy \nenabling them to calculate and price their produce and benefit through safe and judicious \nuse of Agrochemicals.  \n \n \n***** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|3|UNSTARRED |155", "t": "Development of Tourism in Tripura", "head": "भारत सरकार \nमानव संसाधन ͪवकास मंğालय \nउÍ चतर ͧश¢ा ͪवभाग \n \nलोक सभा \nतारांͩकत Ĥæन संÉयाः155 \nउ×तर देने कȧ तारȣखः 02.03.2020 \n \n \nराçĚȣय ͧश¢ा नीǓत \n \n†*155. Įी बालूभाऊ उफ[ सुरेश नारायण धानोरकर: \nÈया मानव संसाधन ͪवकास मंğी यह बतानेकȧ कृपा करɅगे ͩकः \n(क) राçĚȣय ͧश¢ा नीǓत (एनईपी) को कब तक काया[िÛवत ͩकए जाने कȧ संभावना है; \n \n(ख) Èया सरकार का एनईपी काया[िÛवत करने के  ͧलए राÏय-वार काय[-योजना तैयार करने का Ĥèताव है; \n \n(ग) यǑद हां, तो त×संबंधी Þयौरा Èया है और यǑद नहȣं, तो इसके Èया कारण है; \n \n(घ) È