������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������\n������ ���������������� �������������� ���������� ���������������������� ������ ������������������ ������������������ �������� ������������������ ��������\n��������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������\n��\n��\n��������\n��������\n������������������������������������������������������ �������� ������ ��������������������\n������������������\n���� ������������������������������������������������������ ������\n���� �������������������������������������������������������������������� ������\n���� ���������������������������������������������������������������������������� ������\n���� �������� ������������������������ ���������������� ������ ��������������������\n������������������\n������\n���� �������� ������������������������ ���������������� ������ ������ ��������\n��������������������������������������\n������\n���� �������� ������������������������ ���������������� ������ �������� ������������������������\n����������������\n��������\n \n������������"}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|3|UNSTARRED |325", "t": "Electronic Charging Stations", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF HEAVY INDUSTRIES & PUBLIC ENTERPRISES \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 325 \nANSWERED ON 04.02.2020 \n \nELECTRONIC CHARGING STATIONS \n \n325. SHRI KANUMURU RAGHU RAMA \n        KRISHANA RAJU: \n \nWill the Minister of HEAVY INDUSTRIES AND PUBLIC ENTERPRISES भारी उद्योग \nएवं ऱोक उद्यम मंत्री be pleased to state: \n \n(a) whether the Government has sanctioned Electronic Charging Stations under Faster Adoption \nand Manufacturing of Hybrid and Electric Vehicles in India (FAME India) Phase-II to the States \nin the country; \n \n(b) if so, the details thereof; and \n \n(c) the details of cities that have been identified in the State of Andhra Pradesh for this purpose? \n \nANSWER \n \nTHE MINISTER OF HEAVY INDUSTRIES & PUBLIC ENTERPRISES \n(SHRI PRAKASH JAVADEKAR) \n \n(a) to (c): Yes, Sir. Department of Heavy Industry has sanctioned 2,636 Electric Vehicles (EVs) \nCharging Stations in 62 cities across 24 States/UTs under FAME India  (Faster Adoption and \nManufacturing of Hybrid & Electric Vehicles in India) scheme phase II . In Andhra Pradesh, 92 \nCharging Stations to Vijayawada, 71 Charging Stations to Vishakhapatnam, 68 Charging \nStations to Tirupati and 35 Charging Stations to Kakinada have been sanctioned under the \nScheme. \n \n***** \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|3|UNSTARRED |326", "t": "Criteria for Construction of Roads under PMGSY", "head": "O.I.H.\n \n \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA\n \nMINISTRY OF AGRICULTURE\n \nAND FARMERS WELFARE\n \nDEPARTMENT OF AGRICULTURE, \nCOOPERATION\n \n& FARMERS WELFARE\n \n \nLOK  SABHA\n \nSTARRED QUESTION NO.\n \n326\n \nTO BE ANSWERED ON THE \n  \n17\nTH\n \nMARCH, 2020\n \n \nAMENDMENT OF PRADHAN MANTRI FASAL BIMA YOJANA\n \n \n*326. \n    \nSHRIMATI JASKAUR MEENA: \n \n              \nSHRI NIHAL CHAND: \n \n \nWill the Minister of AGRICULTURE\n \nAND FARMERS \nWELFARE\n  \nकृ\u0000ष\n \nएवं\n \n\u0000कसान\n \nक\u0000याण\n \nमं\u0000ी\n \n \nbe pleased to state\n:\n \n \n(a) \n \nwhether the Government proposes to amend the Pradhan Mantri Fasal Bima \nYojana; \n \n \n(b) \n \nif so, the details thereof along with the reasons therefor; and \n \n \n(c) \n \nthe time by which the said amendments are likely to be carried out, give details?\n \n \n \n \nANSWER\n \nMINISTER OF AGRICULTURE\n \nAND FARMERS WELFARE\n \nकृ\u0000ष\n \nएवं\n \n\u0000कसान\n \nक\u0000याण\n \nमं\u0000ी\n \n \n \n \n  \n(SHRI NARENDRA  SINGH TOMAR)\n \n \n \n(a) to (\nc\n):\n \nA statement is laid on the Table of the House.\n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n\n \nSTATEMENT REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PARTS (a) TO (\nc\n) OF L\nOK SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION \nNO.\n   \n326\n  \nDUE FOR REPLY ON \n17\nTH\n \n \nMARCH\n, 2020.\n \n \n(a) to (c):     T\nhe Pradhan Mantri Fasal Bima Yojana (PMFBY) and Restructured Weather \nBased Crop Insurance Scheme (RWBCIS\n) are regularly\n \nreviewed especially the challenges \nfaced by the stakeholders in its implementation si\nnce\n \ntheir inception in Kharif 2016 season.  \nAccordingly, \nt\nhis Department had made \ndetailed \nconsultations with all stakeholders viz. State \nGovernments, farmer organization\ns, insurance companies, reinsurance companies, financial \ninstitutions, concerned organizations, and Central Government Departments at various \nforums to identify the challenges and finalise the possible solutions/remedial measures etc.   \nBased on their feed\nback and suggestions changes\n \nhave \nrecently \nbeen incorporated in the \nprovisions/ parameters of \nongoing PMFBY/RWBCIS for its implementation from Kharif 2020.  \nDetails of Change\n \nmade in the  provisions/parameters of the schemes are \ngiven below\n \n:\n \n\n \nTo address th\ne demand of farmers, the scheme has been made voluntary for all \nfarmers. There is no change in farmers’ share of premium.\n \n\n \nThe\n \npremium subsidy sharing pattern between Centre and \nNorth Eastern States \nhas been\n \nchang\ned from 50 : 50 to \n90:10\n.  This has been don\ne to allow more \nStates to notify the scheme and existing States to notify more crops and areas to \nfacilitate greater coverage of farmers under the scheme.  For remaining States, \nsubsidy sharing pattern will continue as 50 : 50. \n \n\n \nInsurance companies will no\nw be \nselected\n \nby the States for 3 years in a go \ninstead of one year thereby increasing their commitment and accountability to the \nfarmers.\n \n\n \nOption has been given to the States to choose the notional value of average yield \nor the Scale of Finance as sum insu\nred in the interest of the farming community.\n \nContd…2/\n-\n \n \n\n \n-\n2\n-\n \n\n \nThe issue of high premium rate for few crops/areas due to adverse selection has \nbeen suitably addressed. The requisite central share of premium subsidy will be \nprovided for areas/crops having gr\noss premium rate upto 25% for irrigated and \nupto 30% for un\n-\nirrigated areas/crops. Besides, alternate risk mitigation measures \nwill be explored for these areas/crops.\n \n\n \nIn view of the demand of many states, option has been given to states to choose \nadditiona\nl risk covers besides shortfall in yield\n-\nbased cover depending upon the \nlocal weather challenges and requirements of the farmers.\n \n\n \nThe states delaying the release of subsidy beyond \nstipulated\n \ntimelines \ncan not \nparticipate in upcoming seasons.\n \n\n \nTwo\n-\nstep process of crop yield estimation using weather and satellite indicators \netc. \nis \nadopted\n,\n \nwhich will help in early assessment of loss\n.\n \n \n\n \nUse of smart sampling technique through satellite data for crop cutting \nexperiments by some states has shown inc\nreased efficiency in implementation. \nThis will now be universalized.\n \n\n \nThe delay by some States in submission of crop yield data will now be suitably \naddressed using technological solutions. \n \n\n \nProvision has been made for earmarked administrative expenses @ 3%\n \nfor \nstrengthening the infrastructure and technology for better delivery of the Scheme.\n \n*****\n \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|3|UNSTARRED |327", "t": "Advanced Forensic DNA Lab", "head": " \n \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF HOME AFFAIRS \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 327 \n \nTO BE ANSWERED ON THE 04TH FEBRUARY, 2020/MAGHA 15, 1941 (SAKA) \n \nADVANCED FORENSIC DNA LAB \n \n327.   SHRI DHANUSH M. KUMAR: \nSHRI GAUTAM SIGAMAANI PON: \nDR. HEENA GAVIT: \n \nWill the Minister of HOME AFFAIRS be pleased to state:  \n \n(a) whether the Government has established a new Advanced Forensic DNA \nAnalysis Lab under Nirbhaya Fund Scheme; \n \n(b) if so, the details thereof along with the fund allocation and its capacity of \nexamining cases per year; \n \n(c) the nature of cases to be handled by this new Advanced Forensic DNA \nAnalysis Lab; \n \n(d) whether the Government has made provisions for all States/UTs to utilize \nthis Lab and if so, the details thereof; \n \n(e) whether the Government has taken steps to set up similar facilities in \nrespective States for quick disposal of forensic cases and if so, the details \nthereof; \n \n(f) whether the Directorate of Forensic Science has set up Central Forensic \nScience Laboratories (CFSL) across the country; and \n \n(g) if so, the details thereof along with research and development activities \nundertaken by the CFSL during each of the last three years? \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF HOME AFFAIRS \n(SHRI G. KISHAN REDDY) \n(a) to (c):  A State-of-the-art DNA Analysis Centre has been set up in Central \nForensic Science Laboratory, Chandigarh under the Nirbhaya Fund scheme with \nan allocation of Rs.99.76 crores. This facility has an annual capacity to examine \n-2/... \n \n\n \n-2- \nLS.US.Q.NO.327 FOR 04.02.2020 \n2000 forensic cases relating to sexual assault, homicide, paternity, human \nidentification and Mitochondrial DNA.  \n (d)  Yes, sir.  Central Forensic Science Laboratories, including the new DNA \nAnalysis Centre, have been mandated to receive cases/exhibits referred by \nCentral Government Agencies, Higher/Lower Courts, Autonomous bodies, Public \nSector Undertakings, Banks and from States/Union Territories.  \n(e)   Ministry of Home Affairs has approved setting-up and upgrading of DNA \nanalysis and cyber forensic units in State Forensic Science Laboratories in 13 \nStates/ Union Territories under the Nirbhaya Fund scheme.  Apart from this, 25 \nStates have been given an allocation of Rs.108.40 crore for strengthening \nForensic Science facilities, including DNA analysis facilities, as part of annual \nState Action plan under the scheme for Modernization of Police Forces in the \nyear 2019-20. \n(f) and (g): The Directorate of Forensic Science Services has six Central \nForensic Science Laboratories (CFSLs) situated at Bhopal (Madhya Pradesh), \nChandigarh, Guwahati (Assam), Hyderabad (Telangana), Pune (Maharashtra) and \nKolkata (West Bengal). Details of Research & Development activities taken in \nthe last three years are as below:  \nYear Research papers \npublished \nResearch & \nDevelopment \nprojects \nNeed based \nresearch on \nemerging fields  \n2016-17 21 2 2 \n2017-18 15 2 1 \n2018-19 34 2 2 \n \n***** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|3|UNSTARRED |328", "t": "Gramin Haats", "head": " \n                                  \n \n \n \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA\n \nMINISTRY OF AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE\n \nDEPARTMENT OF AGRICULTURE, COOPERATION AND FARMERS WELFARE\n \n \nLOK  SABHA\n \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.\n \n328\n \nTO BE ANSWERED ON THE \n4\nTH\n \nFEBRUARY\n,\n \n20\n20\n \n \nGRAMIN HAATS\n \n \n328. COL. RAJYAVARDHAN RATHORE\n:\n \n \nWill the Minister of AGRICULTURE\n \nAND FARMERS WELFARE\n   \nकृ\u0000ष\n \nएवं \u0000कसान क\u0000याण मं\u0000ी\n \nbe pleased to state:\n \n \n(a) \n \nthe number of Gramin Haats modernised under the Gramin Agriculture Market Scheme \ntill date along with the details thereof, State\n-\nwise; \n \n \n(b)\n \n \nthe current status of the Scheme along with the details of\n \nthe outcome of the \nmodernisation process achieved till date;\n \n \n(c) \n \nwhether there is any plan/proposal to modernise the remaining Gramin Haats and if so, \nthe proposed timeline thereof;\n \n \n(d)\n \n \nwhether the Government is aware that there are no Gramin Haats in\n \nRajasthan; \n \n \n(e) the reasons for the lack of Gramin Haats in Rajasthan; and\n \n \n(f)\n \n \nwhether the Government has taken any measure to ensure establishment of Gramin \nHaats in the regions which lack the same and if so, the details thereof?\n \n \nANSWER\n \n \nM\nINISTER \n \nOF\n \nAGRICULTURE\n \nAND FARMERS WELFARE\n \n \nकृ\u0000ष\n \nएवं\n \n\u0000कसान क\u0000याण \nमं\u0000ी\n \n \n   \n(\nSHRI NARENDRA SINGH TOMAR\n)\n \n \n(a) to (c)\n: \n  \nAs per Union Budget Announcement, 2018\n-\n19, the Government has decided to \ndevelop and upgrade existing 22,000 rural haats into Gramin Agricultural \nMarkets (GrAMs). As \na part of modernization, strengthening   of physical infrastructure and facilities in Gramin \nAgriculture Markets (GrAMs) such as platform, drinking water, drainage etc. is carried out under \nMahatma Gandhi National Rural Employment Guara\nntee Scheme (MGNREGS) programme. So \nfar, development of physical infrastructure has been undertaken in\n \n1154 Gramin haats in the \ncountry under MGNREGA programme. State\n-\nwise details of Gramin haats undertaken under \nMGNREGA is at \nAnnexure\n-\nI\n. \n \n \n \nThe Government\n \nhas approved an Agri\n-\nMarket Infrastructure Fund (AMIF) with a corpus \nof Rs. 2000 crore for developing and upgrading agricultural marketing infrastructure in the \nGrAMs and Agriculture Produce Market Committee (APMC) Markets and circulated the Scheme \nGuidel\nines to the States/Union Territories (UTs) for submission of proposal under the Scheme.\n \n \n \nFurther, Department of Agriculture, Cooperation & Farmers Welfare (DAC&FW) has \ncirculated Guidelines on ‘Operation and Management of GrAMs’ to all States/UTs to facil\nitate \nand promote better management in GrAMs.\n \n \n \n(d) to (f)\n:   \n \nDirectorate of Marketing and Inspection (DMI), an attached of DAC&FW has been \nundertaking a survey of rural \nhaats to find out their\n \nlocation, througho\nut, periodicity and \ninfrastructural status \netc.  As per information received from DMI, total number of 152 rural haats \nhave so far been surveyed in State of Rajasthan.\n \n \n\n \nAnnexure\n-\nI\n \nState\n-\nwise \ndetails of Gramin haats undertaken under MGNREGA \n \n(As on 03.01.2020)\n \nS. No. \n \nState\n \nOngoing work \nsince inception \n \nCompleted work \nsince inception\n \n1.\n \nAndhra Pradesh \n \n111\n \n14\n \n2.\n \nArunachal Pradesh \n \n1\n \n3\n