e \nprovided central assistance for acquisition of advanced weaponry such as INSAS \nRifles & AK series rifles; all types of intelligence equipments including \nUnmanned Aerial Vehicles (UAVs), Night Vision Devices (NVDs), CCTV \nSurveillance system & body worn camera systems; modern communication \nequipment and state-of-the-art equipment for security / training / forensics / cyber \ncrime / traffic policing. Further, ‘construction’ and ‘purchase of operational \nvehicles’ is permitted in the insurgency affected north-eastern States and Left \nWing Extremism (LWE) affected districts. The Crime and Criminal tracking \nNetwork & System (CCTNS) project has been implemented with the aim of \ninterlinking Police Stations all over the country with the objective of tracking \ncrime and crime related data across the country in common networked eco-\nsystem.  \n(c) & (d) Ministry of Home Affairs is implementing Emergency Response Support \nSystem (ERSS) project through States/ Union Territories. ERSS seeks to enable a \npan-India, single, internationally recognized number i.e. 112 based emergency \nresponse mechanism for various emergencies, including police, medical and fire, \nwith computer-aided dispatch of field resources. 112 service is accessible \nthrough call, SMS, e-mail, panic button and a 112 India mobile app. ERSS has \nbeen operationalized by 28 States/UTs in the country.  \n***** \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|3|UNSTARRED |333", "t": "E-PoS Machines", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF HEAVY INDUSTRIES & PUBLIC ENTERPRISES \nDEPARTMENT OF HEAVY INDUSTRY \nLOK SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION NO. 333 \nANSWERED ON 17.03.2020 \n \nCHARGING STATIONS FOR EVS \n \n*333. SHRI GIRISH BHALCHANDRA BAPAT: \n          ADV. ADOOR PRAKASH: \n \nWill the Minister of HEAVY INDUSTRIES AND PUBLIC ENTERPRISES  भारी \nउद्योग एवं ऱोक उद्यम मंत्री be pleased to state: \n \n(a) whether the Government proposes to set up  charging stations for electric vehicles in the \ncountry under the Faster Adoption and Manufacturing of Hybrid  and Electric Vehicles in \nIndia (FAME-India) Scheme, Phase-II; \n \n(b) whether the Government has started setting up  of charging stations for electric vehicles in \nthe country; \n \n(c) if so, the funds allocated for this purpose and the expenditure incurred so far; \n \n(d) whether electric charging stations are being set  up in cities in Maharashtra and other \nStates; and \n \n(e) if so, the details thereof? \n \n \nANSWER \nTHE MINISTER OF HEAVY INDUSTRIES & PUBLIC ENTERPRISES \n(SHRI PRAKASH JAVADEKAR) \n \n(a) to (e): A statement is laid on the Table of the House. \n \n******** \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n\nSTATEMENT \n \nStatement referred to in reply to parts (a) to (e) of Lok -Sabha Starred Question No. 333 \nfor 17.03.2020 asked by Shri Girish Bhalchandra  Bapat and Adv. Adoor Prakash \nregarding “Charging Stations for EVs” \n \n(a) to (e): Yes, Sir. The FAME -India Scheme Phase -II envisages support for setting up of \nadequate public charging infrastructure to instill confidence among EV users, through active \nparticipation and involveme nt of various stakeholders including Government agencies, \nindustries and Public Sector Enterprises (PSEs). A budget provision of Rs. 1000 Crore for a \nperiod of 3 years [2019 -20 to 2021 -22] has been earmarked for establishment of charging \ninfrastructure under this Phase of FAME Scheme. Department of Heavy Industry had invited \nthe Expression of Interest (EoI) from million -plus cities, smart cities, State/UT capitals and \ncities from special category states for submission of proposal for availing incentives und er \nFAME India Scheme Phase II for deployment of EV charging infrastructure within Cities. In \nresponse to the proposals received under this EoI, Department of Heavy Industry has \nsanctioned 2,636 Electric Vehicles (EVs) Charging Stations amounting to Rs 500 Crore \n(Approx.) in 62 cities across 24 States/UTs including Maharashtra under FAME India (Faster \nAdoption and Manufacturing of Hybrid & Electric Vehicles in India) scheme phase II as per \ndetails annexed at ANNEXURE-I. \n \n***** \n \n\nAnnexure-I \nList of cities and numbers of Electric Vehicle Charging Stations considered for Sanction \nSr. \nNo. \n          Name of State    Name of City State Total \n1 Andhra Pradesh  Vijaywada  \n \n266 \n2 Vishakhapatnam \n3 Tirupati \n4 Kakinada \n5 Assam Jorhat 20 \n6 Guwahati \n7 Bihar Patna 37 \n8 Chandigarh Chandigarh 70 \n9 Chattishgarh Raipur 25 \n10 Delhi Delhi 72 \n11 Gujarat Ahmedabad  \n228 12 Vadodara \n13 Gandhi Nagar \n14 Haryana  Gurugram 50 \n15 Karnal \n16 Sonipat \n17 Himanchal Pradesh Dharamshala 10 \n18 Karnataka Bengaluru  \n172 19 Gulbarga \n20 Mangalore \n21 Kerala Kochi  \n131 22 Thrissur \n23 Kannur \n24 Kozhikode \n25 Maharashtra Mumbai  \n317 26 Nashik \n27 Nagpur \n28 Thane \n29 Madhya Pradesh Bhopal  \n159 30 Indore \n31 Jabalpur \n32 Meghalaya Shillong 40 \n33 Odisha Bhubaneshwar 18 \n34 Puducherry Pondicherry 10 \n35 Rajasthan Jaipur  \n \n205 \n36 Jodhpur \n37 Kota \n38 Udaipur \n39 Ajmer \n40 Sikkim Gangtok 29 \n41 Srinagar Srinagar 25 \n42 Tamil Nadu Chennai  \n \n \n256 \n43 Coimbatore \n44 Madurai \n45 Vellore \n46 Salem \n47 Thanjavur \n48 Erode \n \nContd…..2/- \n \n\n-2- \n \n \n49 Telangana Hyderabad  \n138 50 Warangal \n51 Karimnagar \n52 Uttar Pradesh Lucknow  \n \n \n \n207 \n53 Varanasi \n54 Allahabad \n55 Kanpur \n56 Noida \n57 Aligarh \n58 Saharanpur \n59 Bareilly \n60 Jhansi \n61 Uttarakhand Dehradun 10 \n62 West Bengal Kolkata  141 \nTotal 2636 \n*******  \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|3|UNSTARRED |334", "t": "Modern Equipment for Para-military Forces", "head": "O.I.H.\n \n \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA\n \nMINISTRY OF AGRICULTURE\n \nAND FARMERS WELFARE\n \nDEPARTMENT OF AGRICULTURE, \nCOOPERATION\n \n& FARMERS WELFARE\n \n \nLOK  SABHA\n \nSTARRED QUESTION NO.\n \n334\n \nTO BE ANSWERED ON THE \n  \n17\nTH\n \nMARCH, 2020\n \n \nISSUANCE OF SOIL HEALTH CARD\n \n \n*334. \n   \nDR. CHANDRA SEN JADON: \n \n \nWill the Minister of AGRICULTURE\n \nAND FARMERS \nWELFARE\n  \nकृ\u0000ष\n \nएवं\n \n\u0000कसान\n \nक\u0000याण\n \nमं\u0000ी\n \n \nbe pleased to state\n:\n \n \n(a) \n \nwhether the Government has launched a comprehensive scheme to provide soil \nhealth card to all the farmers across the country; \n \n \n(b) \n \nif so, the norms laid down for issuing soil health card to farmers along with the policy \nguidelines issued to sustain the f\nertility of soil; \n \n \n(c) \n \nthe details of the funds allocated for this purpose during the current year, State\n-\nwise; \n \n \n(d) \n \nwhether the Government proposes to set up soil testing and research laboratories \nand mobile soil testing laboratories at various places\n \nin the country; and \n \n \n(e) \n \nif so, whether such laboratories are also proposed to be set up in Faizabad district of \nUttar Pradesh?\n \n \n \nANSWER\n \nMINISTER OF AGRICULTURE\n \nAND FARMERS WELFARE\n \nकृ\u0000ष\n \nएवं\n \n\u0000कसान\n \nक\u0000याण\n \nमं\u0000ी\n \n \n \n \n  \n(SHRI NARENDRA  SINGH TOMAR)\n \n \n \n(a) to \n(\ne\n):\n \nA statement is laid on the Table of the House.\n \n \n \n \n \n\nSTATEMENT REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PARTS (a) TO (\ne\n) OF L\nOK SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION NO.\n \n334\n  \nDUE FOR REPLY ON \n17\nTH\n \n \nMARCH\n, 2020.\n \n \n(a)  & (b)\n:    \n  \nGovernment has \nbeen implementing \nSoil Health Card (SHC) \nScheme\n \nsince \n2015 \nto\n \nprovide \nsoil health cards to all farmers across the country once in a cycle of 2 years. \nSoil Health Card provides information to farmers on nutrient status of their soil \nbased on 12 \nparameters namely \nprimary nutrients (N,P & K); second\nary nutrient (S); micronutrients (B, \nZn, Mn, Fe, & Cu); and others (pH, EC & OC) \nalong with recommendations on appropriate \ndosage of nutrients to be applied for improving soil health and its fertility\n \nfor major crops of \nthe area\n.\n \nIn cycle\n-\n1(2015\n-\n17), 10.74\n \ncrore and in cycle\n-\n2 (2017\n-\n19), 11.75 crore SHCs were \ndistributed to the farmers. D\nuring 2019\n-\n20, a pilot project ‘Development of Model Villages’ \nhas been \ninitiated\n \nin 6954 villages( one village/ revenue block)  \nwhere\n \nindividual farm holding \nbased sample \ncollection \nwith farmer’s participation \nis ensured along with awareness \ncampaign and demonstration of application of fertilizers as per prescription of SHC. So far \n14.32 lakh soil health cards have been issued in the Model Villages along with \ndemonstrations\n. \n \n \n(c):     State\n-\n \nwise funds allocated for SHC scheme during the current year (2019\n-\n20) is given \nat\n \n \nAnnexure\n.\n \n \n(d):     Static and mobile Soil Testing Laboratories (STLs) are sanctioned as per proposals / \nrequirement of State Government under Soil \nHealth Management component of National \nMission for Sustainable Agriculture (NMSA). During 2014\n-\n15 to 2019\n-\n20, 10845 STLs have \nbeen sanctioned to States including 429 static STLs, 102 mobile STLs, 8752 mini STLs and \n1562 village level STLs. Indian Council \nof Agricultural Research (ICAR) has been sanctioned \n1076 Mini STLs for KVKs for creating awareness about soil health among farmers through \ntesting of soil samples.\n \n \n \n(e):  \n \n  \nAs per information from the State Government of Uttar Pradesh, the state already \nhas 243 static soil testing labs, having STLs in each district including 3 in Ayodhya, the \nerstwhile Faizabad district.\n \n\nAnnexure \n \n \nState\n-\nwise Allocation for Soil Health Card (SHC) scheme during current year \n(2019\n-\n20)\n \n \nSl. No\n.\n \nState\n \nAmount (Rs \n–\n \nlakh)\n \n1\n \nAndhra Pradesh\n \n1197.41\n \n2\n \nArunachal Pradesh\n \n159.07\n \n3\n \nAssam\n \n610.81\n \n4\n \nBihar\n \n1534.95\n \n5\n \nChhattisgarh\n \n239.50\n \n6\n \nGoa\n \n19.74\n \n7\n \nGujarat\n \n432.78\n \n8\n \nHaryana\n \n228.06\n \n9\n \nHimachal Pradesh\n \n172.76\n \n10\n \nJammu