uding OBCs for reservation in \nadmission; \n(d) whether the Government will consider extending  reservation for OBCs in Sainik  \nSchools also; and \n(e) if so, the details thereof and if not, the reasons therefor? \n \n \nA    N    S    W    E    R \n \nMINISTER OF STATE     (SHRI SHRIPAD NAIK) \nIN THE MINISTRY OF DEFENCE    \nरक्षा राज्य मंत्री     (श्री श्रीपाद नाईक)    \n(a) At present, there are 31 Sainik Schools functioning in the country. State wise \ndetails of Sainik Schools in the country is placed at Annexure. \n \n(b)  to (e):  Admission to Sainik School is strictly merit based through a competitive \nexam and medical fitness. As per Sainik School Society Rules and Regulations 1997, \nseats for admission in Sainik Schools are reserved for candidates belonging to SC, ST \nand Defence categories. It has been decided on 10 th January, 2020 to have 27% \nreservation for OBCs in admissions to align with the formulations of the M /o Human \nResources Development on the matter of admissions. \n***** \n\nANNEXURE IN RESPECT OF PART (a) OF LOK SABHA UNSTARRED \nQUESTION NO. 531  FOR ANSWER ON 05.02.2020 REGARDING \n“RESERVATION IN SAINIK SCHOOL” \nLIST OF SAINIK SCHOOLS (STATE WISE) IN THE COUNTRY  \nS. No. Name of School State \n1. Sainik School Korukonda Andhra Pradesh \n2. Sainik School Kalikiri \n3. Sainik School East Siang Arunachal Pradesh \n4. Sainik School Goalpara Assam \n5. Sainik School Nalanda Bihar 6. Sainik School Gopalganj \n7. Sainik School Ambikapur Chhattisgarh \n8. Sainik School Balachadi Gujarat \n9. Sainik School Kunjpura Haryana 10. Sainik School Rewari \n11. Sainik School SujanpurTira Himachal Pradesh \n12. Sainik School Nagrota Jammu & Kashmir \n13. Sainik School Tilaiya Jharkhand \n14. Sainik School Bijapur Karnataka 15. Sainik School Kodagu \n16. Sainik School Kazhakootam Kerala \n17. Sainik School Rewa Madhya Pradesh \n18. Sainik School Satara Maharashtra 19. Sainik School Chandrapur \n20. Sainik School Imphal Manipur \n21. Sainik School Chhingchhip Mizoram \n22. Sainik School Punglwa Nagaland \n23. Sainik School Bhubaneswar Odisha \n24. Sainik School Kapurthala Punjab \n \n…2/- \n \n \n\n: 2 : \n \n25. Sainik School Chittorgarh Rajasthan 26. Sainik School Jhunjhunu \n27. Sainik School Amaravathi Nagar Tamil Nadu \n28. Sainik School Ghorakhal Uttarakhand \n29. Sainik School Mainpuri Uttar Pradesh 30. Sainik School Jhansi \n31. Sainik School Purulia West Bengal \n \n***** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|3|UNSTARRED |532", "t": "ROBs in Karnataka", "head": " \n \n \n \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF RAILWAYS \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.532 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 05.02.2020 \n \nROBs IN KARNATAKA \n \n532.    SHRI  D.K. SURESH: \n                                             \n           Will the Minister of RAILWAYS be pleased to state : \n \n(a) whether the Government has received any representations seeking \nconstruction of Road Over Bridges (ROBs) in lieu  of Level Crossings in \nthe State of Karnataka and if so, the details thereof along with the \nnumber of requests considered in the State;  \n \n(b) the number of ROBs constructed in lieu of Level Crossings during the \nlast three years and till date; \n \n(c) the details along with the works of ROBs completed, under \nconstruction and those proposals pending; \n \n(d) whether the Government has set an y timeframe to complete the \nconstruction of such ROBs; and \n \n(e) if so, the details thereof? \n \n  \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF RAILWAYS AND COMMERCE & INDUSTRY \n \n(SHRI PIYUSH GOYAL) \n \n(a) to (e): A Statement is laid on the Table of the House.  \n***** \n \n \n \n  \n\n \nSTATEMENT REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PARTS (a) TO ( e) OF \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.  532 BY SHRI D.K. SURESH  TO BE \nANSWERED IN LOK SABHA ON 05.02.2020 REGARDING ROBs IN \nKARNATAKA \n \n(a): Railway have received proposals for construction of Road Over \nBridges (ROBs) in lieu of level crossings on cost sharing basis in the State \nof Karnataka.  Accordingly, the details of sanctioned ROBs in Karnataka \nduring the last three years are as under:  \n2016-17 2017-18 2018-19 Total \nROB ROB ROB ROB \n7 4 3 14 \n \n(b) and (c): The number of ROBs cons tructed on cost sharing basis in the \nState of Karnataka during the last three years is as under: \n2016-17 2017-18 2018-19 Total \nROB ROB ROB ROB \n1 5 1 7 \n \nAs per Pink Book, 2019 -20, 42 ROBs have been sanctioned on cost \nsharing basis in Karnataka.  Out of t hese, 6 ROBs have been fully \ncompleted, railway portion of 7 ROBs has been completed and 29 are in \nvarious stages of planning, estimation and execution.  No such valid \nproposals are pending with Railway.  \n \n(d) and (e): Normally, Railway undertakes construction of ROBs in Railway \nportion whereas approaches are being constructed by State Government.  \nGenerally, there is no problem of construction of ROBs in Railway portion.  \nHowever, it is difficult to construct ROBs on approaches because it \ndepends upon many  factors such as availability of land, removal of \nencroachments, fixing the alignment of approaches, allocation of \nrequisite funds etc.  Therefore, it is not possible to fix the time schedule \nfor construction of ROB projects. \n***** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|3|UNSTARRED |533", "t": "Chabahar Port", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA\nMINISTRY OF EXTERNAL AFFAIRS\nLOK SABHA\nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.533\nTO BE ANSWERED ON 05.02.2020\nCHABAHAR PORT\n533.  SHRI RITESH PANDEY:\n         SHRI JAGDAMBIKA PAL:\nWill the Minister of EXTERNAL AFFAIRS be pleased to state\n(a)the details of progress made with respect to Chabahar Port in Iran;\n(b)the proposed deadline for the completion of the same;\n(c)whether  the  recent  American  sanctions  on  Iran  are  likely  to  affect  the\ncompletion of the said project; and\n(d)if so, the details thereof and if not, the reasons therefor?\nANSWER\nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF EXTERNAL AFFAIRS\n[SHRI V. MURALEEDHARAN]\n(a) to (d) India is participating in the development of the first phase of Shahid\nBehesti Port in Chabahar in cooperation with the Government of Islamic Republic\nof Iran.  The Indian company, India Ports Global Limited, has taken over port\noperations in December 2018 and has successfully handled over 5 lakh tons of\ncargo.  This  includes  exports  from  Afghanistan  through  Chabahar  Port  which\nbegan in February 2019.\n The U.S. has shown understanding of the importance of the Chabahar Port\noperations for continued humanitarian supplies to Afghanistan and to provide\nAfghanistan with economic alternatives.\n***"}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|3|UNSTARRED |534", "t": "Psychological Counselling for Soldiers", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF DEFENCE \nDEPARTMENT OF MILITARY AFFAIRS  \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.534 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 5th February, 2020 \n \nPSYCHOLOGICAL COUNSELLING FOR SOLDIERS \n534. SHRI SUMEDHANAND SARASWATI: \nWill the Minister of DEFENCE j{kk ea=h  \nbe pleased to state: \n (a) whether the Government is aware that mental stress  is the biggest challenge for \nsoldiers in discharging their duties; \n(b) if so, whether the Government has taken measures  to reduce stress and other \nproblems among the soldiers; \n(c) if so, the details thereof; and \n(d) the details of facilities being made available to the  soldiers for psychological \ncounselling? \n \n \nA    N    S    W    E    R \n \nMINISTER OF STATE     (SHRI SHRIPAD NAIK) \nIN THE MINISTRY OF DEFENCE    \nरक्षा राज्य मंत्री     (श्री श्रीपाद नाईक)    \n \n(a) to ( d):  Yes, Sir. Various measures taken by the Government to reduce stress and \nother problems among the soldiers, inter alia, include: \n1. Mental Health Programme for the Armed Forces has been prepared in  \nSeptember, 2008 by Director General of Armed Force Medical Services \n(DGAFMS). \n2. An elaborate Mental Health Programme has been formulated and is in vogue \nsince 2009. \n \n \n...2/- \n\n: 2 : \n \n3. The issue of stress and strain is being addressed by the Commanders in a  \ncomprehensive manner at various levels.  Sessions for stress management are \norganized by Commanders and Psychiatrists at all major stations. \n4. Specific measures include sensitization of Commanders on the subject \naddressing grievances through interactions , implementation of ‘buddy’ system, \nwelfare measures like provision of adequate married accommodation, good \nschooling, recreation facilities etc. \n5.   Personnel at high risk of combat stress (vulnerable) are identified and \ncounseled by unit Commanding Offi cers, Regimental Medical Officers and \nJunior Leaders.  Any stress marker/warning signs of stress are looked for and \nmotivational talk is rendered to all. \n6. Religious Teachers (RT) JCOs, besides preaching religion, are also \nbeing trained to augment the availability of psychological counsellors in units. \n \n \n***** \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|3|UNSTARRED |535", "t": "MII in Telecom Sector", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF COMMERCE & INDUSTRY \nDEPARTMENT FOR PROMOTION OF INDUSTRY AND INTERNAL TRADE \n \nLOK SABHA \n \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 535. \nTO BE ANSWERED ON WEDNESDAY, THE 05TH FEBRUARY, 2020. \n \nMII IN TELECOM SECTOR \n \n535.  SHRI KANUMURU RAGHU RAMA KRISHANA RAJU: \n \nWill the Minister of COMMERCE AND INDUSTRY  be pleased to state: \n                   वाणिज्य एवं उद्योग मंत्री \n(a)  whether the representatives of the telecom industry  have asked the \nGovernment for facilitation of funding at  low interest rates, removal of Goods \nand Services Tax on  license fee, low spectrum usage charges, refund of  \naccumulated input tax credit for promotion of Make -In India (MII) programme \nin the telecom sector; \n \n(b)  if so, the details thereof and the response of the Government thereon; and \n \n(c)  whether the Government has any plan for extending  the same benefits to \nother industries also and if so, the details thereof? \n \nANSWER \nवाणिज  ् य एवं उद्योग मंत्री (श्री पीयूष गोयल)  \nTHE MINISTER OF COMMERCE & INDUSTRY \n(SHRI  PIYUSH GOYAL) \n \n(a) & (b):   Telecom industries had requested for adjustment of Input Tax Credit \nagainst future levies of GST payable by TSPs, lowering of GST on \ntelecom equipment, etc. In response, Department of \nTelecommunications has recommended accordingly to Ministry of \nFinance.  \n \n(c):  No such data is maintained centrally. \n \n \n****** \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|3|UNSTARRED |536", "t": "Foreign Investment in West Bengal", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF COMMERCE & INDUSTRY \nDEPARTMENT FOR PROMOTION OF INDUSTRY AND INTERNAL TRADE \n \nLOK SABHA \n \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 536. \nTO BE ANSWERED ON WEDNESDAY, THE 05TH FEBRUARY, 2020. \n \nFOREIGN INVESTMENT IN WEST BENGAL \n \n536. DR. SUBHAS SARKAR: \n \nWill the Minister of COMMERCE AND INDUSTRY  वाणिज्य एवं उद्योग मंत्री  be \npleased to state the total number and list of foreign  companies, along with the \namount of respective investments, that have invested in West Bengal from  Financial \nyear 2011-12 to till date? \n \nANSWER \nवाणिज  ् य एवं उद्योग मंत्री (श्री पीयूष गोयल)  \nTHE MINISTER OF COMMERCE & INDUSTRY \n(SHRI  PIYUSH GOYAL) \n \nThe State -wise classification of foreign investment in India is available only for \nForeign Direct Investment (FDI). FDI Equity inflow in Bengal is provided in the table \nbelow. The State -wise classification of Foreign Portfolio Investments (FPI) is not \ncentrally maintained.  \n \n   \nS. \nNo. \nFinancial Year FDI Inflow (Amount in \nRs Crore) \n1. 2011-12 1,828.58 \n2 2012-13 2,319.59 \n3 2013-14 2,620.91 \n4 2014-15 2,139.08 \n5 2015-16 2,396.78 \n6 2016-17 301.13 \n7 2017-18 1,219.74 \n8 2018-19 6,788.62 \n9 2019-20 ( up to Sept., 2019) 2,652.80 \nSource: Reserve Bank of India, Mumbai. \n \n******** \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|3|UNSTARRED |537", "t": "Setting Up of Redgram Board", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA MINISTRY OF COMMERCE & INDUSTRY (DEPARTMENT OF COMMERCE) LOK SABHA UNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 537 TO BE ANSWERED ON 05 th FEBRUARY, 2020 TO BE ANSWERED ON 05 th FEBRUARY, 2020 \nSETTING UP OF REDGRAM BOARD 537.DR. G. RANJITH REDDY Will the Minister of COMMERCE & INDUSTRY (वािण�य एवं उ�ोग मं�ी ) be pleased to state: (a) whether there have been demands from various sections, including Redgram farmers, for (a) whether there have been demands from various sections, including Redgram farmers, for setting up Redgram Board in Tandur in Ranga Reddy district of Telangana; (b) if so, the details of the efforts being made by the Government to set up Redgram Board in Tandur; (c) whether any consultations in this regard have been held; and (d) if so, the outcome of the same? \nANSWER okf.kT; ,oa m|ksx ea=h ( Jh ih;w’k xks;y )THE MINISTER OF  COMMERCE AND INDUSTRY (SHRI PIYUSH GOYAL) (a): No Sir.  No demand from any section, including Redgram farmers, has been received in the Department of Commerce, for setting up Redgram Board in Tandur in Ranga Reddy district of Telengana. \n           (b) to (d):  Does not arise.            (b) to (d):  Does not arise. . **** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|3|UNSTARRED |538", "t": "Training to Afghan Diplomats", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA\nMINISTRY OF EXTERNAL AFFAIRS\nLOK SABHA\nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.538\nTO BE ANSWERED ON 05.02.2020\nTRAINING TO AFGHAN DIPLOMATS\n538. SHRI A.K.P . CHINRAJ:\nWill the Minister of EXTERNAL AFFAIRS be pleased to state:\n(a) whether India and China have decided to give training  jointly at Foreign\nService  Institute  (FSI)  at  New  Delhi  to  the  Afghan  diplomats  for  the\ndevelopment of war-torn Afghanistan;\n(b) if so, the details thereof;\n(c) the nature and tenure of training to these diplomats;\n(d) whether FSI has given any similar kind of training to any other country's\ndiplomats; and\n(e) if so, the details thereof?\nANSWER\nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF EXTERNAL AFFAIRS\n[SHRI V. MURALEEDHARAN]\n(a) to (c) The 1 st Joint India-China Capacity Building Programme for Afghan\nDiplomats  was  held  at  the  Foreign  Service  Institute  (FSI)  of  the  Ministry  of\nExternal Affairs of India from 15 to 26 October 2018 as part of an understanding\nreached between Prime Minister of India and President of China at the Wuhan\nInformal Summit. 10 Afghan diplomats participated in the training programme in\n2018.\nThe  2 nd Joint  India-China  Capacity  Building  Programme  for  Afghan\nDiplomats was held at FSI from 11 to 23 November 2019. 10 young diplomats\nfrom Afghanistan participated in the Course.\nThe  intensive  two-week  Course  included  training  modules  on  various\ntopics  covering  India’s  foreign  policy,  key  international  issues,  multilateral\ndiplomacy and diplomatic skills. The participants also got an exposure to modern\nIndia’s economic and technological developments and undertook study tours to\nexperience the rich cultural heritage of India.\n(d) & (e) The  Joint  India-China  Capacity  Building  Programme  for  Afghan\nDiplomats is the only trilateral training programme conducted by FSI. However,\ncourses for foreign diplomats are regularly conducted by FSI. The list of such\ncourses conducted in 2019 is in the Annexure.\n1\n\nANNEXURE TO LOK SABHA QUESTION NO.538\nT raining of Foreign Diplomats Conducted by FSI in 2019\n                                                                                        \nS.No\nTraining Course\nDates Number of\nParticipating\nDiplomats\n1. 1st Annual Disarmament & International \nSecurity Affairs Fellowship Programme\n14 Jan-1 Feb 26\n2. 2nd Special Course for Syrian Diplomats 4-16 Feb 41\n3. 2nd Familiarisation Programme for Resident \nHoMs\n18-22 Feb 31\n4. 2nd Special Course for Sudanese Diplomats 25 Feb-9 Mar 46\n5. 67th Professional Course for Foreign Diplomats 13 Mar-12 Apr 48\n6. 1st Special Course for Moroccan Diplomats 15-27 Apr 14\n7. 1st Special Course for Senegalese Diplomats 15-27 Apr 10\n8. 1st Special Course for Angolan Diplomats 6-18 May 6\n9. 1st Special Course for Botswana Diplomats 6-18 May 18\n10. 1st Special Course for Liberian Diplomats 6-18 May 20\n11. 1st Special Course for Sao T ome & Principe \nDiplomats\n6-18 May 4\n12. 1st Special Course for Equatorial Guinea \nDiplomats\n6-18 May 4\n13. 1st Special Course for Egyptian Diplomats 10-22 Jun 13\n14. 1st Special Course for Central Asian Diplomats 24 Jun-6 Jul 29\n15. 2nd Special Course for Maldivian Diplomats 24 Jun-6 Jul 8\n16. 1st Special Course for League of Arab States 22-28 Jul 20\n17. 2nd Special T raining Prog. for CARICOM \nDiplomats \n12-23 Aug 23\n18. 3rd Familiarisation Programme for Resident \nHoMs\n19-23 Aug 30\n19. 3rd Special Course for Iraqi Diplomats 26 Aug-7 Sept 30\n20. 1st Special Course for Palestinian Diplomats 2-14 Sept 15\n21. 1st Special Course for Libyan Diplomats 6-21 Sept 15\n22. 68th Professional Course for Foreign Diplomats 16 Sep-11 Oct 55\n23. 5th Special Course for ASEM Diplomats 14-21 Oct 20\n24. 13th Special Course for ASEAN Diplomats 14-26 Oct 15\n25. 1st Special Course for Mongolian Diplomats 28 Oct-9 Nov 24\n26. 3rd Special Course for Maldivian Diplomats 28 Oct-9 Nov 6\n27. 1st Special Course for Dominican Republic \nDiplomats\n28 Oct-9 Nov 8\n28. 2nd India-China Joint Capacity Building \nProgramme for Afghan Diplomats\n11-23 Nov 10\n29. 1st Special Course for Beninese Diplomats 25 Nov – 7 Dec 7\n30. 1st Special Course for Cameroonian Diplomats 25 Nov – 7 Dec 10\n31. 1st Special Course for Democratic Republic of \nCongo Diplomats 25 Nov – 7 Dec 11\n32. 1st Special Course for Gabonese Diplomats 25 Nov – 7 Dec 13\n2\n\nS.No\nTraining Course\nDates Number of\nParticipating\nDiplomats\n33. 1st Special Course for Cote d’Ivore Diplomats 25 Nov – 7 Dec 10\n34. 1st Special Course for Kenyan Diplomats 25 Nov – 7 Dec 10\n35. 1st Special Course for Namibian & Malawian \nDiplomats 1-13 Dec 22\n36. 4th Special Course for Bangladeshi Diplomats 9-22 Dec 22\n***\n3"}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|3|UNSTARRED |539", "t": "Linking of Aadhaar Number with PF Number", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF COAL \n  \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.539 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 05.02.2020 \n  \nLinking of Aadhaar Number with PF Number \n  \n539.  SHRI DEVUSINH JESINGHBHAI CHAUHAN: \n  \n         Will the Minister of COAL be pleased to state: \n  \n(a) whether workers engage through contractors in coal mines suffer regarding their \nProvident Fund Accounts; \n  \n(b) if so, the details thereof;  \n  \n(c) whether Government is considering a proposal to link Aadhaar number of coal mines \nworkers to their Provident Fund to protect them from exploitation; \n  \n(d) if so, the details thereof along with the time by which the proposal will be implemented \nand workers will be benefited; and \n  \n(e) the details of the measures being taken by the Government for the welfare of coal  mine \nworkers in the country? \n  \nA N S W E R \n  \nMINISTER OF PARLIAMENTARY AFFAIRS, COAL AND MINES  \n(SHRI PRALHAD JOSHI) \n  \n(a) & (b): No.  \n \n  \n(c) & (d): No such proposal is under consideration of the Government.  \n \n  \n(e): The focus of welfare activities of Coal India Limited (CIL) is welfare of its employees and their \nfamilies. The subsidiaries are paying great attention to the welfare of their workers. Every effort is \nbeing made to improve the living conditions of coal miners. In order to create a sense of belonging \nand involvement in work, top priority is given by the management in  the following areas: \nHOUSING FACILITIES \nTo provide shelter is the main thrust area of the organization. CIL has achieved the  goal  of “Shelter \nfor All” for workers with landmark of  100%  housing  satisfaction  for  fulfilling  the  basic needs of \nthe workers. \nWATER SUPPLY \nOne  of the   major  welfare  activities  of the  company  is to  provide  clean drinking  water  to    the \nemployees and their families. In  this  direction  many  water  supply  schemes  have  been  taken up. \n\nSupply of Water through natural resource like river, tank etc are supplemented by underground mine \nwater after proper treatment. \nEDUCATION FACILITIES \nThe primary responsibility of providing educational facilities lies with the State Government. \nHowever, the subsidiary companies of CIL have been providing financial assistance and \ninfrastructure facilities to certain schools like DAV, Kendriya Vidyalaya, Delhi Public School etc \nand other educational institutions run by the State Government to provide quality education to the \nemployees’ children. In addition, financial assistance and infrastructure facilities are also provided to \ncertain privately managed s chools and other educational institutions by the subsidiary coal \ncompanies functioning in and around coalfield areas.   CIL is also providing various types of \nscholarship, cash award and financial assistance to the children of employees. \n  \nMEDICAL FACILITIES \nCIL and its subsidiaries are extending medical facilities to the employees and their families   through \nvarious medical establishments from the dispensary level to the central and apex hospitals in \ndifferent parts of the coalfields.   Special  emphasis  is  also  given on occupational \nhealth,  HIV/AIDS  \n \nawareness programme. \n  \nSTATUTORY WELFARE FACILITIES \nIn accordance with the provision of the Mines Act, 1952 and rules & regulations framed \nthereunder, subsidiaries of CIL are maintaining various statutory welfare facilities for the coal mines \nsuch as canteen, rest shelters and pit-head baths. \n  \nNON-STATUTORY WELFARE MEASURE \nIn order to supply essential commodities and consumer goods at a cheaper rate in the collieries, \nCentral Co-operative and Primary Co-operative Stores are functioning in the coalfield areas of CIL. \nIn addition, Co-operative Credit Societies are also functioning in the coal companies. \nBANKING FACILITIES \nThe Management of Coal companies are providing infrastructure facilities to the var ious \nnationalized banks for opening their branches and extension counters in the coalfields for the benefit \nof coal workers. Workers are   educated  to  draw their salaries   from the banks   and  they  are also \nencouraged to practice thrift for the benefit of their families. \nHOLIDAY HOMES \nCoal India Limited has maintained Holiday Homes in important spots across the country. \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|3|UNSTARRED |54", "t": "Skill Development Mission in Rajasthan", "head": " \n \n \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF RAILWAYS \n \nLOK SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION NO. 54 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 05.02.2020 \n \nABSENCE OF PROPER LIGHTING AT STATIONS \n \n †*54. SHRI KAUSHALENDRA KUMAR: \n \nWill the Minister of RAILWAYS be pleased to state: \n \n(a) whether the Government is aware that passengers face \ndifficulties in identifying the station in darkness due to lack of \nproper lighting at railway stations located in rural areas; \n \n(b) whether passengers also face difficulties due to the absence of \nan announcement system in the trains about the next station; \n \n(c) whether the Government proposes to make arrangements for \nthe advance announcement of name of the next station as well \nas written information in each coach thro ugh screening board \non the lines of metro trains to address the said problem; and \n \n(d) if so, the time by which it is likely to be done? \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF RAILWAYS AND COMMERCE & INDUSTRY \n \n  (SHRI PIYUSH GOYAL) \n \n  \n(a)  to (d):  A Statement is laid on the Table of the House. \n***** \n \n\n \n \nSTATEMENT REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PARTS (a) TO (d) OF \nSTARRED QUESTION NO. 54 BY SHRI KAUSHALENDRA KUMAR TO BE \nANSWERED IN LOK SABHA ON 05.02.2020 REGARDING ABSENCE OF \nPROPER LIGHTING AT STATIONS \n \n(a): Based on suggestion received from Consultative Committee of MPs \nfor the Ministry of Railways  for need of proper illumination of station \nname boards displayed at the two ends of  the platforms, it was \nreiterated to Zonal Railways that electric poles provided at the \nplatforms of all electrified stations should be  adjusted in such a \nmanner that station name boards provided at the two ends of the \nplatforms are properly illuminated so as to be read easily during night. \nIt was also emphasized  that lights specifically provid ed for \nillumination of the station name boards are kept glowing during the \nnight. So far, 8016 stations name board are illuminated during night \ntime. \n(b) to (d) : Suggestion has been received from Member of Consultative \nCommittee that coaches should have fa cilities of display of name of \napproaching stations.  \nIn the existing coach design, next station name announcement is \nprovided on some of the trains like Electrical Multiple Units \n(EMUs)/Mainline Electric Multiple Units (MEMUs) suburban trains and \nother select group of trains like Rajdhani, Shtabdi, Tejas and Humsafar \netc. \nContd.2/- \n\n \n \n-2- \nFurther, Modern Coach Factory (MCF) and Rail Coach Factory (RCF) \nare a dvised for fitment of system as per Research Designs and \nStandards Organization (RDSO)  trial specification for field trial on 50 \ncoaches each by MCF and RCF and fitment is under process.  All newly \nmanufactured EMU/MEMU rakes are already equipped with such \ntechnology/system. \nTo further proliferate this system, specification of Public Address \nand Passenger Information System (PAPIS) has been issued in August \n2019. Display of name of approaching station is part of PAPIS.  Initially \nfitment of PAPIS for field trial has been started in 100 coaches and will \nbe completed on priority.  \n***** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|3|UNSTARRED |540", "t": "Internet Facility in Remote Villages of Maharashtra", "head": " \n \n1 \n \n \n \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF COMMUNICATIONS  \nDEPARTMENT OF TELECOMMUNICATIONS \n \nLOK  SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 540  \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 5TH FEBRUARY, 2020 \n \nINTERNET FACILITY IN REMOTE VILLAGES OF MAHARASHTRA \n \n†540.  DR. BHARATI PRAVIN PAWAR:  \nSHRI SADASHIV KISAN LOKHANDE:  \n \nWill the Minister of COMMUNICATIONS be pleased to state:  \n \n(a)  whether there is no internet facility in remote villages of Maharashtra and if so, the details \nthereof and the reasons therefor along with the reaction of the Government thereto;  \n \n(b)  whether internet/broadband speed is very slow in remote areas where internet/broadband \nfacility is available;  \n \n(c)  if so, the details thereof and the reasons therefor; and  \n \n(d)  the efforts made by the Government to overcome this problem? \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF STATE FOR COMMUNICATIONS,  \n HUMAN RESOURCE DEVELOPMENT AND  \nELECTRONICS & INFORMATION TECHNOLOGY  \n(SHRI SANJAY DHOTRE) \n(a) Internet is currently being accessed mainly through mobile wireless 2G, 3G and 4G \ntechnologies which at present cover more than 95% of population of the country. As per data \ncollected from the Telecom Service Providers (TSPs) in 2019, it is estimated that out of total 40,959 \ninhabited villages (as per the Census 2011) in Maharashtra, 37,970 are covered by mobile services.  \nThere are 36.84 rural internet subscribers per 100 population in Maharashtra as per “The \nIndian Telecom Services Performance Indicators”  report of Telecom Regulatory Authority of India \n(TRAI) dated 8th January, 2020.  The National Digital Communications Policy-2018 has been notified \nwith strategic objectives which inter-alia includes provisioning of Broadband for all. \n(b) & (c)  Internet s peed depends inter -alia on various parameters such as the technology \ndeployed, network coverage, number of simultaneous users accessing the internet, connectivity to the \nwebsite being accessed etc.  The speed of mobile internet is further dependent on a nu mber of factors \nsuch as distance from the Base Transceiver Station (BTS) serving the customer, the number of \nsimultaneous users being served by the BTS, the traffic handled by the BTS etc.  As such, the mobile \nusers may experience different speed at different locations and time of usage. \n\n \n \n2 \n \n(d)  Following initiatives/actions have been taken by the Government towards provisioning and \nimproving internet/broadband services: \n(i) BharatNet project is being implemented in a phased manner to provide broadband/internet \nconnectivity to all the Gram Panchayats (GPs) (approx. 2.5 lakh) in the country, including \nMaharashtra. A total of 1,34,248 GPs have been made Service Ready as on 24.01 .2020, \nincluding 15,534 in Maharashtra.  \n(ii) As part of BharatNet project, the last mile connectivity through Wi -Fi or any other suitable \nbroadband technology to access internet/broadband services is to  be provided at all the GPs in \nthe country including Maharashtra.  \n(iii)Universal Service Obligation Fund (USOF) scheme for setting up of 25,000 public Wi -Fi \nhotspots including 3800 in Maharashtra using the infrastructure of telephone exchanges of \nBharat Sanchar Nigam Limited (BSNL) in rural areas. \n(iv) Under Left Wing Extremist (LWE) Phase -II project, installation of 2217 mobile towers is \nplanned for providing mobile connectivity covering the 10 States including 125 towers in \nMaharashtra. \n \n************ "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|3|UNSTARRED |541", "t": "Extension of Lokmanya Tilak Express", "head": " \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF RAILWAYS \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 541 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 05.02.2020 \n \nEXTENSION OF LOKMANYA TILAK EXPRESS \n \n   541. SHRI SAPTAGIRI SANKAR ULAKA: \n \n            Will the Minister of RAILWAYS be pleased to state: \n \n(a) whether Railways is considering the request to extend Lokmanya \nTilak Express - 18519/18520(Mumbai - Vishakhapatnam) to Rayagada, \nwhich is commercially viable without additional expenses apart from \nserving the needs of tribal dominated undivided Koraput District;  \n \n(b) if so, the details thereof and the timeline by when this would be \nextended; and  \n \n(c) if not, the reasons therefor and the alternate arrangement to \naddress the demands of people? \n  \n \nANSWER \n  \nMINISTER OF RAILWAYS AND COMMERCE & INDUSTRY  \n \n(SHRI PIYUSH GOYAL) \n \n \n(a) to (c) No, Sir.  Extension of 18519/18520 Visakhapatnam -\nLokmanya Tilak (T) Express upto Rayagada is not feasible, at \npresent, due to operational and resource constraints including non -\navailability of maintenance facilities at Raya gada. However, \npassengers of Rayagada/Koraput may avail change -over at \nVizianagaram/Visakhapatnam. \n***** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|3|UNSTARRED |542", "t": "Opening of Reservation Centres Round the Clock", "head": "    \n     \n \n       GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF RAILWAYS \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 542 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 05.02.2020 \n \nOPENING OF RESERVATION CENTRES ROUND THE CLOCK \n \n†542. SHRI RAVINDRA KUSHWAHA:  \n  \n Will the Minister of RAILWAYS be pleased to state: \n \n(a) whether any proposal/scheme is under consideration of the \nGovernment to open round the clock Reservation Counters to facilitate \nthe rail passengers in view of their increasing number; \n(b) if so, the details thereof; \n(c) whether any success is likely to  be achieved through curbing the \nblack marketing of Rail Reservation Tickets after the implementation of \nthe said system; and \n(d) if so, the details thereof? \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF RAILWAYS AND COMMERCE & INDUSTRY \n \n(SHRI PIYUSH GOYAL)   \n \n(a)  to (d):   A Statement is laid on the Table of the House. \n \n   ****** \n\n \nSTATEMENT REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PARTS (a) TO (d) OF \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 542 BY SHRI RAVINDRA KUSHWAHA \nTO BE ANSWERED IN LOK SABHA ON 05.02.2020 REGARDING \nOPENING OF RESERVATION CENTRES ROUND THE CLOCK.  \n \n(a) and (b): At present, the facility to book reserved tickets is \navailable through computerised Passenger Reservation System \n(PRS) counters of Indian Railways and through internet.  PRS \ncounters generally function between 0800 hours and 2000 hours on \nweekdays and from 0800 hours to 1400 hours on Sundays. At some \nlocations, working hours of these counters are also \nenhanced/reduced depending on the number of transactions \nhandled.  In addition, at certain major locations, reservation \ncounters also operate to issue tickets against vacant \naccommodation available after preparation of first reservation \ncharts or to cancel accommodation already booked as per \nprovisions of extant Refund Rules.  \n \n  At present, more than 70% of reserved tickets of Indian \nRailways a re booked online through website of IRCTC where this \nfacility is available throughout the day except for a short period of \napproximately 4 0 minutes between 234 0 hours and 0020 hours \nduring which user login is disabled.  The facility to procure reserved \ntickets is also available through Yatri Ticket Suvidha Kendra \n(YTSK). YTSKs are located outside station premises thus sparing \nthe general public from queuing up PRS counters of Indian Railways \nto source tickets. These arrangements are considered adequate. \nContd.  2/- \n\n    \n- 2 - \n(c) and (d):  Measures to streamline reservation system and to deter \nunscrupulous elements from misusing the reservation facility meant \nfor genuine passengers is a continuous and an on -going process. \nSome of the important initiatives taken in this regard are as under:   \ni. Instructions have been issued to ensure that tickets are not \nbooked on abbreviated names and full name of the passenger \nand the surname wherever applicable are captured at the time \nof booking reserved tickets.  \nii. Carrying of prescribed identity proof has been made compulsory \nfor one of the passengers while undertaking journey in reserved \nclass. \niii. Regular checks are conducted in mass contact areas such as \nPassenger Reservation System (PRS) centres, booking offices, \nplatforms, trains  etc. to prevent unauthorized ticketing \nactivities. Such checks are also intensified during peak period \nlike festivals, holidays, etc. \niv. Checks conducted at reservation offices are supplemented by \nchecks on the trains to detect the cases of transferred reser ved \ntickets i.e. persons found travelling fraudulently against \naccommodation actually reserved in the name of another \npassenger. \nv. Surveillance at reservation offices is stepped up by installing \nClose Circuit Television at important PRS locations to keep \nwatch on any possible misuse of reservation system.  \nvi. In case of online booking of tickets, checks have been applied \non minimum time required to enter the passenger details and \nCAPTCHA and no ticket can be booked before 35 seconds. User \nIDs are checked on dail y basis and those IDs found using \nmalpractices like fast booking of tickets are deactivated. \n\n- 3  - \nvii. A limit of booking 6 Railway tickets by an individual user in a \nmonth in case of Indian Railway Catering and Tourism \nCorporation (IRCTC) portal has been fixed . This limit has been \nrevised to 12 Railway tickets in a month for those individual \nusers who have linked their IRCTC user ID with their \nrespective Aadhaar number and ensured that at least one of \nthe passengers in the passenger list is verifiable through \nAadhaar. \nviii. The authorized agents of IRCTC have been restricted from \nbooking tickets during first fifteen minutes of opening of \nAdvance Reservation Period (ARP) booking and Tatkal \nbooking.  \nix. General public are also educated through Public Address \nSystem and med ia, not to buy tickets from unscrupulous \nelements and consequences of buying tickets from these \nsources. \nx. In order to cater to additional demand during peak rush period, \nspecial trains are run and carrying capacity "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|3|UNSTARRED |543", "t": "Field Firing Range", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF DEFENCE \nDEPARTMENT OF MILITARY AFFAIRS  \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.543 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 5th February, 2020 \n \nFIELD FIRING RANGE \n543. SHRI RAHUL KASWAN: \nWill the Minister of DEFENCE j{kk ea=h  \nbe pleased to state: \n (a) number of field firing ranges in the country at present; \n(b) the criteria prescribed by the Government for setting up of new field firing ranges; \n(c) whether there is need for setting up more firing ranges; \n(d) if so, the details thereof, location-wise; \n(e) whether the Government has received proposals  for setting up of more field firing \nranges by the Army in any district of Rajasthan; and \n(f) if so, the details thereof? \n \n \nA    N    S    W    E    R \n \nMINISTER OF STATE     (SHRI SHRIPAD NAIK) \nIN THE MINISTRY OF DEFENCE    \nरक्षा राज्य मंत्री     (श्री श्रीपाद नाईक)    \n \n(a)  There are 52 Field Firing Ranges (FFRs) with the Army. \n(b)  Criteria for setting up of new firing range are as under:- \n(i)  Training and operational requirements of the Field Formations. \n       (ii)  Dependency of troops on the Field Firing Range. \n(iii) Availability of land to satisfy the danger area template for the weapon \nsystem(s) envisaged to be fired.  \n \n…2/- \n \n\n: 2 : \n \n(iv)  Availability of good road and rail communication network. \n(v) Economically viable justification of acquiring a Field Range in the vicinity \nof the cantonments. \n(c) & (d): The requirement of setting up firing ranges is to cater for induction of new \nequipment as and when inducted into Service.  The same is worked out as per the \ncapability development plan. \n(e) No, Sir. \n(f)  Not applicable. \n \n \n***** \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|3|UNSTARRED |544", "t": "Performance of Planning Commission", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF PLANNING \n \nLOK SABHA  \nUN-STARRED QUESTION NO. 544 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 05/02/2020  \n \nPERFORMANCE OF PLANNING COMMISSION \n \n544. SHRI PRATHAP SIMHA: \n \n  Will the Minister of PLANNING be pleased to state: \n \n(a) whether the Government has reviewed the success achieved by t he Planning Commission in \nits objective of raising the living standard of the people;  \n \n(b) if so, the details and the outcome thereof;  \n \n(c) whether the Government proposes to constitute an expert group to evaluate the performanc e \nof the Planning Commission and redefine its role and objectives so as to relate the planning \nprocess to the life of the common man; and  \n \n(d) if not, the reasons therefor and the steps taken to revamp and reform the plannin g process in \nthe country? \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF STATE (INDEPENDENT CHARGE) OF THE MINISTRY OF PLANNING AND \nMINISTER OF STATE (INDEPENDENT CHARGE) OF THE MINISTRY OF STATISTICS \nAND PROGRAMME IMPLEMENTATION \n(RAO INDERJIT SINGH) \n(a) & (b): No such review was carried out. Sir. \n  \n(c) & (d): In order to evolve a shared vision of national development priorities, sectors and \nstrategies with the active involvement of States in the light of national objectives, the \nGovernment of India has replaced Planning Commission with NITI Aayog with eff ect from \nJanuary 1, 2015. NITI Aayog has been providing directional and policy inputs to Central \nMinistries/Departments and States Governments across the range of sectors such as \nagriculture, science & technology, education, healthcare, water resources, ur banization, \nenergy, transport, and so on. In performance of its integrated role in the development of a \nshared vision of national priorities and strategies, NITI Aayog actively engages with States, \ncivil society and other think tanks. NITI Aayog has come o ut with i) a three-year Action Plan \nfor 2017-18 to 2019 -20 based on the predictability of resources during the currency of 14 th \nFinance Commission Award period, ii) Strategy for New India @ 75 document with policy \nrecommendations in 41 key areas with drive rs of growth, infrastructure, inclusion and \ngovernance and iii) 15 years Vision Document which is currently under preparation in \nconsultation with concerned stakeholders.  \n \n***** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|3|UNSTARRED |545", "t": "Track Electrification", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF RAILWAYS \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 545 \n TO BE ANSWERED ON 05.02.2020 \n \nTRACK ELECTRIFICATION  \n \n \n 545.   SHRI VIJAY KUMAR DUBEY: \n     SHRI MANOJ TIWARI: \n   \n  \n          Will the Minister of RAILWAYS be pleased to state: \n \n(a)   whether the Government has achieved its target of track \nelectrification in the entire country; \n \n(b)     if so, the details thereof; \n \n(c)  if not, the reason therefor; and \n \n(d)   the details of timeline for the completion of any such \nproposal in the near future? \n \n \n \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF RAILWAYS AND COMMERCE & INDUSTRY \n \n(SHRI PIYUSH GOYAL) \n \n \n \n(a) to (d)  A Statement is laid on the Table of the House. \n \n \n***** \n \n  \n\nSTATEMENT REFERRED TO IN  REPLY TO PARTS (a) TO ( d) OF \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 545 BY SHRI VIJAY KUMAR DUBEY AND \nSHRI MANOJ TIWARI TO BE ANSWERED IN LOK SABHA ON 05.02.2020 \nREGARDING TRACK ELECTRIFICATION \n \n(a) & (c) No, Sir. However, Ministry of Railways has planned to electrify \nbalance Broad Gauge (BG) routes of Indian Railways (IR) by December, \n2023. \n \n(b) Does not arise. \n \n(d) The time line for completion of Electrification of entire                            \nBG routes of IR is as under: \n \nYear Target (Route Kilometre) \n2019-20 6,000 \n2454 route kms achieved upto 01.01.2020 \n2020-21 6,000 \n2021-22 6,000 \n2022-23 6,500 \n2023-24  \n(upto Dec-23) \n4,310 \nTotal 28,810 \n \n    ****** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|3|UNSTARRED |546", "t": "Utilization of Social and Infrastructure Development Fund", "head": "Government of India \nMinistry of Development of North Eastern Region \nLOK SABHA \nUnstarred Question No.546 \nTo be answered on \nWednesday, February 5, 2020/Magha 16, 1941 (Saka) \nQuestion \nUtilization of Social and Infrastructure Development Fund \n \n546.       SHRI VINCENT H. PALA: \nWill the Minister of DEVELOPMENT OF NORTH EASTERN R EGION be     \npleased to state: \n(a) the details of expenditure made so far from the  Social and Infrastructure \nDevelopment Fund, State-wise; \n(b) whether the funds have been  under utilized; an d  \n   (c) the manner in which Government intends to in vest this fund for the \ndevelopment of the North Eastern Region? \n \nANSWER \nThe Minister of State (Independent Charge) of the Ministry of Development of \nNorth Eastern Region \n[Dr. Jitendra Singh] \n \n(a) to (c)  Against a total package amount of Rs.58 6.20 crore under Social and \nInfrastructure Development Fund (SIDF), projects wo rth of Rs.587.17 crore has \nbeen sanctioned by Ministry of Development of North  Eastern Region. Out of \nthis, an amount of Rs.504.10 crore has already been  released and balance \namount will be released on receipt of physical & fi nancial reports from the \nconcerned State Governments. The State wise details  of these projects are as \nunder: -  \n             \n                                     \n\n  (Rs. in crore) \nName of State No. of Project Approved \nCost \nFunds released  \nArunachal Pradesh 11 187.53 115.75 \nAssam 8 103.51 96.67 \nManipur 6 124.92 123.87 \nMeghalaya 2 18.84 18.19 \nMizoram 1 1.99 1.00 \nNagaland 4 68.09 66.96 \nSikkim 4 44.20 43.58 \nTripura 1 38.08 38.08 \nTOTAL 37 587.17 504.10 \n \n*****  "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|3|UNSTARRED |547", "t": "Implementation of Agri Export Policy", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA MINISTRY OF COMMERCE & INDUSTRY (DEPARTMENT OF COMMERCE) LOK SABHA UNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 547  TO BE ANSWERED ON 05 th FEBRUARY, 2020 TO BE ANSWERED ON 05 th FEBRUARY, 2020 \nIMPLEMENTATION OF AGRI EXPORT POLICY 547. SHRI CHANDRA SEKHAR SAHU: Will the Minister of COMMERCE & INDUSTRY (वािण�य एवं उ�ोग मं�ी ) be pleased to state: (a) whether there is any proposal to adopt a focused approach for ensuring greater involvement (a) whether there is any proposal to adopt a focused approach for ensuring greater involvement of the State Governments for effective implementation of Agri Export Policy (AEP); (b) if so, the details in this regard; (c) whether the Government has requested the States to finalize action plan for AEP with the aim to double such exports; (d) if so, the response of States thereto along with the current status of the action plan in other States; and (e) the c orrective steps so far taken to expedite the formulation and implementation of AEP? ANSWER okf.kT; ,oa m|ksx ea=h ( Jh ih;w’k xks;y )THE MINISTER OF  COMMERCE AND INDUSTRY (SHRI PIYUSH GOYAL) (a& b) : The Government has introduced a comprehensive Agriculture Export Policy (AEP), which inter -alia aims at greater involvement of the State Governments in promoting agricultural exports.  Involvement of the States has been envisaged in the following: i. Inclusion of Agri Export Policy in the State Export Policy. ii. Identification of State Nodal Agency for promotion of agri exports. ii. Identification of State Nodal Agency for promotion of agri exports. iii. To set up institutional mechanism at State and Cluster level by forming Committees to support exports iv. Infrastructure and logi stics to facilitate agricultural exports (c& d) :Yes, Sir. Department of Commerce has requested all the State Governments and UTs to finalize their action plan for AEP with aim to double agriculture exports. Eight States have finalised the State specific Action Plans . 25 States and 2 UTs have designated nodal agencies to promote agricultural exports. 16 States have constituted State Level Monitoring Committees. \n\n(e ): To monitor the implementation of the AEP, an Inter Ministerial Committee (IMC) has been fo rmed. Agricultural and Processed Food Products Export Development Authority (APEDA) has held a series of meetings with states and stakeholders, convened national w orkshops , made field visits, identified existing infrastructure gaps in the clusters/states, organised buyer -seller meets, launched farmer connect portal on its website, opened market intelligence cell etc., for expediting the implementation of AEP. ********"}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|3|UNSTARRED |548", "t": "Stoppage at Bagdogra", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF RAILWAYS \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.548 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 05.02.2020 \n \nSTOPPAGE AT BAGDOGRA \n \n548. SHRI RAJU BISTA: \n  \nWill the Minister of RAILWAYS be pleased to state: \n \n(a) whether any new steps are being taken to create train stoppages at \nBagdogra, Naxalbari, Phansidewa, Chopra and Thakurganj. \n \n(b) if so, whether the Ministry is aware that this route caters to the most \nimportant Agricultural, Horticultural and Animal Husbandry belt in \nDarjeeling Terai Region; and  \n \n(c) if so, the potential steps taken/proposed to be taken by the Ministry to \naddress the transportation of Agricultural and Dairy products from this belt \nto the rest of the nation? \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF RAILWAYS AND COMMERCE & INDUSTRY \n \n(SHRI PIYUSH GOYAL) \n \n(a) Provision of stoppages of trains on Indian Railways, is an on -going \nprocess, subject to operational feasibility, traffic justification, etc. \nAccordingly, stoppage of 15721/15722 Digha - New Jalpaiguri Paharia  \nExpress has been provided at Thakurganj station w.e.f. 10.03.2019. \nFurther, both Bagdogra & Naksalbari are well served by 03 pairs of \nMail/Express trains. However, no stations named “Phansidewa” and \n“Chopra” are located on Kishanganj -Thakurganj-Siliguri s ector of the \nNortheast Frontier Railway.  \n(b) and (c) There is a potential for parcel traffic including agricultural \nproducts through Brake Van of train no. 15464/15720/15722. However, at \npresent no demand has been received from any “Stakeholder/Party.”   \n***** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|3|UNSTARRED |549", "t": "Indians Settled Abroad", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA\nMINISTRY OF EXTERNAL AFFAIRS\nLOK SABHA\nUNSTARRED QUEATION NO.549\nTO BE ANSWERED ON 05.02.2020\nINDIANS SE