     Will the Minister of HOUSING & URBAN AFFAIRS be pleased to state: \n \n(a) whether the Union Government has received any p roposal \nregarding the poverty alleviation and creation of employment in \nMaharashtra during the last three years;  \n(b)  if so, the details thereof and the number of p roposals that have \nbeen approved; \n(c)  the proposals still to be approved and the rea sons for the delay \nin approving those proposals; and  \n(d)  the action taken by the Government to approve the pending \nproposals and the outcome thereof? \nANSWER \n \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE (INDEPENDENT CHARGE) OF THE \nMINISTRY OF HOUSING AND URBAN AFFAIRS \n \n(SHRI HARDEEP SINGH PURI) \n \n(a):  No, Sir. The Ministry of Housing and Urban Af fairs is, \nhowever, implementing a Centrally Sponsored Scheme viz. \n“Deendayal Antyodaya Yojana- National Urban Livelih oods \nMission (DAY-NULM)” in the statutory towns to reduc e \npoverty and vulnerability of urban poor households by \nenabling them access to gainful self-employment and  skilled \nwage employment opportunities for improvement in th eir \nlivelihoods on a sustainable basis, facilitating  s ubsidised \ncredit, building strong grassroot level community i nstitutions \nfor the urban poor, providing shelter equipped with  essential \nservices for the urban homeless, and addressing liv elihood \nconcerns of the urban street vendors by facilitatin g access \nto suitable vending spaces, institutional credit, s ocial \nsecurity, etc. Since 2014-15, Rs. 372.40 crore has been \nreleased to Maharashtra for implementation of DAY-NULM.  \n(b) to (d): Does not arise in view of (a) above. \n***** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|3|UNSTARRED |745", "t": "SAI Training Centres", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF YOUTH AFFAIRS AND SPORTS \n(DEPARTMENT OF SPORTS) \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 745 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 06.02.2020 \n \nSAI Training Centres \n \n745.  SHRI ANTO ANTONY: \n \n  Will the Minister of YOUTH AFFAIRS AND SPORTS  be pleased to  \nstate: \n \n(a) whether the Government has any statistics regarding the number \nof training centres set up by Sports  Authority of India (SAI) in the \ncountry during each of the last five years; \n(b) if so, the details of such centres set up and the  staff, including \ncoaches and their assistants in the country, State-wise; \n(c) whether the Government has any plan to enhance  the number of \nSAI Centres in the country; \n(d) if so, the details thereof and the steps taken by  the Government \nin this regard; \n(e) whether the G overnment has any statistics  regarding the funds \nallotted to SAI Centres in the country; \n(f) if so, the details of funds given to SAI Centres  during each of the \nlast five years; \n(g) whether the Government has received any  complaints from any \nquarter regarding the misbehaviour of coaches and other trainers; and \n(h) if so, the details thereof and the steps taken by  the Government \nin this regard? \n \nANSWER \n \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE (INDEPENDENT CHARGE) \nFOR YOUTH AFFAIRS AND SPORTS \n(SHRI KIREN RIJIJU) \n \n(a) to (d): The Sports infrastructure facilities available with Sports \nAuthority of India (SAI) &  Lakshmibai National Institute of Physical \nEducation (LNIPE) have sufficient capacity to cater to the training \nneeds of a large number of athletes including high performanc e \n\ntraining to elite athletes and hence no new Trai ning Institute/ Training \nCentre has been established during last five years and no proposal is \nunder consideration to open any new Training Institute. \n(e) & (f) The details of the funds allocated by the Min istry to SAI is \nas under: \n                                                               ( in crore) \nS.No. Financial  Year Funds \n1 2014-15 ₹ 355.28 \n2 2015-16 ₹ 345.78 \n3 2016-17 ₹ 367.20 \n4 2017-18 ₹ 495.73 \n5 2018-19 ₹ 395.00 \n  \n(g) & (h) Yes Sir, in the last 4 years, 19 complaints were received  of \nwhich, 4 were withdrawn.  Services of 3 officials have been \nterminated and 6 officials have b een charge -sheeted.  Inquiry \nproceeding is not yet complete in the remaining cases.    \n***** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|3|UNSTARRED |746", "t": "Use of FASTags at Toll Plazas", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA\n \nMINISTRY OF ROAD TRANSPORT AND HIGHWAYS\n \nLOK SABHA \n \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. \n746\n \nANSWERED ON \n6\nTH\n \nFEBRUARY\n, \n2020\n \nUSE\n \nOF FASTAG\nS AT TOLL PLAZAS\n \n74\n6\n.\n \nSHRI \nBHAGWANTH KHUBA\n:\n \n \n \nWill the Minister of ROAD TRANSPORT AND HIGHWAYS\n \nlM+d\n \nifjogu\n \nvkSj\n \njktekxZ\n \nea=h\n \nbe pleased to state:\n \n(a)  whether  the  Government  is  aware  that  only\n \n50 per cent of FASTags are issued by various \nagencies\n \nin  the  country;\n \n(b)  if  so,  the  details  thereof;\n \n(c)  the  number  of  toll  plazas  which  have  hybrid\n \nlane\ns  in  the  country;  and\n \n(d)  the  time likely  to  be  taken  to  make  all  the  toll\n \nplazas  FASTag  compliant  and  avoid  \nhardship  to  road\n \nusers\n?\n \nANSWER\n \nTHE MINISTER OF ROAD TRANSPORT AND HIGHWAYS\n \n(\nSHRI \nNITIN JAIRAM GADKARI\n)\n \n(a)\n \n&(b) \nFASTag \nis being issued through multiple channels and Point of Sale (PoS) including e\n-\ncommerce platforms. \nThere are twenty\n-\nfour (24) banks (including Public and Private sector banks) \nengaged as Issuer Banks for FASTag issuance to road users and eight (08) Acquirer\n \nBanks \nto process \nthe transactions at f\nee plazas.\n \nAs on 28.01.2020, more than 1.4 Crores FASTag\ns\n \nha\nve\n \nbeen issued. \n \n(c) &(d) \nIn July 2019, Government announced the FASTag mandate of declaring all lanes except one \nlane of National Highways fee plazas as “FA\nSTag lane of Fee Plaza” w.e.f. 1st December 2019. In \norder to facilitate sufficient lead time to citizens to obtain FASTag, the mandate was shifted to 15th \nDecember 2019 and the system was implemented across the country. Considering the request of \nNational\n \nHighways Authority of India (NHAI) and keeping in view inconvenience to citizens, \nGovernment decided that 25 percent of the FASTag lanes may be temporarily converted to hybrid \nlanes with the approval of Regional Officer concerned for next 30 days from 15t\nh December 2019. It \nwas further decided to relax the conditions regarding declaration of FASTag fee lanes for 30 days \nfrom 15th January 2020 at only 65 fee plazas of NHAI so that citizens are not put into inconvenience. \nThese 65 fee plazas have been allowe\nd to operate up to 25 per cent of all fee lanes as hybrid (cash \nplus FASTag) lanes during this period. For rest of the fee plazas the system has been implemented as \nper mandate.\n \n*****\n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|3|UNSTARRED |747", "t": "Fit India Movement", "head": " \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF YOUTH AFFAIRS & SPORTS \nDEPARTMENT OF SPORTS \n \nLOK SABHA \n \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.747 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 06.02.2020 \n                       Fit India Movement \n747. SHRI MOHAMMED FAIZAL P.P.: \n \n Will the Minister of YOUTH AFFAIRS AND SPORTS be pleased \nto state: \n \n(a)  whether the Government  is planning to promote sports through \n‘Fit India Movement’; \n(b)  if so, the details thereof and the steps taken in this regard;  \n(c) the difficulties faced by the Government in the promotion of \ntraditional rural and indigenous sports in the country ; \n(d) whether the Government has initiated any awareness drive for \nthe promotion of traditional rural and indigenous/tribal sports, if \nso, the details thereof and the response thereto; and   \n(e) the other steps taken by the Government to encourage youth s \nto take interest in such sports? \n \nANSWER \n \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE (INDEPENDENT CHARGE)  \nFOR YOUTH AFFAIRS AND SPORTS \n[SHRI KIREN RIJIJU] \n \n(a) & (b) For the Fit India Movement, the Government is playing \nthe role of a catalyst so that Fit India become  a people’s movement. \nFit India is a people centric movement to be run on voluntary basis \nwith participation of as many citizens as possible. The main \nobjective of Fit India Movement is to create awareness amongst all \nthe citizens of India about fitness an d its importance in daily life \n\nthrough sports, yogasan, walking, cycling, dancing or any other \nphysical activity.  \n \n(c)  Traditional rural and indigenous games generally exist in \ninformal setup and do not have adequate leaders to represent. \nGovernment’s emphasis on promotion of rural and indigenous games \nhas encouraged leadership and formalization of the same. \n \n(d)& (e)  The following initiatives have been taken by the \nGovernment to promote the traditional rural and indigenous games \nin the country: \n \ni. Mallakhamb, Kalaripayattu, Gatka and Thang -Ta are being \nsupported under the vertical of ‘Promotion of Rural and \nIndigenous/Tribal Games’ under the Khelo India Scheme. \n \nii. Total amount of Rs.10.85 crore  have been sanctioned for the \nyear 2019 -20 for infrastructure development, equipment support, \nappointment of coaches, training of coaches and scholarships. \n \niii. Out of 335 Medal Winners of Indigenous Games, 247 \nrecommended athletes by the 04 NSFs are receiving  scholarships \namounting to Rs.10,000/- per month per athlete. \n \niv. Kho-Kho and Kabaddi disciplines were included in Khelo India \nYouth Games 2020 held at Guwahati . Kabaddi discipline has been \nincluded in the first edition of Khelo  India University Games being \nheld in Bhubaneshwar from February 22nd to 1st March, 2020. \n \n**** \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|3|UNSTARRED |748", "t": "Sports Stadia", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF YOUTH AFFAIRS AND SPORTS \n(DEPARTMENT OF SPORTS) \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 748 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 06.02.2020 \n \nSports Stadia \n†748. SHRI ARUN SAO: \n \nWill the Minister of YOUTH AFFAIRS AND SPORTS  be pleased to \nstate: \n \n(a) the details of sports stadia operated and  administered by the \nSports Authority of India (SAI) inthe country, State/UT -wise including \nChhattisgarh; \n(b) whether there is more than 100 per cent rise in the fees for the use \nof any sports facilities being provi ded by the SAI and if so, the details \nthereof, sports-wise and the reasons therefor; \n(c) the details of plans implemented by the SAI,  plan-wise and the \namount of funds sanctioned/  released/ spent under these plans for \nmaintenance of SAI stadia  during the l ast three years and the current \nyear; and \n(d) the manner in which these plans are beneficial  for sports skill \ndevelopment among youths of the country? \n \nANSWER \n \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE (INDEPENDENT CHARGE) \nFOR YOUTH AFFAIRS AND SPORTS \n(SHRI KIREN RIJIJU) \n \n(a) There are 05 Sports stadia located in New Delhi being operated \nand administered by Sports Authority of India (SAI) viz. Jawaharlal Nehru \nStadium, India Gandhi Indoor Stadium, Major Dhyan Chand National \nStadium, Dr.  Shyama Prasad Mookherjee Swimming Pool C omplex and \nDr. Karni Singh Shooting Range. \n \n(b) No Sir.  The Department of Sports has made sports facilities under \nthe Department of Sports and maintained by Sports Authority of India \n\n(SAI), Lakshmibai National Institute of Physical Education (LNIPE), \nGwalior and LNIPE, Guwahati, available free of cost w.e.f. 01.11.2019. \n \n(c) & (d) The SAI is providing training to the athletes under its \nRegional Centres, Netaji Sub has National Institute of Sports (NSNIS), \nPatiala, and other training centres established by S AI at different \nlocations.  The Ministry is providing block grants to SAI for running the \nSAI including its training facilities and stadia.  The information for \ncurrent year and the previous 3 years of block grants provided to SAI is \nas below:    \n                                                      \n(in crore) \nS. No. Financial  Year Funds allocated by Ministry \n1 2016-17 ₹ 367.20 \n2 2017-18 ₹ 495.73 \n3 2018-19 ₹ 395.00 \n4 2019-20 (upto 31.01.2020) ₹ 539.00 \n \n The year-wise expenditure of SAI for operation a nd maintenance of \nSAI stadia located in New Delhi is as below: \n                                                         (in crore) \nS. No. Financial  Year Expenditure incurred for operation and \nmaintenance of sports stadia in New \nDelhi \n1 2016-17 ₹ 114.04 \n2 2017-18 ₹ 90.78 \n3 2018-19 ₹ 114.79 \n4 2019-20 \n(upto 31.12.2019) \n₹ 82.05 \n \n \n***** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|3|UNSTARRED |749", "t": "MSMEs Engaged in Domestic Defence Production", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA\n \nMINISTRY OF MICRO, SMALL AND MEDIUM ENTERPRISES\n \n \nLOK SABHA\n \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 749\n \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 06.02.2020\n \n \nMSMEs ENGAGED IN DOMESTIC DEFENCE PRODUCTION \n \n \n749.\n \nSHRI  SUMEDHANAND SARASWATI :        \n \n  \n \nWill the Minister of \nMICRO, SMALL AND MEDIUM ENTERPRISES pleased to state:\n \n \n(a) \n \nthe number of Micro, Small and Medium enterprises (MSMEs) engaged in \ndomestic defence production in the country;\n \n(b) \n \nthe measures being taken by the Ministry to promote MSMEs in defence productio\nn \nsector;\n \n(c) \n \nwhether the Ministry is providing protection to the domestic MSMEs engaged in \ndefence production against foreign Original Equipment Manufacturer (OEM) companies \nas a result of foreign investment limit reaching 74 per cent; and\n \n(d) \n \nif so, th\ne details thereof?\n \n \n \nANSWER\n \n \nMINISTER OF MICRO, SMALL AND MEDIUM ENTERPRISES\n \n \n(SHRI NITIN GADKARI)\n \n \n(a):     As reported by Department of Defence Production, number of MSME vendors of \nDefence\n \nPublic Sector Enterprises / Ordinance  Factory Board till 30.09.2019  is 10506.\n \n \n(b):\n \n \nMinistry of Micro, Small and Medium Enterprises implements various schemes \nand programmes for promotion and development of Micro, Small and Medium Enterprises \n(MSMEs) i\nncluding defence sector MSMEs across the country. These include Prime \nMinister’s Employment Generation Programme (PMEGP), Scheme of Fund for \nR\negeneration of Traditional Industries (SFURTI), A scheme for Promoting Innovation, \nRural Industry and Entrepreneur\nship (ASPIRE), Credit Guarantee Scheme, Credit Linked \nCapital Subsidy Scheme (CLCSS), Technology Centre Systems Programme (TCSP), \nMicro and Small Enterprises\n–\nCluster Development Programme (MSE\n-\nCDP), Procurement \nand Marketing Support Scheme etc. \n \n \n(c) & (d)\n:   In Make \n–\n \nI category of development of prototype of defence products/ \nplatforms, projects not ex\nceeding development cost of Rs.\n10 cr\nore and procurement cost \nof Rs.50 c\nr\nore\n \nare reserved for MSMEs. Similarly, under industry funded Make \n–\n \nII \ncategory of d\nevelopment of prototype of defence products/ platforms, projects not \nexceeding development cost o\nf Rs.\n3\n \ncrore and procurement cost of Rs.50 c\nr\nore\n \nare \nreserved for MSMEs. \n \n \n*****\n \n \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|3|UNSTARRED |75", "t": "Skill Development to Youth of Other Backward Class", "head": " \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF SKILL DEVELOPMENT AND ENTREPRENEURSHIP \n \nLOK SABHA \n \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 75 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 03.02.2020 \n \nSKILL DEVELOPMENT TO YOUTH OF OTHER BACKWARD CLASS \n \n75. SHRI RAJESH VERMA: \n \nWill the Minister of SKILL DEVELOPMENT AND ENTREPRE NEURSHIP be \npleased to state : \n \n(a) the number of youths of backward classes/extrem ely backward classes benefited \nunder the skill development scheme across the country during the last three years; \n \n(b) the details thereof along with the number of yo uths of backward classes/ \nextremely backward classes benefited under this sch eme particularly in Sitapur district \nof Uttar Pradesh; \n \n(c) the total number of skill development centres o pened so far in Uttar Pradesh; and \n \n(d) the details thereof, district-wise? \n \n     \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF  \nSKILL DEVELOPMENT AND ENTREPRENEURSHIP \n(SHRI R.K. SINGH) \n \n \n \n(a) Under Ministry of Skill Development and Entrepr eneurship (MSDE’s) flagship \nscheme Pradhan Mantri Kaushal Vikas Yojana (PMKVY 2 .0) 2016-20 as on 17 \nth  \nJanuary, 2020, more than 73 lakh candidates have be en trained across the country out \nof which more than 21 lakh candidates belong to OBC  category. Year wise details of \nnumber of OBC candidates trained/oriented, certifie d and placed under PMKVY are at \nAnnexure-I.  \n  \nFurther, in long term training imparted through Ind ustrial Training Institutes (ITIs), \nthere is a provision for reservation of seats for t raining of candidates belonging to \nOBCs. During last three years (2017-19), a total of  18,22,787 OBC candidates have \nbeen imparted training through ITIs in various trades across the country.  \n \n  \n \n\n \n \nMinistry of Social Justice and Empowerment through National Backward Classes \nFinance and Development Corporation (NBCFDC) also f acilitates skill development \ntraining to its target group including OBCs. Year-w ise details of OBC candidates trained \nby Ministry of Social Justice are placed at Annexure-II. \n \n(b) Under PMKVY (2016-2020), as on 17 \nth  January, 2020, a total of 2848 OBC \ncandidates have been trained in Sitapur district of  Uttar Pradesh. Year wise details of \nnumber of OBC candidates trained/oriented, certifie d and placed under PMKVY in \nSitapur district of Uttar Pradesh are at Annexure-III. \n During last three years (2017-19), a total of 2610  OBC candidates have been \nimparted training through ITIs in Sitapur district of Uttar Pradesh. \n(c) & (d) Under PMKVY as on 17 th  January 2020, 1721 training centres were \nimparting training in the State of Uttar Pradesh. T here are 3229 ITIs in the State of Uttar \nPradesh. The district wise details of training centres and ITIs are at Annexure-IV.  \n  \n\nAnnexure-I \n  \nAnnexure referred to in reply to LSUQ No. 75 to be answered on 03.02.2020 asked \nby SHRI RAJESH VERMA regarding ‘SKILL DEVELOPMENT T O YOUTH OF \nOTHER BACKWARD CLASS’ \nPMKVY 2016-20-CSCM-OBC Progress as per SDMS Reports till 17-Jan-2020 \n \nFY    Trained / Oriented  Certified  Reported Placed  \nShort Term Training (STT) \nFY-16-17 15,714 3,090 86 \nFY-17-18 5,75,467 4,21,818 1,60,449 \nFY-18-19 3,02,164 3,12,836 2,31,984 \nFY-19-20 2,96,385 2,42,906 1,44,417 \nSTT-Total 11,89,730 9,80,650 5,36,936 \nSpecial Project (SP) \nFY-16-17 313 22 0 \nFY-17-18 7,499 4,546 1,932 \nFY-18-19 13,708 9,637 4,965 \nFY-19-20 19,814 15,177 6,837 \nSPL-Total 41,334 29,382 13,734 \nRecognition of Prior Learning (RPL) \nFY-16-17 80,649 37,169 NA \nFY-17-18 1,70,515 1,51,690 NA \nFY-18-19 2,69,901 1,49,439 NA \nFY-19-20 3,68,679 3,56,016 NA \nRPL -Total  8,89,744  6,94,314  NA  \nGrand Total 21,20,808 17,04,346 5,50,670 \n  \n  \n\nAnnexure-II  \n \nAnnexure referred to in reply to LSUQ No. 75 to be answered on 03.02.2020 asked \nby SHRI RAJESH VERMA regarding ‘SKILL DEVELOPMENT T O YOUTH OF \nOTHER BACKWARD CLASS’ \n \nDetails of training of OBC candidates done by Ministry of Social Justice and \nEmpowerment   \nYear No. of trainees \n2016-17 18748 \n2017-18 23380 \n2018-19 25040 \n2019-20 33149 \nTotal 100317 \n  \n\nAnnexure-III \n \nAnnexure referred to in reply to LSUQ No. 75 to be answered on 03.02.2020 asked \nby SHRI RAJESH VERMA regarding ‘SKILL DEVELOPMENT T O YOUTH OF \nOTHER BACKWARD CLASS’ \nPMKVY 2016-20-CSCM-OBC Progress in Sitapur District as per SDMS \nReports till 17-Jan-2020 \n \nFY  Trained / Oriented  Certified  Reported Placed  \nShort Term Training (STT)  \nFY-16-17 0 0 0 \nFY-17-18 1,273 969 443 \nFY-18-19 553 640 525 \nFY-19-20 613 386 206 \nSTT-Total 2,439 1,995 1,174 \nSpecial Project (SP)  \nFY-16-17 0 0 0 \nFY-17-18 0 0 0 \nFY-18-19 0 0 0 \nFY-19-20 44 42 0 \nSPL-Total 44 42 0 \nRecognition of Prior Learning (RPL)  \nFY-16-17 0 0 NA \nFY-17-18 0 0 NA \nFY-18-19 317 143 NA \nFY-19-20 48 86 NA \nRPL -Total  365  229  0 \nGrand Total 2,848 2,266 1,174 \n \n \n \n \n  \n\nAnnexure-IV \n \nAnnexure referred to in reply to LSUQ No. 75 to be answered on 03.02.2020 asked \nby SHRI RAJESH VERMA regarding ‘SKILL DEVELOPMENT T O YOUTH OF \nOTHER BACKWARD CLASS’ \nSkill Development Centre"}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|3|UNSTARRED |750", "t": "Action Plan for Floods", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA\nMINISTRY OF JAL SHAKTI,\nDEPARTMENT OF WATER RESOURCES, RIVER DEVELOPMENT & GANGA REJUVENATION\nLOK SABHA\nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. †750\nANSWERED ON 06.02.2020\nACTION PLAN FOR FLOODS\n†750. SHRI LALLU SINGH\nSHRI ANIL FIROJIYA\nWill the Minister of JAL SHAKTI be pleased to state:\n(a) whether the Union Government in collaboration with State Governments is considering to formulate\nany action plan to overcome the danger of floods and prevent scams in relief and rescue measures tak en in\nflood affected areas;\n(b) if so, the details thereof; and\n(c) the time by which the above scheme is likely to be implemented to contain loss of life and property?\nANSWER\nTHE MINISTER OF STATE FOR JAL SHAKTI & SOCIAL JUSTICE AND EMPOWERMENT\n(SHRI RATTAN LAL KATARIA)\n(a) to (c) Flood management schemes are formulated and implemented by concerned State Governments\nas per their priority. The Union Government supplements the efforts of the States by providing technical\nguidance and promotional financial assistance for management of floods in critical areas.\nThe Government of India has been making regular efforts to assist the State Governments in\neffective flood management and it had taken several important actions, including setting up of Vario us\nExpert Committees / Task Forces/ Commissions from time to time, which studied the flood problems and\nrecommended suitable flood management measures for implementation by the State Governments. A gist\nof important initiatives is as under:\n(i) Ganga Flood Control Commission (GFCC) was set up at Patna in 1972 to address the flood and\nerosion problems of Ganga basin States. It has prepared 23 Comprehensive Master Plans for the\nGanga Basin States and circulated them to concerned States for implementation.\n(ii) Rashtriya Barh Ayog (RBA) was set up in 1976 which submitted its report in 1980 recommending\nvarious measures of flood control.\n(iii) Brahmaputra Board was set up in 1980 to address the flood erosion problems in the North Eastern\nStates including North Be ngal and Sikkim. It has prepared 57 Master Plans and circulated them to\nconcerned States for implementation.\nContd…P/2\n\n//2//\n(iv) The Task Force -2004 on Flood Management / Erosion Control, set up in August, 2004, submitted\nits Report in December, 2004 recommending immediate, short -term and long -term measures for\nflood management and erosion control in the States of Assam & neighbouring North-Eastern States,\nBihar, West Bengal, Eastern Uttar Pradesh.\n(v) The Government of India issued National Water Policy -2012 which emphasizes construction of\nlarge storage reservoirs and other non-structural measures for integrated flood management.\n(vi) The Government of India is having continuous dialogue with neighbouring countries viz. Nepal,\nBhutan, Bangladesh and China on flood management aspects in India, besides other issues related\nto water resources.\n(vii) As a non-structural measure, Central Water Commission (CWC) has established a flood forecasting\nnetwork across the country and issues flood forecasts at 325 stations. The level forecasts help the\nuser agencies in deciding mitigating measures like evacuatio n of people and shifting people and\ntheir movable property to safer locations. The Inflow Forecasting is used by various dam authorities\nin optimum operation of reservoirs for safe passage of flood downstream as well as to ensure\nadequate storage in the reservoirs for meeting demand during non-monsoon period.\n(viii) Government of India launched Flood Management Programme (FMP) during XI Plan for providing\ncentral assistance to the State Governments for taking up works related to river management, flood\ncontrol, anti-erosion, drainage development, flood proofing works, restoration of damaged flood\nmanagement works and anti-sea erosion works which was continued during XII Plan. A total of 522\nprojects costing Rs.13238.36 crore were approved and included under FMP. Since start of XI Plan,\ntotal Central Assistance released to States inder FMP up to till March, 2019 is Rs.5863.95 crore.\n(ix) During XII Plan, Government of India approved implementation of the Central Sector Scheme\n“River Management Activities and Works rel ated to Border Areas (RMBA)” for carrying out\nactivities relating to investigation of projects, flood forecasting, flood management works on\ncommon/border rivers with neighbouring countries.\n(x) FMP and RMBA schemes have been merged into a single scheme \"Floo d Management and Border\nAreas Programme\" (FMBAP) for the three year period from 2017 -18 to 2019-20 with an outlay of\nRs.3342 Crore. Since start of XI Plan, total Central Assistance released to States under FMP up till\nMarch, 2019 is Rs.5863.95 crore.\n*****"}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|3|UNSTARRED |752", "t": "Piped Water Supply", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF JAL SHAKTI \nDEPARTMENT OF DRINKING WATER AND SANITATION \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 752 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 06.02.2020 \n \nPiped Water Supply \n \n752: ADV. ADOOR PRAKASH: \n Will the Minister of JAL SHAKTI be pleased to stat e: \n(a) whether the Union Government has received a proposa l from the State \nGovernment of Kerala seeking financial assistance f or providing piped water \nsupply in the State;  \n \n(b) if so, the details thereof;  \n \n(c) whether any action has been taken by the Union Government on the same; and \n \n(d) if so, the details and the present status thereof? \n \n \n \nANSWER  \nMINISTER OF STATE FOR JAL SHAKTI  \n(SHRI RATTAN LAL KATARIA) \n \n \n (a) & (b) Government of Kerala has requested for c hanging funding pattern \nunder Jal Jeevan Mission (JJM) for the flood-affect ed nine districts of Kerala i.e. \nPathanamthitta, Kottayam, Alappuzha, Idukki, Thriss ur, Ernakulam, Malappuram, \nWayanad and Palakkad at 90:10 cost sharing basis be tween Centre and State. The \nfund under JJM is allocated among the States/ UTs as per the approved criteria.  \n \n(c) & (d)  The State has been suitably replied in t his regard. During 2019-20, \nunder JJM, Kerala has been allocated Rs. 248.76 Cro re, out of which Rs. 101.29 \nCrore has already been released in August 2019. As reported by the State, as on \n29.01.2020, an amount of Rs. 78.44 Crore is still l ying unspent with the State. Due to \nslow pace of implementation and resultant poor util ization of funds, this Department \nis yet to receive the proposal for release of 2 nd  instalment amounting Rs. 147.48 \nCrore. Government of Kerala has been requested to e xpedite expenditure and send \nproposal for release of 2 nd  instalment. "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|3|UNSTARRED |753", "t": "DDUGJY", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF POWER \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.753 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 06.02.2020 \n \nDDUGJY \n \n753. SHRI SHANTANU THAKUR: \nSHRI PRADEEP KUMAR SINGH: \nSHRI KAPIL MORESHWAR PATIL: \nSHRIMATI RATHVA GITABEN VAJESINGBHAI: \nSHRI NARANBHAI KACHHADIYA: \nSHRI PARBATBHAI SAVABHAI PATEL: \n \n Will the Minister of POWER \nbe pleased to state: \n \n(a)  whether some States are lagging behind in rural electrification under the \nDeen Dayal Upadhyaya Gram Jyoti Yojana (DDUGJY) and if so, the details thereof \nand the reasons therefor; \n \n(b)  the details of the villages partially electrified and fully electrified so far  \nunder the yojana along with the details of the villages that are yet to be covered, \nState/UT-wise; \n \n(c)  the target fixed for implementation of the said yojana  in various States and \nthe target achieved so far along with the reasons for not achieving the targets, \nState/UT-wise; \n \n(d)  the details of the proposals received from various States/UTs for loans \n/grants under the said yojana during the 11th and 12th five year plan periods; \n \n(e)  the details of the proposal taken up by the Rural Electrification Corporation \n(REC) for electrification of rural areas in each State and the amount sanctioned \nand spent so far during the 11th and 12th Five Year Plan periods; and \n \n(f)  whether households belonging to BPL, SC and ST categories are being given \nelectricity connection under DDUGJY and if so, the details thereof during the last \ntwo years, State/UT-wise? \nA N S W E R \n \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE (INDEPENDENT CHARGE) FOR POWER, NEW & RENEWABLE \nENERGY AND THE MINISTER OF STATE FOR SKILL DEVELOPMENT & \nENTREPRENEURSHIP \n \n( SHRI  R.K. SINGH)  \n \n(a):  As reported by states, 73% of works under Deen Dayal Upadhyaya Gram \nJyoti Yojana (DDUGJY) have already been completed. \n…………2. \n\n \n- 2 - \n \n(b) & (c) : As reported by the States, all the inhabited census villages across the \ncountry stand electrified on 28.04.2018.  \n \n(d) & (e) : Under the then prevalent scheme Rajiv Gandhi Grameen  Vidyutikaran \nYojana (RGGVY) which was later subsumed in DDUGJY, based on projects proposal \nreceived from states and techno -economic appraisal done by the nodal agency, \nduring the XI Plan, 570 projects of Rs.28,869 crore were sanctioned and a Grant of \nRs 2 2,854.20  crore had been released against these projects as on 31.12.2019. \nDuring the XII Plan, 560 projects of Rs.23,735.60 crore had been sanctioned and a \nGrant of Rs15,950.20 crore had been released against these projects as on \n31.12.2019. State-wise details of project cost sanctioned and Grant released during \nXI & XII Plan are given at Annexure-I. \n \n(f) :  Under DDUGJY, free electricity service connections to 36,52,714 and \n54,33,999 Below Poverty Line (BPL) households, including SC & ST categories, were \nprovided during the last two years i.e. 2017 -18 & 2018 -19 respectively. The State -\nwise details are given at Annexure-II.  \n \n************* \n  \n\n \n \nANNEXURE-I \n \nANNEXURE REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PARTS ( d) & (e) OF UNSTARRED QUESTION NO.  \n753 TO BE ANSWERED IN THE LOK SABHA ON 06.02.2020. \n*************** \n \n Projects sanctioned under DDUGJY (including RE component) during XI & XII Plan and \nGrant released. \nAs on 31.12.2019 (Rs. in Crore) \nSr. No. State XI Plan  XII Plan \nNo.of projects Project  \nCost \nGrant \nReleased \nNo.of \nprojects \nProject \nCost \nGrant \nReleased \n1 Andhra Pradesh 61 112.7 99.8 205 49.1 27.8 \n2 Arunachal Pradesh  14 953.9 845.7 0 0 0 \n3 Assam 20 2424.5 2203.4 16 1621.0 1158.8 \n4 Bihar 28 5076.6 3560.1 27 5251.5 3019.4 \n5 Chhattisgarh  24 1097.3 874.4 28 310.8 223.5 \n6 Gujarat  22 250.0 224.1 0 0 0 \n7 Haryana  17 134.3 104.2 0 0 0 \n8 Himachal Pradesh  11 258.8 222.3 0 0 0 \n9 J&K 9 351.1 294.0 3 101.2 27.0 \n10 Jharkhand 9 1524.8 1269.03 17 1260.9 922.1 \n11 Karnataka   10 365.5 331.1 45 130 53.9 \n12 Kerala  13 179.3 160.1 15 5.3 1.24 \n13 Ladakh 2 475.8 367.5 0 0 0 \n14 Madhya Pradesh 69 2385.6 1768.8 34 1427.3 941.7 \n15 Maharashtra  31 594.2 521.3 0 0 0 \n16 Manipur   7 349.2 262.7 6 222.1 130.1 \n17 Meghalaya 5 402.4 342.5 0 0 0 \n18 Mizoram  6 181.2 162.6 8 74.0 58.4 \n19 Nagaland    9 226.0 201.5 11 92.0 54.7 \n20 Odisha  28 3133.8 2720.3 38 3548.7 2589.5 \n21 Punjab  17 34.9 51.43 0 0 0 \n22 Rajasthan   15 732.3 642.5 28 1453.1 1062.1 \n23 Sikkim   2 121.7 105.6 0 0 0 \n24 Tamil Nadu  29 365.3 319.2 0 0 0 \n25 Telangana 44 117.5 92.8 0 0 0 \n26 Tripura   3 141 127 8 313.6 246.1 \n27 Uttar Pradesh  47 4541.6 3105.7 64 7266.0 4989.5 \n28 Uttarakhand 2 5.0 3.3 0 0 0 \n29 West Bengal  16 2274.1 1870.1 7 607.6 443.6 \nTotal 570 28811.50 22854.20 560 23735.60 15950.20 \n \n*************** \n  \n\n \n \nANNEXURE-II \n \nANNEXURE REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PART (f) OF UNSTARRED QUESTION NO. \n753 TO BE ANSWERED IN THE LOK SABHA ON 06.02.2020. \n*************** \n \nFree electricity service connections provided to BPL Households in last two years \ni.e. 2017-18 and 2018-19 under DDUGJY.  \n    \nSI. No. Name of the State 2017-18 2018-19 \n1 Andhra Pradesh"}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|3|UNSTARRED |754", "t": "Work on NH-6 in Maharashtra", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF ROAD TRANSPORT AND HIGHWAYS \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 754 \nANSWERED ON 6TH  FEBRUARY, 2020 \n \nWORK ON NH-6 IN MAHARASHTRA \n \n754. SHRI PRATAPRAO JADHAV: \n \nWill the Minister of ROAD TRANSPORT AND HIGHWAYS \nसड़क परिवहन औि िाजमार्ग मंत्री \n \nbe pleased to state:  \n(a) the present status of construction of National Highway -6 from Pimprala at Khamgaon to \nChikhali Malkapur under Buldhana region in Maharashtra; \n \n(b) whether the work on the said NH has been held up and if so, the date since when the said \nconstruction work has remained closed alongwith the reasons therefor; and \n \n(c) the steps taken by the Government for timely completion of the said project? \n \n \nANSWER \n \nTHE MINISTER OF ROAD TRANSPORT AND HIGHWAYS \n \n(SHRI NITIN JAIRAM GADKARI) \n \n(a) to (c) National Highway -6 from Pimprala at Khamgaon to Chikhali Malkapur under \nBuldhana region in Maharashtra is part of Amravati - Chikhli section. Earlier, 4 -laning work of \nAmravati-Chikhli Stretch was implemented on BOT (Toll).  Appointed Date of the project was \ndeclared on 9.11.2016. The Concessionaire achieved physical progress of 22% upto July 2018. \nSubsequently the work was stopped due to the financial crisis with the Conces sionaire. \nTherefore, Concession Agreement was terminated on account of Concessionaire’s default on \n02.08.2019.  \nFor completing the balance work of the project, the bids have been received and evaluation of \nbids has been initiated. \n***** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|3|UNSTARRED |758", "t": "Water Issues", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA\nMINISTRY OF JAL SHAKTI,\nDEPARTMENT OF WATER RESOURCES, RIVER DEVELOPMENT & GANGA REJUVENATION\nLOK SABHA\nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 758\nANSWERED ON 06.02.2020\nWATER ISSUES\n758. SHRI KOMATI REDDY VENKAT REDDY\nSHRI Y.S. AVINASH REDDY\nWill the Minister of JAL SHAKTI be pleased to state:\n(a) whether the Union Government has recently convened a meeting with the officials of the Governments\nof Andhra Pradesh and Telangana to discuss the issues related to new water projects, working of K rishna\nRiver Management Board (KRMB) and sharing of Krishna River Water etc.;\n(b) if so, the details thereof; and\n(c) the details of the deliberations of the meeting and the steps being taken to resolve the problems between\nthe States?\nANSWER\nTHE MINISTER OF STATE FOR JAL SHAKTI & SOCIAL JUSTICE AND EMPOWERMENT\n(SHRI RATTAN LAL KATARIA)\n(a) to (c) A meeting was convened with the officials of the States of Andhra Pradesh and Telangana on\n21.01.2020 at New Delhi under the chairmanship of Secretary, Department of Water Resources, RD&GR,\nMinistry of Jal Shakti to discuss various issues of Krishna River Management Board (KRMB) and\nGodavari River Management Board (GRMB) including functionin g of KRMB and GRMB, regulation of\nsupply of water from projects and Jurisdiction of Boards.\n*****"}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|3|UNSTARRED |759", "t": "Sports Infrastructure in Rajasthan", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF YOUTH AFFAIRS & SPORTS \n(DEPARTMENT OF SPORTS) \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. †759 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 06.02.2020 \n \nSports Infrastructure in Rajasthan \n \n†759. SHRIMATI RANJEETA KOLI: \n \nWill the Minister of YOUTH AFFAIRS AND SPORTS be pleased to state: \n \n(a) whether the Government has made efforts to set up sports infrastructure \nin Rajasthan; \n \n(b) if so, the details thereof; and \n \n(c) the amount allocated so far for the said purpose  alongwith the details of \nthe infrastructure set up in this regard? \n \nANSWER \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE (INDEPENDENT CHARGE)  \nFOR YOUTH AFFAIRS & SPORTS  \n{ SHRI KIREN RIJIJU } \n \n(a) to (c) Sports being a State subject the primary responsibility of \ndevelopment of sports related infrastructure  rests with the concerned \nState/Union Territory Government. The Central Government supplements \ntheir efforts by bridging the critical gaps. Under the Khelo India Scheme of \nthe Government of India, a total of 38 sports infrastructure projects have \nbeen sanctioned in the State o f Rajasthan so far. The details of the same \nare at Annexure-I. In addition to this, t he Government has also released Rs. \n618.82 lakh to Sports Authority of India in 2018 -19 for providing Sports \nequipment and creation of sports infrastructure in 20 schools / institutions \nin Jaipur (Rural).  \n\n \nANNEXURE REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PARTS (a) TO (c) OF LOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. †759 TO BE ANSWERED ON 06.02.2020 \nREGARDING “SPORTS INFRASTRUCTURE IN RAJASTHAN ” ASKED BY \nSHRIMATI RANJEETA KOLI, HON’BLE MEMBER OF LOK SABHA \n \nANNEXURE - I \n \nDETAILS OF SPORTS INFRASTRUCTURE PROJECTS SANCTIONED IN RAJASTHAN UNDER KHELO \nINDIA SCHEME \n(Amount in Rs. lakhs) \nS. \nNo. \nName of Project Amount \nSanctioned \nDate of \nSanction \nAmount \nReleased \nTill Date \n% of Work \nCompleted \n1.  Work of Synth etic Flooring and 100 lux \nlighting at Multipurpose Indoor Hall at SAI \nSTC Jaipur \n99.00 28.07.2017 99.00 Completed \n2.  Construction of Multipurpose Indoor Hall, \nFootball Ground and Athletic Track at LBS \nGovt. PG College Kotputli \n312.00 06.03.2017 312.00 95% \n3.  Construction of Multipurpose Indoor Hall at \nGovt. Senior Secondary School, \nJamwaRamgarh \n294.00 28.09.2017 150.00 80% \n4.  Construction of Multipurpose Indoor Hall at \nGovernment Senior Secondary School \nPragpura District Jaipur \n299.00 29.09.2017 290.00 65% \n5.  Laying of Synthetic Athletic Track in District \nStadium at Churu \n630.00 05.04.2017 400.00 Completed \n6.  Laying of Synthetic Hockey Field at \nMaharanaPratapKheloGaon at Udaipur \n550.00 28.07.2017 550.00 Completed \n7.  Construction of Multi -purpose Indoor Hall at \nfive block Headquarters;- \n(i) Govt. Secondary School, Bapini, Jodhpur, \n(ii) Block Headquarter, Khinwsar, Nagaur,  \n(iii) Sunhel, Jhalawar,  \n(iv) Block Headquarter, Jaitran, Pali and \n (v) Siswali, Anta, Baran \n400.00 11.08.2017 400.00 Completed \n8.  Laying of synthetic athletic track at Maharaja \nGanga Singh Stadium at Sri Ganganagar \n700.00 27.09.2017 700.00 Completed \n9.  Construction of Academy  Building & Solar \nLights at Deedwana Stadium \n100.00 05.01.2018 100.00 Completed \n10.  Construction of Basketball, Volleyball, Sol ar \nLight, Water Tank, 200 Mtr. Athletic Track at \nKhinwsar Stadium \n150.00 05.01.2018 150.00 90% \n11.  Laying of synthetic athletic track at \nSwarnjayanti Stadium, Jhunjhunu. \n700.00 08.03.2018 600.00 50% \n12.  Construction of multi -purpose indoor hall at \nSwarnjayanti Stadium, Jhunjhunu. \n800.00 13.03.2018 600.00 70% \n13.  Construction of Boundary wall, Basketball, \nVolleyball, Cricket Ground, Solar Lights, \nOffice Building Toilet Block at Dechu \nStadium, District Jodhpur \n150.00 28.03.2018 150.00 Completed \n14.  Construction of Bo undary Wall, Basketball, \nVolleyball, Cricket Ground, Solar Lights, \nOffice Building Toilet Block at Lohawat \nStadium, District Jodhpur \n150.00 28.03.2018 150.00 Completed \n15.  Construction of Boundary Wall, Basketball, \nVolleyball, Cricket Ground, Solar Lights, \nOffice Building Toilet Block at Bapini Stadium \nDistrict Jodhpur \n150.00 28.03.2018 150.00 Completed \n\nS. \nNo. \nName of Project Amount \nSanctioned \nDate of \nSanction \nAmount \nReleased \nTill Date \n% of Work \nCompleted \n \n \n16.  Construction of Indoor Hall & Ticket Block at \nSri Karnapur Stadium, District Sri \nGanaganagar \n100.00 28.03.2018 100.00 95% \n17.  Construction of Badmintion Are na with four \nChampionship Badminton courts with \ninfrastructure at SMS Stadium, Jaipur \n360.00 28.03.2018 360.00 55% \n18.  Construction of one all glass championship \ncourt at SMS Stadium, Jaipur \n150.00 28.03.2018 150.00 Completed \n19.  Completion of Shooting Range a t Bhilwara \nStadium \n100.00 28.03.2018 100.00 Completed \n20.  Construction of Multipurpose Hall at Rajiv \nGandhi Stadium, Hanumangarh \n399.00 30.07.2019 200.00 Nil \n21.  Construction of Multipurpose Hall at Sikar 399.00 30.07.2019 200.00 Nil \n22.  Development of Playfield  at Govt. Sr. Sec. \nSchool- Chandwaji \n885.00 28.09.2018 885.00 \n"}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|3|UNSTARRED |76", "t": "Review of Mid Day Meal Scheme", "head": " \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF HUMAN RESOURCE DEVELOPMENT \nDEPARTMENT OF SCHOOL EDUCATION & LITERACY \n \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 76 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 03.02.2020 \n \nReview of Mid Day Meal Scheme \n \n76. KUMARI SHOBHA KARANDLAJE: \n \nWill the Minister of HUMAN RESOURCE DEVELOPMENT be pleased to state: \n \n(a) whether the Government is reviewing the Mid Day Meal Scheme in terms of \nrevision of various components of the Scheme and if so, the details thereof;  \n \n(b) whether a Joint Review Mission has been started to review the present status of \nimplementation of the scheme and if so, the details thereof; \n \n(c) the number of children enrolled under MDMS database as on 31st December, 2019 \nalong with the number out of these, who had linked their Aadhaar numbers by \nOctober 31, 2019;  \n \n(d) the number of duplicate names deleted after Aadhaar authentication, as reported by \nStates as on 31.10.2019; and  \n \n(e) whether the Government has adopted any elaborate monitoring mechanism to ensure  \nquality food is served to children under the Scheme and if so, the details thereof? \n \n \nANSWER \nMINISTER OF HUMAN RESOURCE DEVELOPMENT \n (SHRI RAMESH POKHRIYAL ‘NISHANK’) \n  \n(a): As per the extant policy, all Ministries / Departments have been asked to undertake \nan outcome review of their ongoin g Schemes at the end of 12 th Five Year Plan for \nappraisal and further continuation.  Accordingly, the Government reviewed the \nMid-Day Meal Scheme (MDMS) in 2018 -19. The details of revisions/modifications \nin the scheme are given at Annexure-I. \n \n (b): Joint Review Missions (JRMs) under  the Mid-Day Meal Scheme were started in \n2009.   So far, 11 JRMs have been conducted  and 12th JRM is in process.  JRMs \nconsisting of educational and nutritional experts, review the Scheme through field \nvisits from time to time.   \n\n \n   JRMs review various aspects of the implementation of the Scheme.  The reports of \nJRMs are shared with concerned States and UTs for taking suitable action on the \nfindings and recommendations.   \n(c) & (d):  As on 31st December, 2019  the number of children enrolled under MDMS \ndatabase is 11,60,05,995. \nAs per information reported by States and UTs (except J&K, Ladakh, Assam & \nMeghalaya which are exempted), out of 11, 02,24,554 enrolled students in \nElementary Level (Classes I -VIII) 9, 67,52,617  (88%) students have Aadhaar \nnumber as on 31. 10.2019. The Sta tes and UTs also reported that 6,40 ,435  \nproxy/duplicate names have been deleted after Aadhaar authentication. \n \n(e): The Government has adopted an elaborate monitoring mechanism at Central,  State \nand District levels to ensure quality food is served to children under the Scheme. At \nnational level, an Empowered Committee, headed by Minister of Human Resource \nDevelopment and also a National level Steering -cum-Monitoring Committee \n(NSMC) as well  as Programme Approval Board (PAB) monitor the scheme and \nsuggest measures for its smooth and effective implementation.  \n \nAt the State level, a State level Steering–cum-Monitoring Committee headed by the \nState Chief Secretary and, at the District Level, a District Level Committee under \nthe Chairpersonship of the senior -most Member of Parliament of Lok Sabha from \nthe district monitors the implementation of the scheme.   \nAt local level Gram Panchayats/Gram Sabhas, members of Village Education \nCommittees (VECs ), Parent -Teacher Associations (PTAs) and the School \nManagement Committees (SMCs) monitor the regularity and wholesomeness of the \nmid-day meal served to children, cleanliness in cooking and serving of the meal, \ntimeliness in procurement of good quality ing redients, fuel, etc., implementation of \nvariety in menu so as to make it attractive to children and ensuring social and \ngender equity on daily basis . In addition to directing States and UTs to carry out \nSocial Audit, the Centre constitutes Joint Review Mis sions (JRMs) to review the \nscheme through field visits from time to time.   \n \n******* \n \n\nAnnexure-I \n \nAnnexure referred to in reply to part (a) of Lok Sabha Unstarred Question No. 76 for \n03.02.2020 raised by Kumari Shobha Karandlaje  regarding Review of Mid Day Meal \nScheme \n \nDetails of revision / modification of Centrally Sponsored National Programme of Mid \nDay Meal in Schools \n \nThe Central Government has approved the revisions/modifications of norms for the following \ncomponents under the Centrally Sponsored National Programme for Mid -Day Meal in \nSchools (NP-MDMS): \n \n  a Continuation of existing components. \ni Cost of food grains @ National Food Security Act (NFSA) rates. \nii Honorarium to Cook-cum helpers \niii Construction of kitchen-cum-stores \niv Provision of MDM during summer vacations in drought affected areas. \n   b Continuation and Revision of the norms of the following existing approved components \nv Revision of cooking cost annually in April on the basis of the Consumer Price Index (CPI). \nvi Revision of the transportation rate for non-special cat"}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|3|UNSTARRED |760", "t": "Haj Quota for MPs", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF MINORITY AFFAIRS \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION No.760 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 06.02.2020 \n \nHAJ QUOTA FOR MPs \n \n760.  SHRI DHARAMBIR SINGH: \n \nWill the Minister of MINORITY AFFAIRS be pleased to state: \n \n(a) whether the Government has any scheme to allot Haj quota to all the Members of \nParliament;  \n \n(b) if so, the details thereof and the date from which this scheme is being implemented ; \nand  \n \n(c) if not, whether the Government proposes to fix a quota of at least 10 Haj pilgrims to a \nMember of Parliament and if so, the details thereof? \n \n \n \nANSWER \n \n \nMINISTER OF MINORITY AFFAIRS \n(SHRI MUKHTAR ABBAS NAQVI) \n \n \n(a)  No, Sir.  \n \n(b) & (c) Does not arise in view of (a) above. \n \n***** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|3|UNSTARRED |761", "t": "Works under ABY in Maharashtra and Rajasthan", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA\nMINISTRY OF JAL SHAKTI,\nDEPARTMENT OF WATER RESOURCES, RIVER DEVELOPMENT & GANGA REJUVENATION\nLOK SABHA\nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. †761\nANSWERED ON 06.02.2020\nWORKS UNDER ABY IN MAHARASHTRA AND RAJASTHAN\n†761. SHRI DEVJI M. PATEL\nSHRI SUDHAKAR TUKARAM SHRANGARE\nWill the Minister of JAL SHAKTI be pleased to state:\n(a) whether the Government has launched Atal Bhujal Yojana (ABY) in view of the problem of drinking\nwater and irrigation in many parts of the country;\n(b) if so, details of the objectives and targets of the said scheme;\n(c) the names of States and districts across the country which have been assimilated thereunder along with\nthe funds allocated and works to be executed under the scheme and the methods of executing the same;\n(d) whether Jalore Sirohi, dark zone area of Rajasthan along with the most draught affected area of the\ncountry like Latur district of Marathwada in Maharashtra have been included under the said scheme;\n(e) if so, the number of works reco mmended to be done in Jalore Sirohi district of Rajasthan under the\nsaid scheme along with the quantum of funds spent so far on the same; and\n(f) the works likely to be executed in Latur district under this Scheme along with the details thereof?\nANSWER\nTHE MINISTER OF STATE FOR JAL SHAKTI & SOCIAL JUSTICE AND EMPOWERMENT\n(SHRI RATTAN LAL KATARIA)\n(a) to (f) Government of India has approved Atal Bhujal Yojana (Atal Jal), a Rs. 6000 Crore Central\nSector Scheme, for sustainable management of ground water resources with community participation in\nwater stressed blocks of Gujarat, Haryana, Karnataka, Madhya Pradesh, Maharashtra, Rajasthan and Uttar\nPradesh. The scheme is being implemented dur ing 2020 -21 to 2024-25 with the financial assistance of\nWorld Bank with a sharing pattern of 50:50 between Government of India and World Bank.\nThe implementation of the scheme is likely to have several positive outcomes including better\nunderstanding of t he groundwater regime, focused and integrated community based approach for\naddressing groundwater depletion issues, sustainable groundwater management through convergence of\nongoing/new schemes, adoption of efficient water use practices for irrigation, aug mentation of\ngroundwater resources in targeted areas.\nNames of States and Districts identified for implementation of the scheme along· with the\ntentative fund allocations are given at Annexure I.\n*****\n\nANNEXURE\nAnnexure referred to in reply to part (a) to (f) of Unstarred Question No. †761 to be answered in\nLok Sabha on 06.02.2020 regarding “ WORKS UNDER ABY IN M AHARASHTRA AND\nRAJASTHAN”.\nNames of States and Districts identified for implementation of the Atal Jal.\nS.\nNo.\nState Districts Tentative\nAllocation\n(Rs. in Crore)\n1 Haryana Bhiwani, Fatehabad, Gurugram, Kaithal\nKurukshetra, Karnal, Panipat, Sirsa, Faridabad,\nMahendragarh, Rewari, Palwal, Yamuna Nagar\n723.19\n2 Gujarat Ahmedabad, Banaskantha, Gandhinagar,\nKachchh, Mehsana, Patan\n756.76\n3 Karnataka Kolar, Chikaballapur, Tumkur, Bangalore Rural,\nRamnagara, Chikkamagalur, Chitradurga,\nDavangere, Bellary, Bagalkot,\nGadag, Belgaum, Chamrajnagar, Hassan\n1201.52\n4 Maharashtra Pune, Satara, Sangli, Solapur, Jalna, Osmanabad,\nLatur, Buldhana, Amravati, Ahmednagar, Nashik,\nJalgaon, Nagpur\n925.77\n5 Rajasthan Rajasmand, Chittorgarh, Jhalawar, Baran, Kota,\nSawai Madhopur, Bhilawara, Ajmer, Dholpur,\nDausa, Alwar, Jaipur, Sikar, Jhunjhunu,\nJaisalmer, Karauli, Hanumangarh\n1189.65\n6 Uttar Pradesh Mahoba, Jhansi, Banda, Hamirpur, Chitrakoot,\nLalitpur, Muzaffarnagar, Shamli, Baghpat,\nMeerut\n729.24\n7 Madhya Pradesh Chhatarpur, Sagar, Tikamgarh, Damoh, Panna 314.54\n*****"}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|3|UNSTARRED |762", "t": "Redevelopment of Central Vista", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF HOUSING AND URBAN AFFAIRS \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION No. 762 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON FEBRUARY 6, 2020 \n \nREDEVELOPMENT OF CENTRAL VISTA \n \nNo. 762 .         PROF. SAUGATA RAY: \nWill the Minister of HOUSING AND URBAN AFFAIRS be p leased to \nstate: \n(a)   the details of redevelopment of Central Vista;  \n(b)  the details of the company which has been awar ded the work;   \nand \n(c) whether the Government has any proposal to rebu ild the \nGovernment quarters in Delhi and if so, the details thereof?  \n \nANSWER \n \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE (INDEPENDENT CHARGE) OF THE \nMINISTRY OF HOUSING AND URBAN AFFAIRS \n \n(SHRI HARDEEP SINGH PURI) \n (a) & (b)  Parliament building was completed in 19 27 and is not able to \naccommodate the present day requirements of the Par liament including \nproviding independent chambers to Members of Parlia ment. Offices of the \nCentral Government are located in different places impacting the \nefficiency of functioning of Ministries. Many of th ese buildings have \noutlived their lives. Therefore, in order to meet t he requirements of the \nParliament, get all offices of Central Government S ecretariat at one place \nand improve the Central vista as a world class publ ic place, the proposal \nof development/ redevelopment of such buildings of the vista has been \ndecided. \nThe work has been awarded to HCP Design Planning and Management Pvt. \nLtd. through a \nquality-cum-cost based (QCBS) tendering system followed \nby Central Public Works Department (CPWD). \n \n(c) Union Cabinet in July, 2016 has approved the re development of the \nseven Government colonies at Mohammadpur, Nauroji N agar, Netaji \nNagar, Sarojini Nagar, Thyagraj Nagar, Kasturba Nag ar and Srinivaspuri. \nThe project envisaged replacement of existing 12,97 0 dwelling units in \nthese colonies with newly built dwelling units in multistoryed buildings. \n***** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|3|UNSTARRED |763", "t": "Prime Minister’s 15 Point Programme", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF MINORITY AFFAIRS \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 763 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 06.02.2020 \n \nPRIME MINISTER’S 15 POINT PROGRAMME \n \n763 SHRI P. RAVEENDRANATH KUMAR: \nWill the Minister of MINORITY AFFAIRS be pleased to state:  \n(a)  the aims and objectives of Hon‟ble “Prime Minister‟s 15 Point Programme” being implemented \nfor the welfare of minorities in the country along with its implementation status in the State of \nTamil Nadu during the last six years and the current year; \n(b) whether the Union Government has evaluated the performance of the Programme and if so, \nthe details thereof; and \n(c) the achievements made under the programme during the said period? \n \nA N S W E R \nMINISTER OF MINORITY AFFAIRS \n(SHRI MUKHTAR ABBAS NAQVI) \n(a) to (c):  The Prime Minister‟s New 15 Point Programme (PM‟s New 15 PP)  for the Welfare of \nMinorities was launched in 2006 with the following broad objectives:  \n \n(i) Enhancing opportunities for education; \n(ii) Ensuring an equitable share for minorities in economic activities and  employment, \nthrough existing and new schemes, enhanced credit support for self -employment, and \nrecruitment to State and Central Government jobs; \n(iii) Improving the conditions of living of minorities by ensuring an appropriate share for them \nin infrastructure development schemes; and   \n(iv) Prevention and control of communal disharmony and violence. \n \nUnder the said Programme, various schemes/initia tives are implemented by  \nMinistries/Departments of the Central Government throughout the country, including Ministry \nof Minority Affairs, either exclusively or by earmarking of 15% of overall physical/financial \ntarget, wherever feasible, for the welfare of six (6) centrally notified minorities viz. Buddhists, \nChristians, Jains, Muslims, Parsis, and Sikhs as per details given below: \n \nI. Educational Empowerment \n(i) Scholarship Schemes - Pre-Matric Scholarship, Post -Matric Scholarship and Merit -\ncum-Means based Scholarship. \n\n(ii) Naya Savera – Free Coaching and Allied Scheme with the aim to enhance skills and \nknowledge of students and candidates from minority communities to get employment \nin Government Sector/ Public Sector Undertaking, jobs in private sector, and \nadmission in reputed institutions in technical and professional courses at under -\ngraduate and post-graduate levels. \n(iii) Maulana Azad National Fellowship Scheme, provides financial assistance to students \nfrom notified minority communities, to pursue higher education such as M.Phil and \nPh.D. \n(iv) In addition, the Maulana  Azad Education Foundation implements the following two \nschemes: \n(a) Begum Hazrat Mahal National Scholarship for meritorious girls belonging to \nminorities studying in Classes IX to XII. \n(b) Gharib Nawaz Employment Programme. \n \nII. Economic Empowerment \n(i) National Minorities Development Finance Corporation (NMDFC) Loan Schemes \nprovide concessional loans for self-employment and income generating activities for \nthe socio -economic development of the „backward sections‟ amongst the notified \nminorities. \n(ii) Priority Sector Lending by Banks.(Department of Financial Services) \n(iii) National Urban Livelihoods Mission (M/o Housing & Urban Affairs) \n(iv) National Rural Livelihoods Mission (M/o Rural Development) \n(v) Deen Dayal Upadhyay – Gramin Kaushal Yojana (M/o Rural Development) \n(vi) Pradhan Mantri Awaas Yojana (Gramin)- (M/o Rural Development) \nAs per the guidelines fo r implementation of the PM‟s New 15 PP, review and monitori ng of the \nperformance of  the schemes is reviewed  with the Nodal Officers from all the \nMinistries/Departments concerned at least once every quarter and to obtain feedback and resolve \nproblems and pr ovide necessary clarifications. Further, the progress of implementatio n of the \nProgramme is monitored once in six months by a Committee of Secretaries and a report is also \nrequired to be submitted to the Union Cabinet.   \nBesides, evaluation of the schemes covered under the PM‟s New 15 PP is also carried out by the \nMinistries/Departments through independent agencies, from time to time. \nState-wise achievements (including that of Tamil Nadu) made under the programme are available \nat the Ministry‟s website www.minorityaffairs.gov.in \n \n***** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|3|UNSTARRED |764", "t": "Expansion of Grid Network", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF POWER \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.764 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 06.02.2020 \n \nEXPANSION OF GRID NETWORK \n \n764. ADV. DEAN KURIAKOSE: \n \n Will the Minister of POWER \nbe pleased to state: \n \n(a)  whether the Government proposes to expand the grid network in the \ncountry; \n \n(b)  if so, the details of the proposed projects thereof; \n \n(c)  the pro posed date of commissioning of Kudankulam -Thirunelveli-Idamon-\nKochi 400 KV line; \n \n(d)  the details of electricity allocated to each State including Kerala from this \nproject; and \n \n(e)  the details of total amount spent for this project? \n \nA N S W E R \n \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE (INDEPENDENT CHARGE) FOR POWER, NEW & \nRENEWABLE ENERGY AND THE MINISTER OF STATE FOR SKILL DEVELOPMENT & \nENTREPRENEURSHIP \n \n( SHRI  R.K. SINGH)  \n \n(a) & (b) : Yes, Sir.  The electricity grid network in the country is being expanded \non continuous basis  to take care of growth in electricity generation and load.  \n \nAs per Section 3 of the Electricity Act 2003, the Central Electricity \nAuthority (CEA) prepares National Electricity  Plan (NEP), which inter-alia includes \nrequired transmission system addition in the country. The NEP, Volume -II \n(Transmission) notified in January 2019 estimated the required transmission \nsystem addition for the period 2017 -22. As per this Plan, about 110, 000 ckm of \ntransmission lines, about 383,000 MVA of transformation capacity in the \nsubstations at 220 kV and above voltage levels and 14,000 MW of HVDC bi -\npole/back-to-back capacity are proposed to be added during the period 2017-22.   \n………..2. \n\n \n- 2 - \n \nTransmission lines of 4,21,244 ckm (voltage level of 220 kV and \nabove) and transformation capacity in substations of 9,52,713 MVA (voltage level \nof 220 kV and above) are in operation as on December, 2019, which is expected to \nincrease to 4,78,132 ckms and 11,0 4,955 MVA respectively by 2021 -22. Further, \n24,000 MW HVDC bi -pole/back-to-back capacity are in operation as on December, \n2019, which are expected to increase to 33,500 MW by 2021 -22. These include \nintra-state and inter -state transmission systems of 220 kV  and above voltage \nlevel. \n \nAlso, the cumulative Inter Regional (IR) transmission capacity of the \nNational Grid, which is presently about 1,00,550 MW, is expected to be enhanced \nto 1,18,050 MW by 2022. \n \n(c) :  Kudankulam – Tirunelveli, Tirunelveli – Edamon, Tirunelveli – Cochin \n400kV D/c lines were planned and implemented as part of Kudankulam Atomic \nPower Plant (KAPP)(2000 MW) Transmission System. The commissioning dates of \nthe lines are as below: \n \nKudankulam – Tirunelveli 400kV (Quad) 2xD/C lines – 01.04.2009 \nTirunelveli – Edamon 400kV D/c line (operating at 220kV) – 01.07.2010 \nTirunelveli – Cochin 400kV (Quad) Circuit-I & II – 27.09.2019 & 20.12.2019  \n \n(d) :  Power is not allocated to States from a specific transmission line. \nHowever, power is allocated from particular generating station, which is delivered \nto State through Inter State Transmission Line. \n \n(e) :  Total expected expenditure of the lines mentioned in para (c) above is \napprox. Rs. 1545.0 Crores. \n \n \n**************** \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|3|UNSTARRED |765", "t": "Houses Proposed under PMAY", "head": " \n \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF HOUSING AND URBAN AFFAIRS \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 765 \n \nTO BE ANSWERED ON FEBRUARY 06, 2020 \n \nHOUSES PROPOSED UNDER PMAY \n \n \nNo. 765. SHRI VINCENT H. PALA:  \n \nWill the Minister of HOUSING AND URBAN AFFAIRS be pleased to \nstate: \n \n(a)  the total number of houses proposed under each  of the phases \nof the Pradhan Mantri Awas Yojana-Urban and the tar get \nachieved; \n(b)  the total funds sanctioned during each of the phases and the \namount of funds released; \n(c) the total number of beneficiaries under each of  the PMAY \ncomponents, phase-wise; and \n(d)  the housing shortage in urban India and the pe rcentage of need \nmet since 2015? \nANSWER \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE (INDEPENDENT CHARGE) OF THE \nMINISTRY OF HOUSING AND URBAN AFFAIRS \n \n(SHRI HARDEEP SINGH PURI) \n \n \n `1  \n \n(a) to (d): In pursuance of the Government’s vision  of “Housing for \nAll” by 2022, Ministry of Housing and Urban Affairs  \n(MoHUA) is implementing Pradhan Mantri Awas Yojana \n(Urban) (PMAY(U) since 25.06.2015 for providing \nassistance to States/ Union Territories (UTs) for \naddressing the housing requirementin urban \nareas.States/UTs have undertaken demand survey unde r \nthe scheme for assessing actual demand of housing.  The \nvalidated demand reported by States/UTs so far is a round \n112 Lakh.   \n \n..2/- \n \n\n..2/- \n \nBased on the project proposals received so far from  the \nStates/UTsunder the Scheme, a total 1,03,22,560 hou ses have been \nsanctionedwhich is around 92% of the validated dema nd of houses \nreported by the States/UTs under the PMAY(U).Out of  the \nsanctioned houses, 60,50,991 are at various stages of construction \nand 32,07,573 are completed/delivered.  \n \n \nCentral Assistance of Rs. 1,63,160.86 crore has bee n approved; out \nof this, Rs. 63,676.50 crore have been released to the States/UTs so \nfar. \n \nThe number of houses sanctioned for beneficiaries u nder each \nvertical of the PMAY(U) is as under: \n \nVertical No of houses \nsanctioned \n \nIn-situ Slum Redevelopment (ISSR) 4.62  lakh  \nAffordable Housing in Partnership (AHP) 28.03  lakh  \nBeneficiary Led Individual House \nConstruction or Enhancement (BLC) \n \n62.15 lakh \nCredit Linked Subsidy Scheme (CLSS) 8.42  lakh  \n \n***** \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|3|UNSTARRED |766", "t": "Resettlement Policy Framework", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF ROAD TRANSPORT AND HIGHWAYS \nLOK SABHA  \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 766 \nANSWERED ON 06th February, 2020 \nRESETTLEMENT POLICY FRAMEWORK \n766. SHRI A. GANESHAMURTHI: \nWill the Minister of ROAD TRANSPORT AND HIGHWAYS \nसड़क परिवहन औि िाजमार्ग मंत्री \nbe pleased to state: \n(a) whether the Government has finalized a draft on resettlement policy framework including tribal \ndevelopment plan to help tribal people who are displaced by green national highways corridor \nprojects; \n(b) if so, the details and the objectives thereof; \n(c) the time by which the draft policy is likely to be ready and implemented; and \n(d) the total number of tribes likely to be benefited as a result of the proposed policy? \n \nANSWER \nTHE MINISTER OF ROAD TRANSPORT AND HIGHWAYS \n(SHRI NITIN JAIRAM GADKARI) \n(a) Ministry of Road Transport and Highways has envisaged a project namely, ‘Green National \nHighways Corridor Project (GNHCP)’ for up -gradation/development of 781.38 km length of \nNational Highways in four states of the country viz. Himachal Pradesh, Rajasthan, Uttar Pradesh and \nAndhra Pradesh. The draft Resettlement Policy Framework (RPF) including Tribal Development \nPlan will be applicable for the selected stretches proposed under GNHCP only. Out of these four \nstates, tribal people are affected only in the State of Andhra Pradesh.  \n(b) The objective  of this policy  is to design and implement projects in a way that fosters full \nrespect for all affected people including Indigenous Peoples’ dignity, human rights, and cultura l \nuniqueness. \n(c) to (d) The draft policy will be finalized after completion of on-going consultations/surveys carried \nout as per the provision s of Right to Fair Compensation and Transparency in Land Acquisition, \nRehabilitation and Resettlement (RFCTLARR) Act, 2013.  \n***** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|3|UNSTARRED |767", "t": "Setting up of MSME Units", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA\n \nMINISTRY OF MICRO, SMALL AND MEDIUM ENTERPRISES\n \n \nLOK SABHA\n \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 767\n \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 06.02.2020\n \n \nSETTING UP OF MSME UNITS\n \n \n  \n767.  \n \nSHRI KAUSHALENDRA KUMAR:\n \n \nSHRI ASHOK MAHADEORAO NETE:\n \n \nSHRI RAVINDRA KUSHWAHA:\n \n \nSHRI\n \nRAVI KISHAN:\n \n \nDR. NISHIKANT DUBEY:\n \n \nWill the Minister of MICRO, SMALL AND MEDIUM ENTERPRISES be pleased to \nstate: \n \n \n(a) \n \nwhether the Government has any proposal to set up Micro, Small and Medium \nEnterprises (MSMEs) units in most backward areas in the coun\ntry and specially in \nBihar State to provide permanent employment to the local youths; \n \n \n(b)\n \n \nif so, the details thereof and if not, the reasons therefor; \n \n \n(c) \n \nwhether the Government has decided to set up more MSME Units for overall \ndevelopment of the cou\nntry and if so, the details thereof, State/UT\n-\nwise; \n \n \n(d) \n \nthe details of the incentives being provided to enthusiastic entrepreneurs to set \nup MSMEs in the country; and \n \n \n(e) \n \nwhether\n \nthe MSME units have been provided with adequate capital investment \nand funds for their operations as per their demand and if so, the details thereof and if \nnot, the reasons therefor?\n \n \nANSWER\n \n \nMINISTER OF MICRO, SMALL AND MEDIUM ENTERPRISES\n \n(SHRI NITIN \nGADKARI)\n \n \n(a) to (c):\n \nMinistry of Micro, Small and Medium Enterprises (MSME) is \nimplementing Prime Minister’s Employment Generation Programme (PMEGP), \nwhich is a major credit\n-\nlinked subsidy programme aimed at generating self\n-\nemployment opportunities throug\nh establishment of micro\n-\nenterprises/units in the \nnon\n-\nfarm sector by helping traditional artisans and unemployed youth.\n \n \n \n \n \nState/UT\n-\nwise target and achievement under the PMEGP Scheme including \nBihar during the year 2019\n-\n20 (as on 21.01.2020) are given at \nAnnexure\n-\nI.\n \n \n \n \n \n\n \n \n \n: 2 :\n \n \n \n(d) & (e):\n \nUnder the scheme, loan is being provided by all Public Sector Banks, \nselected Private Sector Banks and Co\n-\noperative Banks with margin money subsidy by \nMinistry of MSME through KVIC. \n \n \n \nMargin Money subsidy to various \ncategories of beneficiaries is detailed below:\n \n \nProject upto Rs. 25 lakhs in the manufacturing sector and Rs. 10 lakhs\n \nin the \nservice sector are supported under PMEGP. Further, Ministry of MSME has also \nintroduced a component of second financial assistance for expansion/upgrading \nexisting PMEGP/MUDRA units with assistance up to Rs.1 crore for manufacturing \nunit and up to \nRs. 25 lakhs for service/ trading unit with 15% subsidy (20% for NER \nand hilly state) from the year 2018\n-\n19.\n \n \nIn addition, incentives are provided to entrepreneurs under the following schemes:\n \n \n(i)  \n \nUnder Credit Guarantee Scheme for Micro and Small Enterp\nrises (CGTMSE), \ncollateral free credit facility (term loan and/or working capital) is extended by \nlending institutions to new and existing micro and small enterprises. The \nguarantee cover provided is from 75% to 85% for credit up to Rs. 2 crore. \n \n(ii) \n \nUnd\ner Credit linked Capital Subsidy Scheme (CLCSS), 15% up front capital \nsubsidy (limited to maximum Rs. 15 lakhs) is provided on finance availed by \nentrepreneurs for technology upgradation of micro and small enterprises.   \n \n(iii) \n \nUnder Interest Subvention S\ncheme, 2% interest subvention on fresh or \nincremental loans maximum up to Rs. 1 crore are given to MSMEs who have \nvalid GSTN number and Udyog Aadhar number.     \n \n \n \n \n \n*******\n \n \n \n \n \nCategories of beneficiaries under \nPMEGP.\n \nBeneficiaries \ncontributions (of \nthe project cost)\n \nRate of subsidy.\n \n(of the project cost)\n \nArea \n \n(location of the project/ unit)\n \n \nUrban\n \nRural\n \nGeneral category.\n \n10%\n \n15%\n \n25%\n \nSpecial (including SC/ST/ OBC/ \nMinorities/Women, Ex\n-\nservicemen, Physically \nHandicapped, NER, Hill & Border \nAreas. \n \n05%\n \n25%\n \n35%\n \n\nAnnexure\n-\nI\n \n \n \n \nAnnexure I referred to in reply to part (a) to (c) of the Lok \nSabha Unstarred Qn. No. \n767 for answer on 06.02.2020\n \n \nTarget and Achievement under New PMEGP and 2\nnd\n \ndose for existing PMEGP units\n \nDuring 2019\n-\n20 (as on 21.01.2020)\n \n \n \n(Projects & Employment in Nos.)\n \n \n \nSr. \nNo.\n \nState/UT\n \nTarget 2019\n-\n20\n \nAchievement 2019\n-\n20\n \nNo. of \nprojects\n \nEstimated \nEmployment\n \nNo. of \nprojects\n \nEstimated \nEmployment\n \nI. NORTH ZONE\n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n1\n \nJammu & Kashmir\n \n2110\n \n16877\n \n2714\n \n21712\n \n2\n \nHim. Pradesh \n \n1181\n \n9449\n \n818\n \n6544\n \n3\n \nPunjab\n \n1983\n \n15860\n \n992\n \n7936\n \n4\n \nU.T. Chandigarh\n \n32\n \n255\n \n10\n \n80\n \n5\n \nHaryana\n \n2008\n \n16061\n \n1190\n \n9520\n \n6\n \nDelhi\n \n96\n \n764\n \n57\n \n456\n \n \n7\n \nRajasthan\n \n3398\n \n27184\n \n1619\n \n12952\n \n \n \nTotal \n-\nI\n \n10808\n \n86450\n \n7400\n \n59200\n \nII CENTRAL ZONE\n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n8\n \nUttarakhand\n \n1318\n \n10542\n \n986\n \n7888\n \n \n9\n \nUttar Pradesh\n \n8591\n \n70728\n \n2491\n \n19928\n \n10\n \nChhattisgarh\n \n2778\n \n22228\n \n1385\n \n11080\n \n11\n \nMadhya Pradesh\n \n5215\n \n41716\n \n958\n \n7664\n \n \n \nTotal \n-\nII\n \n17902\n \n145214\n \n5820\n \n46560\n \nIII. N.E.ZONE\n \n \n"}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|3|UNSTARRED |768", "t": "Implementation of Automatic Headlamp On (AHO) Rule", "head": " \n \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF ROAD TRANSPORT AND HIGHWAYS \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 768 \nANSWERED ON 6TH FEBRUARY, 2020 \n \nIMPLEMENTATION OF AUTOMATIC HEADLAMP ON (AHO) RULE \n \n768.  SHRIMATI RAKSHA NIKHIL KHADSE: \n  \n \nWill the Minister of ROAD TRANSPORT AND HIGHWAYS  \n \nlM+d ifjogu vkSj jktekxZ ea=h \nbe pleased to state: \n \n(a)  whether the Government has introduced BS4  standard for all two wheelers and to follow \nthe Automatic Headlamp On (AHO) rule in the country and if so, the details thereof; \n \n(b)  whether the Government is aware of any adverse  impact of keeping headlamp on during \nday time on the eyes of human being; \n \n(c)  if so, the details thereof; \n \n(d)  if not, whether the Government proposes to  conduct a study in this regard and if so, the \ndetails thereof; and \n \n(e)  whether the Government proposes to discontinue  the said AHO rule and if so, the details \nthereof? \n \nANSWER \n \nTHE MINISTER OF ROAD TRANSPORT AND HIGHWAYS \n \n(SHRI NITIN JAIRAM GADKARI) \n \n(a) The Ministry  of Road Transport and Highways,  vide GSR 431 dated 4 th July, 2014 has \nmandated that the mass emission standards (Bharat Stage IV) shall come into force for two \nwheelers manufactured on and  after the 1 st April, 2016 for new types of vehicle models and from  \nthe 1st April, 2017 for existing types of vehicle models. Further, this Ministry vide GSR 188 dated \n22nd February, 2016 July has mandated to follow the Automatic Headlamp On (AHO ) rule in the \ncountry. It has been mandated that every two wheeler manufactured on and after the 1st April, \n2017, shall have one or  two head lamps, conforming to the applicable standards in force for \nperformance and  installation requirements as notified by the Central Government from time to \ntime vide according to sub -rule (1) of rule 124, which shall automatically switch on when  the \nengine is running. \n \n(b) to (e) No such information is available. \n  \n \n***** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|3|UNSTARRED |769", "t": "National Highways in Tamil Nadu", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF ROAD TRANSPORT AND HIGHWAYS  \n \nLOK SABHA  \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 769 \nANSWERED ON 06TH FEBRUARY, 2020 \n \nNATIONAL HIGHWAYS IN TAMIL NADU \n769.  DR. T.R. PAARIVENDHAR: \nWill the Minister of ROAD TRANSPORT AND HIGHWAYS \n \nसड़क परिवहन औि िाजमार्ग मंत्री \nbe pleased to state: \n \n(a)  the total number of National Highways (NHs)  passing through the State of Tamil Nadu \n(TN); \n \n(b) whether any four -lane NH has been constructed in the State including Perambalur  region \nduring the last five years and if so, the details thereof; \n \n(c)  whether there is any proposal for construction of new NHs in Musiri or Kulithalai in \nPerambalur region in TN; \n \n(d)  if so, the details and the present status thereof; and \n \n(e)  the funds allocated for construction of NHs in the State during the last three years? \n \nANSWER \n \nTHE MINISTER OF ROAD TRANSPORT AND HIGHWAYS \n(SHRI NITIN JAIRAM GADKARI) \n(a)  Forty nine National Highways pass through the State of Tamil Nadu.   \n(b) During the last f ive years , 300 Kms of various National Highway stretches  have been \nwidened to 4/6 lane standards in the State. \n(c) & (d)  Musri-Namakkal section of NH-381B from Km 60/0 to 103/700 (43.7 Km) and Trichy \n–Karur section of NH -81(new greenfield alignment for 70.3 Km) in Perambalur region is \nincluded for development under Bharatmala  Pariyojana by  National Highways Authority of \nIndia (NHAI).  \n(e)  During the last three years, an allocation of Rs 2210 Cr was made to the State PWD, Govt. \nof Tamil Nadu for development of various National Highways in the State. State -wise allocation \nof funds for development of National Highways are not made by the NHAI.  \n***** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|3|UNSTARRED |77", "t": "New Retail Fuel Policy", "head": "��������� �\n������������������������� �\n������������������� ����������������� �\n�\n�����������������������\n�\n����� ������������������ �\n������������������� �\n�������������� �\n��������������������������� �\n�\n���������� �������������������������������������������������\n�\n����� ���� ��������� ��� �������������� �������� ���� ��� �������� ���\n�������\n�\n���� ������������� ����������� ������������������������������� ������ ��������������������\n���������������������������������������\n�\n�����������������������������������������������������������������������������������\n�\n���������������������������������������������������������������������������������������\n�\n���� �������� ���� ����������� ���� ������ ���� ������ ����� ������� ��� ������� �������\n������ ������ ����� ������� ���� ��� ���� ���� �������� �������� ���� ��� ����� ���� ��������\n�����������\n�\n�������������������������������������������������������������������������������������\n���������������������������������������������������������������������������������\n�������������������\n�\n�����������������������������������������������������������������������������\n������������������������������������������������������������������������������������\n����������\n�\n������ �\n�\n���������� ���������������������������������������������������������������������������������\n�\n������������������������������������� �\n������������������������� �\n�\n�\n������������ ��������� ��� ���������� ���� �������� �������� ���� ����� �����������\n������ ��������� �������� ���� ����������� ���� �������������� ��� �������\n��������������� ������ ������ ����� ����� ���������� ��� ���� �������� ���\n�������������������������������������������������������������������\n���� ������ �������� �������� ��� ������� ��� ������� �������� ���� �����\n�������������������������������������������������������������������\n���������������������������������������\n�������"}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|3|UNSTARRED |770", "t": "Shortage of Water", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA\nMINISTRY OF JAL SHAKTI,\nDEPARTMENT OF WATER RESOURCES, RIVER DEVELOPMENT & GANGA REJUVENATION\nLOK SABHA\nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 770\nANSWERED ON 06.02.2020\nSHORTAGE OF WATER\n770. SHRI MANOJ KOTAK\nWill the Minister of JAL SHAKTI be pleased to state:\n(a) whether it is a fact that there has been acute shortage of water in many parts of the country especially\nin Maharashtra since the last five years and if so, the details thereof;\n(b) whether the Un ion Government has received any proposals from the State Governments including\nMaharashtra regarding financial assistance for providing water or to increase the water lavel;\n(c) if so, the action taken by the Union Government in this regard; and\n(d) the State-wise details of quantum of funds released during the last three years to resolve the water\nscarcity in the country?\nANSWER\nTHE MINISTER OF STATE FOR JAL SHAKTI & SOCIAL JUSTICE AND EMPOWERMENT\n(SHRI RATTAN LAL KATARIA)\n(a) The average annual water availability of any region or country is largely dependent upon hydro -\nmeteorological and geological factors. However, water availability per person is dependent on population\nof the country and for India, per capita water availability in the country is re ducing due to increase in\npopulation. Due to high temporal and spatial variation of precipitation, the water availability of many\nregions of the country is much below the national average and may result in water stress / scarce\nconditions.\n(b) to (d) Water being a State subject, steps for augmentation, conservation and efficient management of\nwater resources are primarily undertaken by the respective State Governments. In order to supplement the\nefforts of the State Governments, Central Government provides technical and financial assistance to them\nthrough various schemes and programmes.\nDuring 2016 -17, ninety -nine (99) on -going Major/Medium irrigation projects under Pradhan\nMantri Krishi Sinchai Yojana - Accelerated Irrigation Benefits Programme ( PMKSY-AIBP), having\nultimate irrigation potential of 76.03 lakh hectare, have been prioritized for completion in consultation\nwith States. Funding mechanism through NABARD has also been approved by the Government for both\nCentral and State share of all pri oritized projects. Central assistance is released from time to time as per\nrequirement.  Details of central assistance released for last 3 years and current year under PMKSY -AIBP\nis at Annexure-I.\nContd…P/2\n\n//2//\nFurther, a special package for completion of 8 Major and Medium Irrigation (MMI) and 83\nSurface Minor Irrigation (SMI) projects in drought prone districts in Vidarbha and Marathwada and rest of\nMaharashtra, having estimated balance cost of Rs.13,651.61 cro re as on 01.04.2018, has been approved\nduring 2018-19. Central assistance component amounts to Rs.3831.41 crore. Central assistance of Rs.800\ncrore [Rs.500 crore in 2018 -19 and Rs. 300 crore in 2019 -20 (so far)] has been released to the\nGovernment of Maharashtra under this package.\nDepartment of Land Resources, Ministry of Rural Development has sanctioned 8214 watershed\ndevelopment projects in states/UTs from 2009 -10 to 2014 -15 covering an area of about 39.07 million\nhectare under Integrated Watershed Mana gement Programme (IWMP) which was amalgamated as the\nWatershed Development Component (WDC) of PMKSY in 2015 -16. The activities being undertaken\nthrough the watershed development projects inter alia include ridge area treatment, drainage line\ntreatment, soil and moisture conservation, rain water harvesting, afforestation, horticulture, etc. The State\nwise details of funds released as Central share to States under WDC-PMKSY are given at Annexure-II.\nCommand Area Development (CAD) works through CADWM Programme of Government of\nIndia are being implemented with the main objective of taking up CAD works to enhance utilisation of\nirrigation potential created, and improve agriculture production on a sustainable basis. The programme has\nbeen brought under Pradhan Mantri Krishi Sinchai Yojna (PMKSY) 2015 -16 onwards. During 2016-17 to\n2019-20 (up to December, 2019), total Central Assistance amounting to Rs 2544.25 crore has been\nreleased. During 2016 -17 to 2019 -20 (up to December, 2019), central assistance amounting to Rs 73.77\ncrore has been released to the State of Maharashtra.\nDepartment of Agriculture Cooperation & Farmers Welfare is implementing Per Drop More\nCrop component of PMKSY which is operational fr om 2015 -16. The PMKSY - Per Drop More Crop\n(PDMC) mainly focuses on enhancing water use efficiency at farm level through precision/micro\nirrigation (Drip and Sprinkler Irrigation). Besides promoting precision irrigation and better on -farm water\nmanagement p ractices to optimize the use of available water resources, this component also supports\nmicro level water storage or water conservation/management activities to supplement Micro Irrigation.\nState wise funds released for micro irrigation under PDMC -PMKSY fr om 2015 -16 to 20"}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|3|UNSTARRED |771", "t": "Ship Breaking Industry", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA\nMINISTRY OF SHIPPING\nLOK SABHA\nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 771\nTO BE ANSWERED ON 6I'I FEBRUARY, 2O2O\nSHIP BREAKING INDUSTRY\n771. MS. LOCKET CHATTERJEE:\nWill the Minister of SHIPPING be pleased to state:\n'n-d cft\"-aq rf*\n{a) whether the Government has taken any steps to promote ship breaking\ni;l;;i, '; \nthe countrv and if so the details thereof'\n(b) whether the Government has taken any steps to.attract the foreign ships in\niil;;;;i;y ind promote the industry in a eco-friendlv wav; and\n(c) if so, the details thereof?\nANSWER\nMl[IISTERoFSTATE(INDEPENDENTCHARGE)FoRSHIPPING\n(SHRi MANSUKH MANDAVIYA)\n(a) to (c) Yes Sir. lndia has acceded to the Hong Kong lnternational Convention\nfor safe and Environmentally sound Recycling of Ships' 2009 on 28 11 2019\nThe Recycling of Ships Act' 2019' which contains legal framework for\nimplementation of provisions of the Hong Kong Convention has been enacted'\nTheGujaratMaritimeBoardisalsoimplementingaproiectforupgradationofthe\nenvironment management plan at Alang-Sosiya ship recycling yard' which too\nwill facilitate environmentally sound and safe recycling of ships and thereby help\nto attract foreign ships for recycling in lndia"}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|3|UNSTARRED |774", "t": "Delay in Organising National Games", "head": "Government of India \nMinistry of Youth Affairs & Sports \nDepartment of Sports \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 774 \nTO BE ANSWERD ON 06.02.2020 \n \nDelay in Organising National Games \n \n774. MS. S. JOTHIMANI: \n \nWill the Minister of YOUTH AFFAIRS AND SPORTS be pleased to \nstate: \n \n(a) the reasons for various postponements and delay in organizing \nthe National Games in India;  \n \n(b) the problems faced by the States in hosting the National Games \nand reasons for cancellation thereof; \n \n(c) the details of schedules and h osts of the upcoming National \nGames in the next five years; \n \n(d) the measures being taken for the promotion of the National \nGames among athletes and spectators; and \n \n(e) the details of upcoming Government sports infrastructure \nprojects in Karur district of Tamil Nadu? \n \n \nANSWER \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE (INDEPENDENT CHARGE) \nFOR YOUTH AFFAIRS AND SPORTS \n[SHRI KIREN RIJIJU] \n \n \n(a) to (d ) National Games are organized by the Indian Olympic \nAssociation (IOA), IOA decides the venue and dates for the National \nGames.  The Central and State Government assist IOA for organizing \nthe National Games.  The IOA has not been able to organize the \nNational Games at regular intervals.  The need to organize National \nGames at reg ular intervals has been impressed upon the IOA.  The \n\nlast National Games were held in 2015 in Kerala.  The next Nation al \nGames are scheduled in Goa from 20 th October to 4 th November, \n2020.  IOA has allotted 37 th, 38 th and 39 th National Games to \nChhattisgarh, Uttarakhand and Meghalaya respectively.  This \nMinistry is organizing Khelo India Games since 2018.  The first \nKhelo India Games were held in Delhi from 31st  January to  8th \nFebruary 2018.  The second and third Khelo India Games were held \nat Pune from 9th January to 20th January, 2019 and at Guwahati from \n10th January to 22 nd January, 2020  respectively.  The Central \nGovernment is also starting annual Khelo India University Games to \npromote sport and a thletes. The first Khelo India University Games \nare scheduled at Cuttack and Bhubaneshwar from 22nd February to \n1st March, 2020.  \n(e)  No proposal for sports infrastructure in Karur district of T amil \nNadu is pending for consideration in this Ministry.  Sports being a \nState subject the primary responsibility for  sports infrastructure , \nincluding at district and sub district level, rests with the State \nGovernment.  \n \n***** \n \n \n \n \n \n \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|3|UNSTARRED |776", "t": "Cities Selected under SCM", "head": " \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF HOUSING AND URBAN AFFAIRS  \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION No.776 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON FEBRUARY 06, 2020 \n \nCITIES SELECTED UNDER SCM \n \nNo.776. SHRI RAJESHBHAI CHUDASAMA: \n \nWill the Minister of HOUSING AND URBAN AFFAIRS be pleased to state : \n(a)  the number of cities which have been selected under  the  Smart    \n     \nCities Mission (SCM);  \n(b)  whether all the selected cities have reported all t heir projects and    \ncost for each of these projects;  \n(c)  if so, the number of projects reported by each city  along with the cost of \neach project, city-wise; and  \n(d)   the number of cities which have identified sources  of finances for these \nprojects? \nANSWER \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE (INDEPENDENT CHARGE) OF THE  \nMINISTRY OF HOUSING AND URBAN AFFAIRS \n \n(SHRI HARDEEP SINGH PURI) \n \n(a)  100 cities have been selected for development as Sm art Cities under \nSmart Cities Mission (SCM). \n \n(b)   to (d) Yes, Sir. A total of 5,151 projects at an e stimated cost of Rs. \n2,05,018 crore have been proposed by 100 selected S mart Cities in their \nSmart City Proposals (SCPs). As per SCM Statement a nd Guidelines, the \nCentral Government proposes to give financial suppo rt to the extent of \nRs. 48,000 crore over five years i.e. an average of  Rs. 500 crore per city \nover the Mission period. An equal amount on a match ing basis is to be \nprovided by the State Governments/Urban Local Bodie s. Apart from \nthese, various cities have proposed to use funding from Public-Private \nPartnerships, loans, other resources, convergence w ith other \nGovernment Programmes/Missions as part of their SCP s. The State & \nCity-wise details of Smart Cities Proposal (SCP) Si ze is given in \nAnnexure-I.   \n \n***** \n\nAnnexure-I in reply to parts (b) to (d) of Lok Sabha Unstarred Question No. 776 for answer on \n6.2.2020 regarding Cities Selected under SCM raised by Shri Rajeshbahi Chudasama, Hon’ble \nMP. \n \n100 Smart Cities: Smart City Proposal Size  \n(Rs. Crores) \nRound 1 Cities \nState/ UT City SCP Size (Rs. Crore) \nAndhra Pradesh Kakinada 1,993 \nAndhra Pradesh Vishakhapatnam 1,602 \nAssam Guwahati 2,256 \nDelhi NDMC 1,897 \nGujarat Ahmedabad 2,290 \nGujarat Surat 2,597 \nKarnataka Belagavi 3,535 \nKarnataka Davanagere 1,307 \nKerala Kochi 2,076 \nMadhya Pradesh Bhopal 2,719 \nMadhya Pradesh Indore 5,100 \nMadhya Pradesh Jabalpur 3,999 \nMaharashtra Pune 2,363 \nMaharashtra Solapur 2,247 \nOdisha Bhubaneswar 4,537 \nPunjab Ludhiana 1,049 \nRajasthan Jaipur 2,341 \nRajasthan Udaipur 1,221 \nTamil Nadu Chennai 1,366 \nTamil Nadu Coimbatore 1,570 \nTotal 48,064 \nAverage SCP Size 2,403 \n \nRound 2 Cities including Fast Track Round \nState/ UT City SCP Size (Rs. Crore) \nAndaman & Nicobar Port Blair 778 \nAndhra Pradesh Tirupati 1,611 \n